<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Statisch Bestimmte Stabsysteme">
  <data key="d0">Statisch Bestimmte Stabsysteme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statically determinate bar systems in which bar forces, stresses, strains, displacements, and length changes can be determined sequentially from equilibrium conditions, elasticity law, and kinematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statisch Unbestimmte Stabsysteme">
  <data key="d0">Statisch Unbestimmte Stabsysteme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statically indeterminate bar systems in which equilibrium conditions, elasticity law, and kinematics must be considered simultaneously; temperature changes typically cause thermal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Statically indeterminate bar systems, a topic covered in the German engineering mechanics lecture document.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hookesches Gesetz">
  <data key="d0">Hookesches Gesetz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hooke's Law, a material model relating normal stress to strain via the elastic modulus, expressed as sigma equals E times epsilon.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalspannung">
  <data key="d0">Normalspannung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress in a cross-section perpendicular to the bar axis, calculated as sigma equals N divided by A.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress component acting perpendicular to the cross-section, which can be positive (tensile stress) or negative (compressive stress).&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental stress concept related to normal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress component acting normal to the cutting surface, causing tension or pressure on an infinitesimal volume element.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784900562</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehnung">
  <data key="d0">Dehnung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elongation component of strain, representing changes in side lengths; appears on the main diagonal of the strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b0e3f83f75ba130c72c201a9b9da1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cebf917adc3d92ce2d5e155d10154396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Dehnung</data>
</node>
<node id="Längenänderung">
  <data key="d0">Längenänderung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Change in length of a bar, calculated via the integral of normal force over tensile stiffness plus thermal expansion over the length of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gleichgewichtsbedingung">
  <data key="d0">Gleichgewichtsbedingung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium conditions used at nodes or on the undeformed system to determine bar forces and internal forces.&lt;SEP&gt;The equilibrium condition, one of three fundamental equation systems used for analyzing axially-loaded structural members.&lt;SEP&gt;The equilibrium condition relating internal forces and external distributed loads in structural bars, visually represented by the diagram under section 10.4 Einzelstab.&lt;SEP&gt;German term for equilibrium condition, referring to the x-direction force balance requirement for the infinitesimal element in plane stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastizitätsgesetz">
  <data key="d0">Elastizitätsgesetz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastizitätsgesetz, the German term for the Constitutive Law of Elasticity, is a fundamental principle in engineering mechanics that defines the relationship between stress (loading) and strain (deformation). Often referred to as Hooke's Law in 3D, this constitutive law is based on Hooke's model and relies on the modulus of elasticity (E-modulus). It is mathematically derived by connecting tensorial strain transformation with specific material constants to accurately predict material behavior under various loading conditions.

The Elastizitätsgesetz primarily assumes homogeneous and isotropic material behavior. It extensively covers stress-strain relationships, including the specific derivation of the relationship between the E-modulus and the shear modulus. The law is applied to numerous structural and mechanical scenarios, such as the structural analysis of bar systems, plane stress states, and biaxially loaded discs or plates. It specifically details how loading a disk solely with shear stresses results in spatial distortion, and it encompasses the three-dimensional formulation of Hooke's Law, which also accounts for thermal expansion.

In an academic context, the Elastizitätsgesetz is a core topic frequently discussed in engineering lectures and validated by textbooks, specifically "Technische Mechanik 2." It is systematically covered in Chapter 12 (and specifically Section 12.2, denoted as "12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz") of German-language academic documents. This educational section provides a comprehensive examination of stress-strain relationships, shear stress on plates, and material constants for isotropic elastic materials. The surrounding text sections frequently feature diagrams and geometric illustrations to serve as a visual and geometric basis for understanding the concepts detailed within the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd468369fbd1eac6b7662866f9ac8fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00185afb9a5d259c31643f50551104c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-818241f00ae24dac642bd60f22620196&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fcd058f225c6403a6dd2ac2e4f810f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db9317a3d674bad613adaf9f20eaf18d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998803</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Elastizitätsgesetz</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematik">
  <data key="d0">Kinematik</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinematics describing geometric displacement relationships, used together with equilibrium and elasticity law especially for statically indeterminate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of the geometry of deformation, using kinematic quantities like displacement and strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinematics, a section introducing displacement u(x) and strain ε as kinematic quantities describing deformation geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of motion and deformation, conceptually bridging basic geometry with the topic of strain.&lt;SEP&gt;The kinematics equations, one of three fundamental equation systems describing geometric relationships in structural member analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;One of three foundational equation types, defining the kinematic relationship between displacement u(x) and strain ε(x).&lt;SEP&gt;Kinematics of one-dimensional deformation of a tensile bar, relating displacement and elongation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Kinematik</data>
</node>
<node id="Dehnsteifigkeit">
  <data key="d0">Dehnsteifigkeit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile stiffness denoted EA, representing a bar's resistance to axial deformation; both bars in the example have equal tensile stiffness.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial stiffness (EA), defined as the product of Young's modulus and cross-sectional area, derived from combining the constitutive law with equilibrium considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;A term meaning "tensile stiffness", representing a special case in the analysis of rods.&lt;SEP&gt;Tensile stiffness, a specific elastostatics topic covered in the referenced textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for axial stiffness, mentioned in the text in relation to EA1 and EA2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7401c00a8316a2124dcb1cf703b5f16d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
