<node id="Material Model">
  <data key="d0">Material Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Describes the physical or phenomenological relationship between stresses and strains; depends on the material and can only be determined through experiments</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Model">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hooke's Model, also referred to by its original names Elastizitätsgesetz and Hookesches Modell, is a fundamental constitutive law used to describe the linear-elastic behavior of materials. The model specifically defines the relationship between stress and strain in materials that are subjected to either tensile or compressive loading. It is applicable under the strict condition that the induced stress remains below or equal to the material's proportionality limit.

In the context of structural analysis, Hooke's Model defines material behavior by incorporating the modulus of elasticity, denoted as E. It serves as a critical component in solving engineering problems. For instance, it provides the essential constitutive law relating stress and strain within bar elements, acting as one of the three fundamental equation types required to solve structural bar problems.

Furthermore, the application of Hooke's Model extends beyond basic one-dimensional scenarios. The model can be expanded to accommodate three-dimensional (3D) states of stress, allowing engineers and researchers to evaluate material behavior under complex conditions such as thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a438fa813ee176d3e3d64cd5a7e2dba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modulus Of Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Modulus Of Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportionality factor E in Hooke's model, measured in MPa (N/mm²), representing material stiffness&lt;SEP&gt;A material property measuring the stiffness of an elastic material, covered within the engineering mechanics document.&lt;SEP&gt;A material property measuring stiffness, defined and discussed in the context of the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab59a548a45f0f75802088fe89a02c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3118530638f20d2b532ee5ac5df3f9a0</data>
