<node id="Yield Stress">
  <data key="d0">Yield Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Yield Stress, commonly denoted as σ_F, is a critical stress limit that separates elastic and plastic material behavior. It represents the specific stress value at which a material transitions from a linear-elastic regime to one of permanent plastic deformation. Once this stress threshold is exceeded, the material begins to deform plastically, meaning the linear-elastic behavior no longer applies.

In technical and engineering contexts, Yield Stress serves as a foundational concept within material models. It dictates that the proportional limit stress must remain below the σ_F value to maintain structural integrity. As detailed in associated technical literature, including discussions in the sections preceding page 12, this threshold effectively categorizes material behavior into distinct elastic and plastic regimes.

From a practical standpoint, Yield Stress is an essential parameter used in engineering design verification. By ensuring that applied loads do not surpass this stress limit, engineers can guarantee that a material maintains its overall elastic behavior, thereby avoiding permanent plastic strain and ensuring the safety and reliability of the design.&lt;SEP&gt;The critical stress threshold observed in a standard uniaxial tensile test at which material yielding or failure initiates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-151ad67dcab298d7226da28fe7afb340&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
