</node>
<node id="Conceptual Overview of Elastostatics Topics (image)">
  <data key="d0">Conceptual Overview of Elastostatics Topics (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image acts as a thematic introduction to Chapter 10 of Prof. Sebastian Münstermann's lecture on Elastostatics. It visually categorizes the essential components of analyzing elastic structures under load: kinematic relationships, stress (Spannung), strain (Dehnung), and elasticity models, specifically within the context of bar elements. The 'LEARN' background underscores the instructional purpose of the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-48b0e3f83f75ba130c72c201a9b9da1d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Lernziele (Learning Objectives) (header)">
  <data key="d0">Lernziele (Learning Objectives) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Slide header in a German mechanics lecture indicating the section that outlines learning objectives for Chapter 12 on strain states and the elasticity law. It bridges the chapter introduction (page 0) with technical content starting on page 2, establishing pedagogical goals before presenting kinematics of deformation, displacement vectors, and strain definitions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d02b084fa5226215ac5abf89c23e96b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dae0b4038721550e833d3e9407ccd22</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971668</data>
  <data key="d7">Lernziele (Learning Objectives) (header)</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 2 - Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 2 - Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '2' identifies the second slide in Chapter 10 of the Elastostatics lecture. This page introduces Section 10.1 on Stress (Spannung), presenting fundamental concepts: bar axis definition, internal force determination via cutting method, Cauchy's normal stress concept, and sign conventions for tensile vs. compressive stress. Foundational content for stress analysis in bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic Diagram of a Tensile Bar and Stress Analysis Plane (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic Diagram of a Tensile Bar and Stress Analysis Plane (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides a visual representation of a prismatic bar subjected to axial tensile forces (F). It serves as the primary illustration for the definition of normal stress (σ = F/A) within the context of elastostatics. The diagram highlights the cross-sectional area (A) and the location of an arbitrary cut plane (s-s), which are essential for determining the uniform distribution of internal stresses according to the elementary theory of tension members discussed in the accompanying text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eae0b855416df86edb6af7ac8d0407fb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Internal Forces and Normal Stress in a Tensile Bar (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Internal Forces and Normal Stress in a Tensile Bar (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical illustration visually defines the relationship between external axial force (F), internal normal force (N), and normal stress (σ) in a straight bar. The lower figure shows the equilibrium of internal forces at a cross-section, while the upper figure details the distribution of these forces as uniform normal stresses. It directly supports the textbook's explanation of 'Technische Spannung' (Technical Stress) and the behavior of a 'Zugstab' (Tensile Bar) in elastostatics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Equation for Uniaxial Loading (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Equation for Uniaxial Loading (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental normal stress formula σ = N/A = F/A for straight bars under axial loading, derived from Cauchy's stress principle. It calculates uniform stress distribution assuming constant cross-section, relating internal normal force or external load to cross-sectional area. Essential for tension/compression member analysis, though limited near stress concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bibliographic Source Citation Footer (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Bibliographic Source Citation Footer (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer provides a bibliographic citation attributing the page's content to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017). It establishes academic authority for the surrounding engineering mechanics concepts, specifically regarding strain states and deformation kinematics, and serves as a reliable reference point for further knowledge retrieval.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6260a38feaaf76ba5695644cd599f7e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-uniform Stress Distribution in Axially Loaded Bars (image)">
  <data key="d0">Non-uniform Stress Distribution in Axially Loaded Bars (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual explanation of stress concentration phenomena in tension bars. It contrasts the theoretical model of a bar under load (a) with the actual, non-uniform stress distributions found at bar ends (b) and near geometric notches (c). By showing how stress vectors ($\\sigma$) vary across the cross-section, the image supports the textual discussion on why elementary stress theories fail at discontinuities and highlights the presence of stress peaks ('Spannungsspitzen') that must be considered in structural design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in an Axially-Loaded Bar (Cauchy Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in an Axially-Loaded Bar (Cauchy Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the technical normal stress σ(x) as the ratio of internal normal force N(x) to cross-sectional area A(x) at position x along a bar. It is the foundational stress formula for axially-loaded members, used for tensile/compressive stress analysis and cross-section dimensioning, with the assumption of uniform stress distribution. It generalizes to bars with weakly varying cross-sections but does not capture stress concentrations at notches or bar ends.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e52c13f9f7327cff72c363dce5291e4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) - Practical Applications (header)">
  <data key="d0">Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) - Practical Applications (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Numbered section header (10.1) in a German mechanics textbook covering stress analysis. This instance on page 4 continues the topic from theoretical foundations (page 2) to practical cross-section dimensioning, required area calculations, and buckling considerations for compression members. Serves as organizational anchor connecting stress theory to engineering design practice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 7 - Spannung Chapter Page Number (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 7 - Spannung Chapter Page Number (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '7' serving as a navigational reference within Chapter 10.1 'Spannung' (Stress) of a German technical mechanics document. Located at page index 3, it falls between stress theory fundamentals (internal forces, normal stress) and practical cross-section design content, aiding document navigation for stress analysis topics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bibliographic Source Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Bibliographic Source Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer serves as a bibliographic citation for the page's content, specifically attributing diagrams and theories regarding failure hypotheses to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017). It provides academic provenance and references the Springer Vieweg publication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e808a43e592e78037efdbadafb6d692e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04350734b994ec6a731daabee6c318c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b562c6cf8ea640066c4829c2dbc8a288</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993224</data>
  <data key="d7">Bibliographic Source Citation (footer)</data>
</node>
<node id="Allowable Stress Design Criterion (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Allowable Stress Design Criterion (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The inequality |σ| ≤ σ_{zul} is the fundamental stress-based design condition ensuring that the magnitude of normal stress (tensile or compressive) in a structural member does not exceed the material's allowable stress. It serves as the primary constraint for cross-section dimensioning and is essential for safe structural design, particularly when stress concentrations from geometric features must be considered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Required Cross-Sectional Area Design Formula (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Required Cross-Sectional Area Design Formula (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A dimensioning equation calculating the minimum required cross-sectional area ($A_{erf}$) as the ratio of absolute normal force ($|N|$) to allowable stress ($\sigma_{zul}$). This fundamental design constraint in structural engineering ensures actual stresses remain below permissible material limits, applicable where uniform stress distributions prevail per elementary tension/compression bar theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 8 - Stress Analysis Chapter (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 8 - Stress Analysis Chapter (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '8' marking page 5 of a German engineering textbook covering stress analysis (Spannung). Located within Chapter 10.1, this page addresses stress distributions in bars with variable cross-sections, stress concentrations at notches, and includes a conical bar example demonstrating normal stress calculations under axial compression loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a375b4397dc3d85d100d03de64a86ab7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic attribution footer citing Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), 'Technische Mechanik 2', Springer Vieweg as the source for content on page 12 of a document covering the Elastizitätsgesetz (Elasticity Law) chapter, including shear stress, shear modulus, and isotropic material constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd50f7cb97e77515d873719c81dafca9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e05e8f931eaf44276170a34134cf8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527b23d18394e188c355b1b1185acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec30ad8072c74dbe906914b85d9353a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d9d584003e02eb55f646636643aaba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00185afb9a5d259c31643f50551104c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784985050</data>
  <data key="d7">Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
</node>
<node id="Conical Compression Bar Stress Analysis Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Conical Compression Bar Stress Analysis Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides the geometric and loading parameters for a mechanics problem concerning a tapered (conical) bar under axial compression. It defines the bar's length (l), end radii (r0 and 2r0), and the applied compressive force (F). By introducing a variable radius r(x) along a longitudinal axis x, the diagram facilitates the derivation of the normal stress distribution σ(x) = N/A(x) as discussed in the context of elastostatic stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b523f7f38322578a875dcfe57c5cdd91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Radius Profile of Conical Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Linear Radius Profile of Conical Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the linearly varying radius r(x) of a conical bar under axial compression, transitioning from r₀ to 2r₀ over length l. This geometric relationship is essential for computing position-dependent cross-sectional area and stress distribution σ(x) = N/A(x) in non-prismatic structural members, illustrating fundamental concepts of stress analysis in variable cross-section components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Conical Compression Rod Normal Stress Distribution (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Conical Compression Rod Normal Stress Distribution (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the position-dependent normal stress σ(x) in a conical compression rod with circular cross-section under axial load F. The radius varies linearly from r₀ to 2r₀ over length l, yielding σ(x) = -F/[π·r₀²·(1+x/l)²]. Demonstrates non-uniform stress distribution in non-prismatic members under constant axial load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Stress Ratio of a Conical Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Stress Ratio of a Conical Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the relationship between the normal stresses at the ends of an axially loaded conical bar. It states that the stress at the smaller end ($x=0$) is four times the stress at the larger end ($x=l$), demonstrating the inverse proportionality between stress and cross-sectional area under a constant axial load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 9 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 9 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '9' serves as a navigational marker for the document page containing a worked example of normal stress in a conical rod and the transition to the topic of strain (Dehnung).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source attribution footer citing Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), Technische Mechanik 2, Springer Vieweg. Located at the bottom of page 6, it attributes the preceding content on stress (Spannung) and strain (Dehnung) to this standard German mechanics textbook, establishing academic provenance for the lecture material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Tensile Deformation of a Prismatic Bar (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Tensile Deformation of a Prismatic Bar (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the fundamental concept of tensile strain (Dehnung) for a prismatic bar with a constant cross-sectional area ($A = \\text{konst.}$). It contrasts the 'Ausgangslage' (initial state) of length $l$ with the 'Nach der Verformung' (deformed state) resulting from applied tensile forces $F$. The visual explicitly defines the elongation $\\Delta l$, providing a geometric basis for the technical strain formula $\\varepsilon = \\Delta l / l$ discussed in the accompanying text on elastostatics and the kinematics of deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Strain Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Technical Strain Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the dimensionless technical strain ($\varepsilon$) as the ratio of the change in length ($\Delta l$) to the original length ($l$). It is a fundamental kinematic relation in mechanics used to quantify deformation in structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 10 - Stress, Strain, and Kinematics Fundamentals (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 10 - Stress, Strain, and Kinematics Fundamentals (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 10 of a German mechanics textbook covering section 10.2 (Dehnung/Strain) and introducing Kinematik. Content includes strain definition ε=Δl/l, local strain for variable cross-sections ε(x)=du/dx, displacement integration, and identification of displacement and strain as kinematic quantities. Connects stress analysis from section 10.1 to deformation kinematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Diagram of Differential Bar Element Deformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Diagram of Differential Bar Element Deformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual derivation of local strain in a deformed bar. By comparing an 'undeformierter Stab' (undeformed bar) with a 'deformierter Stab' (deformed bar), it illustrates how a differential element of initial length '$dx$' undergoes displacement. The shifts of the element's faces are labeled '$u$' and '$u+du$', respectively. This figure is essential for understanding the kinematic relationship where strain ε is defined as the derivative of displacement $u$ with respect to position $x$, a core concept in the study of elastostatics and tension/compression in structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Rod Element Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Rod Element Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates an infinitesimal element of length dx, which is a core concept in the study of strain (Dehnung) and deformation in solid mechanics. It represents the differential segment used to derive the relationship between local displacement u(x) and local strain ε(x), as described in the context of elastostatics and tensile/compressive loading of rods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cebf917adc3d92ce2d5e155d10154396</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="One-Dimensional Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">One-Dimensional Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines local strain ε(x) as the spatial derivative of displacement u(x), equivalent to the engineering strain Δl/l for uniform deformation. This dimensionless kinematic quantity measures deformation intensity under small strain assumptions, serving as the geometric foundation connecting displacement fields to constitutive material models in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Section 10.2 Dehnung (Strain) (header)">
  <data key="d0">Section 10.2 Dehnung (Strain) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">This header introduces Section 10.2 on 'Dehnung' (Strain). It organizes the subsequent definitions of technical strain, positive/negative deformation, and local strain as a function of displacement. This establishes the kinematic foundation required to understand material behavior and stress-strain relationships discussed later in the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 11 - Engineering Mechanics Document (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 11 - Engineering Mechanics Document (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '11' marking the position within a German engineering mechanics textbook covering strain, kinematics, and material laws. Located at page index 7, it follows discussions of deformation concepts and precedes material on stress-strain diagrams and constitutive models. Serves as navigational reference for the academic document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a647d5317d835efced8e4c2f0cdae43</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
