</node>
<node id="Definition of Mechanical Normal Stress (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Mechanical Normal Stress (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal stress σ as force F divided by cross-sectional area A. This foundational relation connects external loading to internal material stress, a Kraftgröße paired with strain ε (a kinematische Größe). It underpins the stress-strain diagram and Hooke's Law, enabling material characterization and elastic/plastic classification central to this strength-of-materials lecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Engineering Strain (Dehnung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Engineering Strain (Dehnung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines strain ε as the ratio of length change Δl to original length l, serving as the fundamental kinematic relationship in one-dimensional bar mechanics. It forms the geometric basis for Hooke's Law and elastic-plastic material modeling within the stress-strain framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic Diagram of a Material Testing Apparatus (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic Diagram of a Material Testing Apparatus (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic depicts a standard experimental setup for performing tensile or compression tests on a material specimen ('Probe'). It identifies key components including the load cell ('Lastzelle'), the extensometer ('Längungsmesser'), and the movable crosshead ('bewegliches Querhaupt'). The illustration serves as a practical reference for the theoretical discussion on kinematics, stress-strain relationships, and Hooke's Law presented in the surrounding text regarding elastostatics in bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress-Strain Diagram Illustrating Engineering vs. True Stress and Plastic Deformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Strain Diagram Illustrating Engineering vs. True Stress and Plastic Deformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes the tensile behavior of a ductile material, plotting Stress ($\\sigma$) against Strain ($\\varepsilon$). It distinguishes between engineering stress (red curve) and true stress (dashed black curve), highlighting the effects of necking ('Einschnürung'). Key features include the yield point ($\\sigma_F$), strain hardening ('Verfestigung'), and the unloading path ('Entlastung') which results in permanent plastic strain ($\\varepsilon_{pl}$). The included specimen schematic provides geometric context for the tensile test variables ($F, A, l$). This figure serves as a practical illustration of the material laws and experimental data discussed in the 'Elastostatik' chapter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="10.3 Stoffgesetz/-modell (Constitutive Law/Model) (header)">
  <data key="d0">10.3 Stoffgesetz/-modell (Constitutive Law/Model) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Introduces constitutive laws defining the physical relationship between stress and strain. It bridges kinematics and kinetics, detailing elastic vs. plastic behavior, Hooke's Law for linear elasticity, and the determination of material properties like Young's Modulus via experimental stress-strain diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Test Principle Figure Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Test Principle Figure Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure caption citing Gottstein (p. 209) as the source for an illustration of the tensile test principle (Prinzip Zugversuch). Located within the constitutive model section (Stoffgesetz), it provides academic attribution for the visual depiction of the primary experimental method used to determine stress-strain relationships and material properties discussed in the surrounding content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
