  <data key="d0">Academic Source Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer provides a bibliographic citation for the slide's content, referencing the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017). It establishes academic provenance for the discussed mechanical engineering concepts, specifically anchoring the summary of failure hypotheses, strain tensors, and Hooke's Law in recognized standard literature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-443e845bfbe242f105299b42d8868dce</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 27 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 27 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 27, acting as a structural navigation marker. It locates the continuation of a section on Mohr's Circle (Mohrscher Spannungskreis), specifically part b) of an example calculating normal and shear stresses at a 30-degree angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a7d19da473048f7577c45363a2702e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7f9fd51bacb1b5261081565de649fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental stress design criterion (Spannungsnachweis) in structural mechanics and strength of materials, expressed as |σ| ≤ σ_zul, where the absolute value ensures the condition applies to both tensile and compressive stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress (Normalspannung) acting on a cross-section, computed as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), where N(x) is the internal normal force and A(x) is the cross-sectional area.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical quantity representing normal stress acting on a cross-section of a structural member under axial loading, with units [MPa = N/mm²], expressing an intensive quantity as the ratio of extensive force to geometric area&lt;SEP&gt;A type of stress represented by the horizontal axis in the Cartesian coordinate system of Mohr's Circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allowable Stress (σ_zul)">
  <data key="d0">Allowable Stress (σ_zul)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The allowable stress (zulässige Spannung), a material-specific limit defined by engineering codes that incorporates safety factors against yielding, fracture, or other failure modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force (N(x))">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force (N(x))</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal normal force used in the computation of normal stress, denoted N(x); in the conical bar example, N(x) = -F produces compression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Sectional Area (A(x))">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area (A(x))</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area used in the computation of normal stress, denoted A(x); the required area A_erf must be chosen so that maximum stress magnitude remains below σ_zul.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Stress">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile Stress, also known by its German equivalent Zugspannung, is a type of normal stress that occurs when a material is subjected to pulling forces, causing it to undergo elongation. Introduced as a theoretical concept within Section 10.1, it is particularly relevant in the analysis of tension and compression members. 

According to standard sign conventions used in engineering and lecture materials, Tensile Stress is consistently designated as a positive normal stress. It is mathematically represented as a positive stress (+sigma) or as a primary normal stress (σ_y = σ) acting vertically, often depicted with outward-pointing arrows on a material element. This positive stress operates perpendicularly to compressive principal stresses and is the specific positive normal stress accounted for in the absolute value formulation of the stress design criterion.

In practical and theoretical applications, this stress manifests as a mechanical load pulling a material outward, such as on the right edge of a disk. Beyond simple elongation, the application of Tensile Stress causes contraction in the orthogonal direction, a mechanical behavior that effectively demonstrates the Poisson effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf382350c1415b7ddd1f82959df3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-818241f00ae24dac642bd60f22620196&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998827</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Stress">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of normal stress (Druckspannung) that is designated as negative according to the sign convention used in the lecture material.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Druckspannung, the type of stress indicated by negative values of normal stress in tension/compression members.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of normal stress (Druckspannung) that occurs when a material is subjected to compressive or pushing forces. Introduced as a theoretical concept within Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;Negative normal stress (Druckspannung) that the absolute value formulation of the stress design criterion accounts for; slender compression members may fail by buckling before reaching σ_zul.&lt;SEP&gt;The negative stress value (Druckspannung) indicating compression in a structural member, represented by the negative sign in stress equations&lt;SEP&gt;A primary normal stress (σ_x = -σ) acting horizontally with inward-pointing arrows on the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-818241f00ae24dac642bd60f22620196</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999279</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Section Dimensioning">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Section Dimensioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A practical engineering method (Querschnittsbemessung) for determining appropriate cross-sectional dimensions of structural components under loading conditions. Covered as practical application content in Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical engineering procedure for sizing cross-sections of structural members, addressed on Page 4 of the textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of determining the required cross-sectional area (Querschnittsbemessung) such that the maximum stress magnitude anywhere in the component remains below σ_zul.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Concentrations">
  <data key="d0">Stress Concentrations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A technical mechanics concept related to notches, also referred to as 'Spannungsspitzen'.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Spannungsspitzen, non-uniform stress distributions that occur at bar ends, notches, and geometric discontinuities, requiring advanced methods beyond the elementary formula.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress phenomena occurring at notches, discussed within the stress theory content.&lt;SEP&gt;Localized stress peaks (Spannungsspitzen) caused by geometric discontinuities such as notches (Kerben) or bar ends (Stabenden), which can locally exceed predictions from elementary bar theory.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of conceptual diagram presented in the main body of the document relating to localized high stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The localized high stresses occurring at geometric discontinuities such as notches (Kerben) and bar ends (Stabenden).&lt;SEP&gt;Localized areas of high stress whose analysis relies on non-zero shear strain indicating angular distortion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buckling">
  <data key="d0">Buckling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure mode (Knicken) for slender compression members that may occur before the allowable stress σ_zul is reached.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure mode (Knicken) specific to slender compression members, which may fail before reaching material stress limits.&lt;SEP&gt;A potential failure mode for slender compression members that may occur before reaching critical stress, considered in stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Von Mises is a fundamental material failure criterion and theory widely utilized in machine design, structural engineering, and engineering mechanics. The term also recognizes a prominent historical figure associated with the conceptual development of these engineering principles. As a comprehensive failure theory, the Von Mises criterion is built upon foundational stress-based design concepts to predict material yielding and structural failure.

The criterion is extensively applied in structural analysis to evaluate the stress state and assess material strength across various components, including plates, beams, and complex machine parts. It plays a critical role in understanding stress transformation during elastic deformation and is fundamental for determining the critical loading directions that lead to mechanical failure. Technically, the Von Mises criterion operates by utilizing principal stresses and relying on advanced stress tensor formulations.

A defining mechanical characteristic of the Von Mises criterion is its prediction that a material will experience no yielding when subjected to pure hydrostatic stress. This specific behavior is directly tied to the Distortion Energy Hypothesis (Hypothese der Gestaltänderungsenergie), a core framework in structural mechanics with which the Von Mises figure is intrinsically associated. Together, these attributes establish the Von Mises criterion as an essential tool for predicting structural integrity and evaluating material performance under complex loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999598</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca">
  <data key="d0">Tresca</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Tresca criterion, also known as the Tresca failure theory or shear stress hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese), is a fundamental material failure model used extensively in structural analysis, machine design, and engineering mechanics. As a foundational, stress-based design criterion, it is specifically recognized as the namesake shear stress hypothesis utilized for evaluating the strength and predicting the failure of ductile materials. It is frequently referenced in the context of stress transformation and elastic deformation analysis.

From a technical perspective, the Tresca criterion evaluates the stress state of a given material by utilizing stress tensor formulations and principal stresses. It serves as a comprehensive framework for determining critical loading directions within engineering mechanics. By analyzing these stress states, engineers can accurately evaluate material strength and anticipate potential points of failure under complex loading conditions.

In practical engineering applications, the Tresca criterion is employed to ensure the structural integrity and reliability of various physical elements. It is directly applied to the structural analysis and failure prediction of critical infrastructure and machinery, specifically including plates, beams, and various machine components. Through this rigorous assessment of stress and material limits, the Tresca criterion provides a vital mathematical basis for safe and effective engineering design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-343794ade2b1d656c68e2c17454713f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr-Coulomb">
  <data key="d0">Mohr-Coulomb</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A more complex failure theory used in machine design and structural engineering, built upon the foundational stress-based design criterion.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure theory fundamental to determining critical loading directions in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c</data>
