<node id="Stress Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Stress Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A significant redistribution of internal forces around discontinuities such as a geometric notch.&lt;SEP&gt;A phenomenon (Spannungsspitzen) characterized by localized peaks of stress, occurring particularly at structural discontinuities such as notches and bar ends. Introduced as part of the theoretical foundations in Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress analysis concept describing non-uniform stress distributions that occur at bar ends and notches, where stress concentration factors diminish with distance from the discontinuity.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental principle in structural design stating that stress concentrates in smaller cross-sections, directly informing the required cross-sectional area to prevent material failure.&lt;SEP&gt;The localized occurrence of high stress magnitude that occurs at the minimum cross-section in non-prismatic members under load</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a375b4397dc3d85d100d03de64a86ab7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
