</node>
<node id="Stress">
  <data key="d0">Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress, also referred to by its German equivalent Spannung, is a fundamental technical mechanics concept and a dynamic quantity (Kraftgrößen) in continuum mechanics, engineering mechanics, and elastostatics. As a force-related quantity (Kraftgröße), it is distinct from, yet complementary to, strain, which is a kinematic quantity. Stress fundamentally describes the internal resistance force, or internal force intensity, per unit area within a material. It is universally denoted by the symbol σ and is typically measured in megapascals [MPa = N/mm²]. 

The calculation of stress can be approached in several ways. Fundamentally, it is calculated as either engineering stress or true stress using the ratio of normal force to cross-sectional area (σ = N/A). In the context of material laws and models, stress represents the physical quantity that constitutive laws phenomenologically connect to strains. It is governed by Hooke's law (σ = E·ε) and is constrained by the material's proportional limit. This constitutive relationship is empirically determined through tensile testing. Additionally, stress can be computed directly from displacement field solutions in axially loaded structural members, as represented by the material response equation σ(x) = E·u'(x) = (G - n·x)/A.

As a basic concept in mechanical and structural mechanics, stress serves as the foundational principle for the logical flow leading to single bar analysis and broader stress analysis. It is extensively utilized to analyze elastic bodies under load and to evaluate internal stress distributions within structural members. Practical applications and computations involving stress are frequently applied to statically determinate rod and bar systems, including scenarios such as conical rods under compression. Furthermore, internal stress within constrained systems can also be generated by thermal effects. This mechanical engineering concept is a core topic covered in German academic lecture documents and educational materials, including textbooks such as Technische Mechanik 2 and specific summary sections in structural mechanics literature (e.g., page 27).&lt;SEP&gt;The fundamental mechanical concept illustrated within a solid body under load.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical quantity confirmed to be a second-rank tensor (Tensor 2. Stufe) that transforms according to specific tensorial rules and is rotation-invariant, meaning the physical state remains unchanged regardless of coordinate system orientation.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical quantities that are proven to be tensors because they are independent of rotation ('rotationsunabhängig').&lt;SEP&gt;The internal forces per unit area within a deformable body.&lt;SEP&gt;The applied force per unit area, denoted as σ, considered in a biaxial stress state where σ₁ = σ and σ₂ = -σ.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e140adbaf6055a32563aa2ca4a630965&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e31c68f0ed98d537493847b61aa6024c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7f9fd51bacb1b5261081565de649fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13b9a52b59b74e8f8b6c3180f71ccba2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
