</node>
<node id="Stress Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Stress Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress Analysis is a fundamental engineering discipline and branch of physics focused on determining the internal forces within materials and structures. As a core field within engineering mechanics, continuum mechanics, and elastostatics, it involves evaluating the stress state at specific points, such as point P, within a deformable body. The foundational concepts of Stress Analysis include the examination of stress vectors, stress tensors, normal stresses, and shear stresses. This discipline extensively utilizes the moment equilibrium equation to demonstrate the symmetry of the stress tensor. Furthermore, it addresses non-uniform stress distributions, evaluating stress concentrations at notches, and informs cross-section design.

As an analytical and technical procedure, Stress Analysis relies heavily on mathematical frameworks to evaluate material stresses. This includes the use of stress transformation equations, trigonometric relationships, and coordinate system rotations to analyze extremal values and principal directions. Key analytical tools within this field encompass the construction of Mohr's circle and the determination of principal stresses (Hauptspannungen). These transformation frameworks are vital for comprehensively understanding the complex stress states and stress components present within engineering components.

In practical applications, Stress Analysis connects theoretical mechanics to structural design, notably in the evaluation of thin-walled pressure vessels. This involves integrating equilibrium-derived boiler formulas, Mohr's circle theory, and failure mechanics to ensure structural integrity in pressure vessel design. Academically, Stress Analysis is a prominent subject covered in recognized German-language engineering documentation, textbooks, and lecture notes. Specifically, these advanced topics, including the fundamental components of stress and failure analysis, are extensively detailed in technical mechanics documents such as the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;A subject area related to the sections cross-referenced within the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e05e8f931eaf44276170a34134cf8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ba563b25b03c792bf6e4d826fd0f062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dead11f22d1654614fa266e394b63218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
