<node id="Cross-Sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-Sectional Area is a fundamental geometric property of a structural member or rod, defined as the internal surface area exposed when the structural bar is theoretically cut. Universally denoted by the symbol A, this property is typically measured in square meters. While it is frequently assumed to be constant across a straight bar, a structural member can also feature varying geometries along its length. In such instances, different cross-sectional areas are assigned to distinct segments of the rod, commonly denoted as A1 for the first segment and A2 for the second.

In structural mechanics and engineering, Cross-Sectional Area is critical for evaluating material behavior under load. It is a primary component in the stress formula σ = N/A, representing the specific area across which stress is assumed to be uniformly distributed. Additionally, this geometric property is integral to mechanical elongation calculations, a context where it is occasionally referred to by the German term Querschnittsfläche. Furthermore, the Cross-Sectional Area of a structural member is directly combined with Young's modulus to compute the axial stiffness and overall axial rigidity of the section, making it an indispensable parameter in the analysis of structural integrity and deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gross">
  <data key="d0">Gross</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gross is an academic author prominently recognized for his substantial contributions to the field of engineering mechanics. He is best known for co-authoring the highly regarded textbook series "Technische Mechanik." Specifically, Gross is credited as an author or co-author of multiple volumes within this series, including "Technische Mechanik 1," "Technische Mechanik 2," and "Technische Mechanik 3."

A significant focus of his academic work centers on the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2," where he is specifically identified as one of four co-authors. This foundational engineering mechanics textbook was published in 2017 by the academic publishing house Springer Vieweg. In addition to these core textbooks, Gross has also co-written accompanying formula collections that serve as exercise materials within the "Technische Mechanik" curriculum.

Within his texts, Gross is noted for explicitly outlining the expected educational outcomes for students, thereby ensuring a structured learning experience. Through his extensive work on these widely recommended academic materials and exercise collections, Gross has established himself as a key contributor to the instruction and advancement of engineering mechanics education.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e05e8f931eaf44276170a34134cf8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527b23d18394e188c355b1b1185acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e808a43e592e78037efdbadafb6d692e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3cdf7e6c0e0814f6340adcf0330c36f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42f0665902c9df9b5ec4573eb02f74a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd50f7cb97e77515d873719c81dafca9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f99cae1296009661e572167acf5fe19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3118530638f20d2b532ee5ac5df3f9a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04350734b994ec6a731daabee6c318c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a7b2afc01b89dc35586063ca901c7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec30ad8072c74dbe906914b85d9353a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f51