<node id="Small Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Small Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">"Small Deformations," also referred to as "kleine Deformationen," denotes minor physical changes in the shape or size of a material body. Within continuum theory, it serves as a fundamental assumption and physical condition wherein changes in side lengths and angular distortions are sufficiently small to be accurately tracked, particularly within the context of the Verzerrungszustand (strain state). 

Mathematically, Small Deformations are formulated using linear approximations based on Taylor series expansions. The concept assumes that these physical changes occur over an infinitesimally small scale, characterized by an extremely small infinitesimal separation dx or a distance (y - y_0) that is infinitesimally small. As a result, higher-order terms in series expansions are neglected, and the Taylor series is truncated after the linear term. Therefore, retaining only the first-order terms corresponds precisely to this assumption, directly enabling the process of linearization.

Theoretically, Small Deformations act as a restriction where the absolute value of epsilon is much less than 1. This constraint is strictly necessary for linearizing subsequent mechanical equations and underpins the applicability of linearized strain theory. By assuming that geometric changes due to deformation are negligible, this condition ensures the validity of the superposition principle. Furthermore, in practical displacement analysis, Small Deformations allow for geometric simplifications where circular arcs can be accurately approximated by their tangents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9f59f4bf11344385ddf5b19b6c93607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
