  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Compatibility">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Compatibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditions (Kinematik) ensuring deformations are geometrically consistent in a structure, required for analyzing statically indeterminate systems&lt;SEP&gt;A physical condition stating that the displacements of the structural bars must conform to the system's geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;The geometric compatibility condition relating bar elongations, specifically Δl1 = Δl2 cos α, contributing two cos α factors to the equation through Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Constitutive Law">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Constitutive Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The material law (Elasitzitätsgesetz) relating stress and strain in elastic deformation, characterized by extensional stiffness EA</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extensional Stiffness">
  <data key="d0">Extensional Stiffness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as EA, a measure of resistance to axial deformation in a structural member, part of the elastic constitutive law&lt;SEP&gt;The axial stiffnesses (Dehnsteifigkeiten) of the bars, where the stiffness of bar 3 equals that of bar 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Strain">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical strain, commonly denoted as ε, represents the elastic deformation of a material resulting from mechanical loading. In the context of uniaxial and axial loading, this strain component represents the mechanical deformation of a bar and can be defined as the derivative of displacement (u') with respect to position (x). Rooted in Hooke's Law, mechanical strain is calculated by dividing the normal stress (σ) by Young's modulus (E). Alternatively, it is determined by dividing the internal normal force (N) by the material's tensile stiffness or axial rigidity (EA), simplifying to the fundamental expression N/(EA).

In more complex scenarios, such as a triaxial stress state, mechanical strain captures both the direct axial deformation and the Poisson effect, which accounts for lateral contraction. Expressed as (1/E)(σ_x - ν[σ_y + σ_z]), where ν is Poisson's ratio, this strain contribution represents a broader component of normal strain derived from the compliance matrix of isotropic materials. Together, these formulations provide a comprehensive understanding of how materials mechanically deform under both simple and multi-axial loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate System">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural system where thermal expansion produces deformation without inducing additional normal forces or stresses, as the structure can freely deform&lt;SEP&gt;A structural system where the phenomenon of internal forces arising from thermal loading without external mechanical loads is absent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f393772a794391e49730d47a8bda4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate System">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural system where constrained thermal expansion generates internal forces and additional stresses beyond mechanical loading&lt;SEP&gt;A system where support reactions cannot be determined by static equilibrium alone, described in the surrounding text.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural system where support reactions cannot be determined by static equilibrium alone; in this case, a compound rod with both ends fixed (beidseitig eingespannt)&lt;SEP&gt;A structural system where internal forces arise from thermal loading even without external mechanical loads.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural system that cannot be solved by equilibrium equations alone and requires additional compatibility conditions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccf2b44f3719aa488a2e1148c9ed36db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f393772a794391e49730d47a8bda4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Total Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The combined deformation resulting from the superposition of thermal strain and mechanical strain, integrated as u = ∫ ε dx for variable cross-sections&lt;SEP&gt;A physical observable representing the overall change in shape or size of a structural member under loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Civil Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of engineering where the thermal expansion law is essential for analyzing thermal stresses in constrained structures and bridge design&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering in which the equilibrium equation is essential for analyzing axially loaded members such as columns, cables, bars, and rods.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering field where stress transformation is used extensively for stress analysis of loaded components&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where the derived mathematical concepts are practically applied.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where stress analysis is applied to plane structural elements.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering field that extensively uses this equation and Finite Element Analysis for calculating stresses in loaded components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical Engineering is an engineering discipline and broader technical context, often studied alongside physics, that is fundamentally focused on the design and analysis of mechanical systems. As a highly practical field, it relies on applying foundational formulas to calculate deformations, evaluate forces, and ensure overall structural integrity across various applications.

A major focus within mechanical engineering is the rigorous stress analysis of loaded components and plane structural elements. The discipline extensively utilizes stress transformation techniques, alongside specific equations and Finite Element Analysis (FEA), to calculate stresses in mechanical components. Engineers in this field rely on equilibrium equations to analyze axially loaded members subjected to body forces or surface tractions. Furthermore, to evaluate complex multi-axial stress states and predict structural failure, mechanical engineering heavily applies strength hypotheses, most notably utilizing the Von Mises stress framework to calculate equivalent stress under complex 3D loadings.

Additionally, mechanical engineering addresses the impact of thermal factors on material behavior and structural stability. For instance, the thermal expansion law is an essential principle within the field, applied specifically for analyzing thermal stresses in pipeline systems and other critical infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999327</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses generated in restrained structures where thermal expansion is prevented, even under zero external load.&lt;SEP&gt;Stresses generated by constrained thermal expansion in structures, which the thermal expansion law helps to analyze&lt;SEP&gt;Stresses induced in structures due to thermal loading, a core topic in engineering mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;Stresses induced by constrained thermal expansion that must be accounted for in structural design to prevent failure.&lt;SEP&gt;Stresses induced by thermal expansion in constrained systems, known as Wärmespannungen, arising even without external loads</data>
