</node>
<node id="Stress-Strain Relationships">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Strain Relationships</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress-Strain Relationships represent the fundamental mechanical correlations between stress and strain in materials, comprehensively detailing how materials deform under applied stress. As a core topic within engineering and structural mechanics, these relationships are essential for understanding and analyzing material behavior. In German academic contexts, this concept is referred to as Spannungen-Dehnungen. 

The analysis of Stress-Strain Relationships is structurally bridged by the kinematic description of deformation, which is mathematically provided by the displacement vector field. Within these structural mechanics relationships, the normal strain measure, denoted as ε_x, serves as an essential evaluative component. 

In educational and technical literature, Stress-Strain Relationships are frequently discussed as a subsequent topic in academic lectures and throughout core engineering mechanics documents. The concept is specifically detailed within the Elastizitätsgesetz (elasticity law) chapter and is a key focus within the plane stress state section. Furthermore, the study and understanding of this topic are directly supported by the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a647d5317d835efced8e4c2f0cdae43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab59a548a45f0f75802088fe89a02c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-873966c9766a35d08748403615a24d1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0deb8f7c8c663233e195e68e3b5afeea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00185afb9a5d259c31643f50551104c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
