<node id="Angle Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Angle Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Angle Alpha is a geometric variable extensively used in structural mechanics and solid mechanics to define the orientation, internal forces, and deformations within physical systems. In the context of discrete structural members, Angle Alpha frequently represents the specific angle formed between interconnected elements, such as horizontal Bar 1 and inclined Bar 2 at a designated connection point like Joint C. Within a three-bar system, this angle is crucial for structural analysis; for example, its cosine appears in compatibility equations that relate the elongations of the individual bars. Angle Alpha also defines the general inclination of structural components, such as specifying the tilt of Bars 1 and 3 relative to the vertical axis. Furthermore, it characterizes the orientation of internal bar forces. It can define the direction of force S_2 through the arc between the horizontal line of force S_1 and the vector of S_2, or establish the geometric relationship formed by bar forces S_1 and S_3 relative to force S_2.

In continuum mechanics and deformation analysis, Angle Alpha describes localized strain and geometric alterations. It can represent the angular tilt of an originally vertical side, such as side PS, which is mathematically related to the material's displacement gradient $\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}$. Additionally, Angle Alpha is used to define the geometric configuration of deformed bodies, such as a rhombus. In these instances, it is marked by black arcs at the bottom vertex of the shape to establish the orientation of the rhombus's edges relative to the primary coordinate axes. Within these specific deformation contexts, Angle Alpha is occasionally assigned an explicit value of $45^\circ$ to quantify the structural deformation and indicate the precise orientation of the deformed edges relative to the original horizontal axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed3c14262e18f66ed415c1b72900af48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f72457639c5767731980d0c54a4fe069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdfa6405eb28772fd593fe970821beaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94aa1e1d6b738ff40ac94957c9a7084a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c113cfbbfa4f8142d716b5538931026&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd4685e0a0874c320cb468d922d54e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
