  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page 17">
  <data key="d0">Page 17</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific page in the textbook where the problem of finding the displacement of Point C is described.&lt;SEP&gt;A page number element from a German engineering mechanics document, serving as a structural navigation element within section 11.2.2 'Hauptspannungen,' marking the transition from coordinate transformation theory to principal stress determination.&lt;SEP&gt;A page in the technical document that discussed the model's validity in arbitrary Cartesian coordinate systems and principal axis systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6681170cb4ff75b4d310df8342f3aad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="r(x)">
  <data key="d0">r(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A continuous, monotonically increasing linear function of position x that describes the linear variation of the radius along the longitudinal axis of a conical bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="r_0">
  <data key="d0">r_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The initial, smaller radius of the circular cross-section at the axial position x = 0 [m].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="l">
  <data key="d0">l</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The variable `l` represents a fundamental geometric parameter denoting the length of a structural element within engineering and mechanics. It generally defines the total or original (undeformed) length of a component.

In the context of one-dimensional structural components such as bars, conical bars, bar elements, and hanging rods, `l` specifies the total physical length. This parameter is crucial for structural analysis and mathematical calculations. Specifically, it is used to determine the length of a bar segment over which distributed loads act and normal forces are evaluated. Additionally, `l` is essential for specifying boundary conditions. For a hanging rod, this length is used to define the position of the free end and serves as a key factor in calculating the component's overall weight. 

Furthermore, the application of `l` extends to two-dimensional structural elements. In the analysis of plates, it denotes the original (undeformed) length of a biaxially loaded plate (referred to as a Scheibe). Across all these varied mechanical contexts, `l` functions as an essential baseline metric required for evaluating forces, loads, and structural integrity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="x">
  <data key="d0">x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial coordinate measured from the smaller-radius end [m], which serves as a normalized coordinate parameter.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable labeled in the diagram indicating the distance from the left origin to the left boundary of the green differential segment in the undeformed bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The longitudinal coordinate along the one-dimensional structural element.&lt;SEP&gt;The position along the rod measured from the fixed support at the top, with x = 0 at the fixed end and x = l at the free end.&lt;SEP&gt;The spatial coordinate along the body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999034</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A(x)">
  <data key="d0">A(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area of the circular cross-section at axial position x, computed as π·r(x)².</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ(x)">
  <data key="d0">σ(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress distribution calculated as N(x)/A(x), representing a position-dependent stress field.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial stress (normal stress) at position x along the hanging rod, varying linearly from G/A at the fixed end to zero at the free end.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="N(x)">
  <data key="d0">N(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant compressive internal force within the bar, equal to -F.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal normal force (Normalkraft) as a function of the longitudinal coordinate, representing the axial force within the structural element.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal normal force at position x, decreasing linearly from G at the support to zero at the free end as only the rod's weight below section x contributes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Truncated Cone Geometry">
  <data key="d0">Truncated Cone Geometry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The geometric shape of the conical bar, characterized by a linear variation in radius and a constant taper angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine Components">
  <data key="d0">Machine Components</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical parts, such as conical shafts, where geometries with varying cross-sections are utilized.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical mechanical parts analyzed using stress transformation equations and failure criteria within structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biomechanical Models">
  <data key="d0">Biomechanical Models</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Theoretical representations of biological systems, such as bone geometry, where variable cross-section structures are found.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
