<node id="σ(x=0) = 4·σ(x=l)">
  <data key="d0">σ(x=0) = 4·σ(x=l)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation evaluating the normal stress function σ(x) at the two boundary points of an axially loaded conical bar, establishing a quantitative 4:1 stress ratio between the narrower end (x=0) and the wider end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Compressive Force F">
  <data key="d0">Axial Compressive Force F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A constant axial compressive force applied to the conical bar, resulting in a uniform internal normal force N(x) = -F along the bar's length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force N(x)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force N(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal normal force within the conical bar, equal to -F and uniform along the entire length l of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The force represented by $E \cdot A \cdot u'$, varying linearly as $N(x) = G(1 - x/l)$ in a statically determinate hanging bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area A(x)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area A(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The circular cross-sectional area at position x, varying from πr_0^2 at the narrower end (x=0) to 4πr_0^2 at the wider end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Solid Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid Mechanics is a comprehensive branch of physics, engineering, and continuum mechanics dedicated to analyzing the physical behavior of solid materials under various external forces, stresses, and displacements. As a fundamental academic field, it provides the theoretical framework for material strength assessment and the production of detailed engineering documentation. The discipline is particularly concerned with continuous media, focusing on how neighboring points displace relative to one another, especially concerning small displacements and local strains. 

The field relies heavily on advanced mathematical concepts and linear approximations to satisfy equilibrium conditions within solid objects. Engineers and physicists utilize specific formulations, such as the normal stress equation defined as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), to evaluate internal forces. Furthermore, Solid Mechanics employs constitutive laws and equations to model specific scenarios, such as biaxially loaded plates. Advanced mathematical tools, including the Cauchy stress tensor and symmetric stress tensors, are fundamental for establishing these constitutive equations.

A major focus within Solid Mechanics is the analysis and transformation of stress and strain states. Concepts like plane stress transformations are commonly applied and visually illustrated using coordinate transformation diagrams. Mohr's Circle is frequently utilized to analyze and represent these stress states. Additionally, the calculation of parameters such as hydrostatic strain energy density is profoundly significant in this domain, aiding in the comprehensive evaluation of stress distributions.

Ultimately, Solid Mechanics is crucial for evaluating material failure and ensuring structural integrity. The domain encompasses elastostatics and specific material failure theories tailored for isotropic, ductile materials. By analyzing principal stresses and applying specific failure criteria—often utilizing mathematical parameters like the first stress invariant—researchers can accurately predict how solid materials will perform under various loading conditions and determine precise material failure points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999164</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Required Cross-sectional Area A_erf">
  <data key="d0">Required Cross-sectional Area A_erf</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The minimum cross-sectional area needed to prevent material failure in structural design, directly informed by stress concentration principles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="End Radius r_0">
  <data key="d0">End Radius r_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius of the narrower end (x=0) of the conical bar, which results in a circular cross-sectional area of πr_0^2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="End Radius 2r_0">
  <data key="d0">End Radius 2r_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius of the wider end (x=l) of the conical bar, twice the value of r_0, resulting in a circular cross-sectional area of 4πr_0^2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Design">
  <data key="d0">Structural Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An engineering discipline in which the principle of stress concentration in smaller cross-sections directly informs the required cross-sectional area to prevent material failure.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering discipline that relies on stress transformation and Mohr's circle for analyzing structural integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Engineering application field where stress transformation equations are essential for determining stress components in any direction&lt;SEP&gt;The field of designing structures, which relies on the theoretical basis provided by stress analysis and failure criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering to which principal stresses and their associated failure criteria are central.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering application area where the identification of principal angles and stresses is critical.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where principal stress analysis is considered fundamental.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Failure">
  <data key="d0">Material Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The failure mode that proper determination of the required cross-sectional area (A_erf) aims to prevent in structural design.&lt;SEP&gt;The breakdown of a material, predicted using derived strain quantities and stresses calculated via Hooke's Law.&lt;SEP&gt;The breaking or breakdown of a material subjected to stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The breakdown of a material, which the Shear Stress Hypothesis posits is determined by the maximum shear stress value.&lt;SEP&gt;The breakdown of a material, which can be predicted by equating distortion energy to uniaxial equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a498a78500a61b206c4b1205b49030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation u = |Δl₁| = (Fl)/(EA·tan α)">
  <data key="d0">Equation u = |Δl₁| = (Fl)/(EA·tan α)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the horizontal displacement of nodal point C in a statically determinate two-bar system under a vertical load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Displacement u">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Displacement u</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The kinematic displacement quantity of node C, measured in meters, directly equaling the longitudinal deformation of the horizontal bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The horizontal component of the movement of point P to its new position P', serving as the base variable for Taylor series expansion of other points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Two-bar System">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Two-bar System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural system consisting of a horizontal bar and an inclined bar, analyzed under specific loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Bar (Stab 1)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Bar (Stab 1)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The horizontal component of the two-bar system that undergoes shortening due to compressive forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inclined Bar (Stab 2)">
  <data key="d0">Inclined Bar (Stab 2)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The inclined component of the two-bar system, positioned at an angle and carrying a portion of the vertical load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Load F">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Load F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The applied vertical force at node C, measured in Newtons, causing structural deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Stiffness EA">
  <data key="d0">Axial Stiffness EA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The product of Young's modulus and cross-sectional area, assumed to be constant along the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The identical axial stiffness (Dehnsteifigkeit) possessed by both bars in the system, determined by material and cross-sectional properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
