<node id="Young's Modulus E">
  <data key="d0">Young's Modulus E</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Young's Modulus E, also referred to as the modulus of elasticity (or *Elastizitätsmodul*), is a fundamental material property that represents the stiffness of a solid material or structural component, such as a bar. As a crucial material parameter, it is extensively utilized in constitutive laws to mathematically relate mechanical stresses to their corresponding strains. Specifically, within the framework of Hooke's law, Young's Modulus E defines the direct proportional relationship between normal stress and normal strain, expressed by the formula σ = E · ε. In this capacity, it functions as an independent material constant that scales stress contributions within the constitutive relations of a material.

In the context of material science, Young's Modulus E serves as one of two independent material constants for isotropic elastic materials. Alongside Poisson's ratio, it provides a complete mechanical characterization of the material and can be used to derive other essential physical properties, such as the shear modulus. To quantify this inherent stiffness, Young's Modulus E is measured in standard units of pressure or stress, most commonly megapascals (MPa) or newtons per square millimeter (N/mm²).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
