<node id="Constitutive Law">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Constitutive Law is a fundamental principle in structural mechanics that describes the physical relationship between applied forces and material deformation. Specifically, it governs the stress-strain behavior of a material through various defining material parameters. In German, this concept is commonly referred to as either *Stoffgesetz* or *Elastizitätsgesetz*. 

In the context of structural analysis, the Constitutive Law is recognized as one of the three fundamental governing equation types—often identified as the third type—necessary to solve for stresses and deformations, particularly in statically indeterminate systems and bar problems. It is typically applied after the three-dimensional strain state has been fully derived, allowing engineers to resolve the complete mechanical behavior of the system. 

A prominent instantiation of the Constitutive Law is Hooke's law, also known as Hooke's model, which directly relates stress to strain via material constants such as Young's modulus. Beyond basic linear elasticity, these laws can also be formulated to incorporate thermal effects, ensuring a comprehensive characterization of how materials structurally respond to diverse physical and environmental conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aac206a8f9ab7199ba4ae6138d74c7ec</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999205</data>
