</node>
<node id="Diagram C">
  <data key="d0">Diagram C</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">This figure corresponds to the cut 's'-s'' at the notch and depicts a pronounced outward curve or 'bulge' indicating stress concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical diagram presenting the Verschiebungsplan, which details the kinematic compatibility of the structural system.&lt;SEP&gt;A three-dimensional perspective technical schematic diagram showing a cylindrical pressure vessel with wall thickness 't', used to analyze force equilibrium in the longitudinal (x) direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdfa6405eb28772fd593fe970821beaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Tensile Forces">
  <data key="d0">Axial Tensile Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">External loads, denoted by F, that pull outward from both ends of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Straight Bar">
  <data key="d0">Straight Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A gray rectangular object subjected to axial tensile forces.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member, also referred to as Stab, with constant or weakly varying cross-sectional areas (schwach veränderliche Querschnittsfläche), subject to uniaxial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Plane S-s">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Plane S-s</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific vertical green dashed line marking a cross-section located near the right end of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Plane S'-s'">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Plane S'-s'</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific vertical green dashed line marking a cross-section located near a V-shaped notch.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V-shaped Notch">
  <data key="d0">V-shaped Notch</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A small geometric discontinuity in the bar's upper edge causing stress concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Peaks">
  <data key="d0">Stress Peaks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Spannungsspitzen, this represents the non-uniform stress distribution occurring where external forces are applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Axis">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The horizontal axis running along the length of the structural bar, along which the external tensile forces act.&lt;SEP&gt;The longitudinal axis of the bar, indicated by a blue arrow labeled 'x'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae0b855416df86edb6af7ac8d0407fb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elementary Assumption Of Uniform Stress">
  <data key="d0">Elementary Assumption Of Uniform Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The basic assumption that stress is distributed uniformly, which does not hold exactly at the bar ends or around discontinuities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Governing ODE For Axial Displacement">
  <data key="d0">Governing ODE For Axial Displacement</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Second-order ordinary differential equation (ODE) for axial displacement of a single rod/bar (Einzelstab) under combined mechanical and thermal loading, synthesizing equilibrium, kinematics, and constitutive law with thermal effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-order ordinary differential equation E·A·u'' = -n representing the axial displacement field u(x) of a one-dimensional structural member under distributed axial load, categorized as statically determinate</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Displacement Field">
  <data key="d0">Axial Displacement Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field denoted as u(x) that describes the longitudinal displacement of a cross-section originally located at a specific coordinate, measured in units of length.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement of a bar cross-section along the longitudinal axis, measured in millimeters.&lt;SEP&gt;Displacement field u(x) describing axial deformation along the position x of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;Displacement function u(x) of a one-dimensional structural member (rod/bar) under distributed axial load&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement along the rod denoted as $u(x)$, representing the deformation at any given point along the length in meters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distributed Axial Load">
  <data key="d0">Distributed Axial Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An external distributed axial load, denoted as n, acting over the infinitesimal length of the structural bar element.&lt;SEP&gt;Load per unit length n = G/l, representing self-weight as a constant Streckenlast applied along the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient Of Thermal Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient Of Thermal Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Coefficient of Thermal Expansion is a fundamental material property that quantifies the degree to which a material expands or contracts in response to temperature changes. Commonly denoted by the symbols α, αT, or alpha_T, this property establishes a direct relationship between a shift in temperature and the resulting thermal strain experienced by the material. Specifically, it measures the free thermal expansion—or the linear thermal strain contribution—generated per unit of temperature change. 

This property is typically measured in units of inverse Kelvin (1/K) and serves as a precise indicator of a material's inherent tendency to expand when heated. It is also widely recognized by its German equivalents, "Wärmeausdehnungskoeffizient" and "thermischer Ausdehnungskoeffizient." Because it accurately captures how materials react to thermal variations, the Coefficient of Thermal Expansion is an essential parameter utilized in governing differential equations within the fields of thermodynamics, engineering, and material mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Relation">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Relation N = E·A·(u' - α_T·ΔT) linking normal force to mechanical strain minus thermal strain, incorporating elasticity and thermal effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hanging Bar Under Self-Weight">
  <data key="d0">Hanging Bar Under Self-Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific statically determinate problem (Hängender Stab unter Eigengewicht) with boundary conditions u(0) = 0 (fixed support at top) and u'(l) = 0 (free, stress-free end at bottom).&lt;SEP&gt;An example system used to demonstrate the integration of the governing differential equation with specific boundary conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Element Formulation">
  <data key="d0">Finite Element Formulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A computational framework for analyzing bar elements, for which the differential equilibrium equation provides the mathematical foundation for determining stress distributions and deformations.&lt;SEP&gt;Numerical formulation for bar elements for which the governing ODE is central, demonstrating thermal stress effects in statically indeterminate systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Rod">
  <data key="d0">Single Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An axial structural element, referred to as Einzelstab, analyzed under linear elastostatics.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural element (also referred to as a bar or Einzelstab) subjected to combined mechanical and thermal loading, governed by the axial displacement ODE.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Weight">
  <data key="d0">Self-Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gravitational load of the structure itself, represented as a constant distributed line load (Streckenlast) in the context of the hanging bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The constant distributed axial load per unit length arising from the total weight of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896991</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Change">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Change</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Temperature Change, commonly denoted by the symbol ΔT (or Delta T), refers to the variation or difference in temperature experienced by an object relative to a reference state. It is typically measured in Kelvin [K] and represents a uniform thermal loading or increase applied to a specific structural component, such as a prismatic bar or rod. In various applications, this uniform temperature difference is applied specifically to discrete sections of these structures, such as Segment 1 (also referred to as Segment ①).

In the context of material science and structural engineering, Temperature Change is a critical factor that directly induces physical deformations. When combined with a material's coefficient of thermal expansion, the uniform temperature difference drives the free thermal expansion of the heated segment. Consequently, this variation in temperature is utilized to calculate the thermal strain component, ultimately contributing to the overall thermal strain and stress experienced by the structural bar or rod.&lt;SEP&gt;The temperature differential ΔT that induces thermal strain, producing only normal strains (Dehnungen) with no shear strains (Gleitungen) in isotropic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The change in temperature, denoted as ΔT, used in calculating thermal strain</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccf2b44f3719aa488a2e1148c9ed36db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Support">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Support</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary condition at the top of the hanging bar (at position 0) where displacement is zero, u(0) = 0.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering representation of a rigid attachment, depicted as a horizontal line with diagonal hatching, indicating that the attached end cannot move.&lt;SEP&gt;Boundary condition u(0) = 0 at the supported end of the structural member where displacement is constrained to zero&lt;SEP&gt;The top support of the hanging bar at position x=0 where the axial displacement is zero.&lt;SEP&gt;A support in the structural system where the boundary condition of zero displacement is enforced.&lt;SEP&gt;A horizontal line with diagonal hatching at the top of the diagram, acting as a rigid anchor for the bar system.&lt;SEP&gt;A fixed, rigid support or boundary condition represented by diagonal hatching lines on the bottom edge of the rectangle, indicating a fixed boundary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Free End">
  <data key="d0">Free End</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary condition at the bottom of the hanging bar (at length l) where the end is free and stress-free, meaning u'(l) = 0.&lt;SEP&gt;Boundary condition u'(l) = 0 at the unconstrained end of the structural member where the strain (and thus stress) is zero&lt;SEP&gt;The stress-free bottom end of the hanging bar located at position x=l.&lt;SEP&gt;The unsupported bottom end of the rod (x = l) which is stress-free (zero strain) and carries no axial load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Axial Load">
  <data key="d0">External Axial Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The external axial load denoted by F, whose line of action coincides with the bar axis (Stabachse).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Stress Principle">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Stress Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental stress principle from which the normal stress equation is derived for a uniaxially loaded structural member.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method of Sections">
  <data key="d0">Method of Sections</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Schnittverfahren, a procedure used to determine the internal normal force resultant by making a cut perpendicular to the bar axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Free Body Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Free Body Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A diagram constructed after making a cut s-s perpendicular to the bar axis, used to apply equilibrium conditions to determine internal forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniform Stress Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Uniform Stress Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assumption that stress is uniformly distributed across the cross-section, valid for bars with constant or weakly varying cross-sectional areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strength of Materials">
  <data key="d0">Strength of Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Festigkeitslehre, a field of study in which the normal stress relationship is foundational, serving as the basis for more complex stress analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;A subject in engineering dealing with the behavior of solid objects subject to stresses and strains, commonly featuring such diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of study in which principal stress analysis is fundamental for determining critical stress states in structural components.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Festigkeitslehre, a subject area typically covered in Volume 2 of the Technische Mechanik textbook series, dealing with the behavior of solid objects subject to stresses and strains.&lt;SEP&gt;A theory dealing with material strength where the decomposition of total internal strain energy into distortional and hydrostatic components is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d9d584003e02eb55f646636643aaba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elasticity Theory">
  <data key="d0">Elasticity Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elasticity Theory, also known in specific contexts as Elastizitätsmodell, is a fundamental branch of physics and mechanics that mathematically describes how solid objects deform under applied stress and return to their original shape upon the removal of these forces. As a broader theoretical framework studied extensively within engineering mechanics—often supported by dedicated textbook content and systematically treated in documents such as section 12.2, Elastizitätsgesetz—it provides the essential context for understanding material behavior under load. This comprehensive framework ultimately leads to the development of failure hypotheses to predict when materials will break or yield.

The theoretical framework relies heavily on rigorous mathematical formulations and foundational mechanical concepts. Free-body diagrams and equilibrium equations serve as primary elements in analyzing the behavior of elastic bodies. Furthermore, the theory is supported by the normal stress relationship and utilizes the symmetric stress tensor as a basis for detailed stress analysis. To accurately formulate its governing mathematical equations, Elasticity Theory employs advanced mathematical tools, including the Taylor expansion. 

Within this mathematical framework, specific metrics are utilized to quantify the elastic behavior of materials. For instance, Poisson's ratio is employed to represent the transverse strain ratio, detailing how materials contract or expand perpendicularly to the direction of the applied load. By combining these mathematical equations, stress tensors, and foundational mechanics principles, Elasticity Theory effectively explains and predicts the complex stresses and deformations experienced by solid objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1f71b01c1aa5c67c36a16a597748fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00185afb9a5d259c31643f50551104c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999116</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Relationships">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Relationships</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Relationships in bar elements that students should learn to understand as part of the learning objectives in the chapter on tension and compression.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Kinematische Beziehungen, relationships connected to the normal stress equation through Hooke's Law and strain calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Geometric relationships describing the deformations or movements in segments of a structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A broader topic in continuum mechanics that encompasses 2D strain analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationships between strains and displacements discussed within the context of strain state.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical relationships connecting strain components (εx, εy, γxy) to displacement fields through differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Relationships discussed in the broader text alongside strain tensors, dealing with the motion of points, bodies, and systems of bodies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dae0b4038721550e833d3e9407ccd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3ee9641e5bb1298b56301c99bff5e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075</data>
