<node id="Elastic Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Elastizitätsmodul, the proportionalitätsfaktor E that linearly maps strain to stress in Hooke's Law.&lt;SEP&gt;The slope of the linear elastic region of the stress-strain curve, which the unloading path runs parallel to.&lt;SEP&gt;A material property (E) used in Hooke's law to relate stress and strain.&lt;SEP&gt;The material property denoted as E in the elongation formula Δl = Nl/EA, representing the stiffness of the bar material</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tension/Compression Testing">
  <data key="d0">Tension/Compression Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Zug- bzw. Druckversuch, a mechanical testing method for which the engineering strain equation is central.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuum Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Continuum Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuum Mechanics is a foundational branch of mechanics, physics, and mechanical engineering that analyzes the kinematics and mechanical behavior of materials modeled as a continuous mass rather than as discrete particles. As an extensive theoretical framework, it provides the bedrock for studying the deformation, stress, and motion of continuous matter, comprehensively covering the essential relationships between kinematic and dynamic quantities. The discipline demands rigorous unit consistency and dimensional homogeneity in its computational practices, establishing canonical formulations for material analysis and serving as a theoretical basis for practical engineering design via stress mapping hypotheses.

A central focus of Continuum Mechanics is the linearized kinematics of deformation, particularly for small deformations. The displacement vector field formulation establishes the kinematic foundation for the field. Within this domain, the Taylor series expansion of displacement fields is applied to derive strain tensors, displacement gradients, and fundamental strain-displacement relations. The discipline defines normal strain—quantifying the relative length change of a material line element originally oriented along a specific axis—and treats technical normal strain and shear strain as fundamental kinematic quantities. Concepts such as the infinitesimal rod element, geometric deformation analysis, and specific geometric constraints (e.g., PQ: dy = 0) are utilized to evaluate the strain state (Verzerrungszustand). Additionally, the discipline bridges mechanical analysis with trigonometric identities, utilizing equations such as the tangent equation for the geometric derivation of rotational angles under small deformations. The field extensively employs symmetric strain tensors and coordinate transformations to analyze plane strain states and shear angles in continuous bodies.

Continuum Mechanics rigorously analyzes stress in three-dimensional continua, encompassing the conceptual transition from stress vectors to the Cauchy stress tensor. Characterized by six independent components and symmetry properties, the stress tensor describes states of stress on mutually perpendicular sectional planes. The field extensively explores the transformation of stress states under coordinate rotation by solving the eigenvalue problem for the Cauchy stress tensor. This includes principal stress analysis—where shear stresses vanish and normal stresses attain extremal maximum and minimum values—and the use of the first stress invariant. Analytical tools such as Mohr's stress circle are routinely applied for the graphical characterization of plane stress conditions, while free-body diagrams, differential and moment equilibrium equations, and finite element computations are utilized to determine critical stress states, maximum shear stress ($\tau_{max} = \frac{\sigma_V}{2}$), and pressure vessel loading.

The theoretical framework of Continuum Mechanics integrates constitutive equations that serve as material laws connecting the stress tensor to the strain tensor. It encompasses the law of elasticity, Hooke's Law, and Hooke's Model, demonstrating the fundamental interdependence of elastic material properties such as Young's Modulus, Poisson's ratio, and Shear Modulus. The field also involves strain energy decomposition, hydrostatic strain energy density, and thermoelastic coupling. It provides the derivation for analyzing material failure and yielding, establishing that hydrostatic stress states do not initiate plastic yielding in standard crystalline metals. Consequently, yield criteria, particularly the von Mises yield criterion and distortion energy analysis, are heavily applied within this domain.

As an academic subject, Continuum Mechanics is a highly technical field of study commonly featured in mechanical engineering literature. The principles of the discipline are routinely illustrated through technical schematic diagrams featuring clean, high-contrast color schemes. A standard reference for these foundational concepts in German engineering education is the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2," which comprehensively covers the mechanics of continuous media.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdbbf7d68148fe88c3174f0ea8e4742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef0d1a935f39ab673c43461c36533f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49d921c1af07b074