<node id="Bar Element">
  <data key="d0">Bar Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Stabelement, a structural member or physical object whose absolute change in length is evaluated when subjected to mechanical loading.&lt;SEP&gt;A one-dimensional structural element (Einzelstab) under axial loading in structural mechanics, analyzed using infinitesimal elements to derive the equilibrium condition.&lt;SEP&gt;A single structural element (Einzelstab) subjected to point forces and distributed line loads, used in trusses, columns, and other load-bearing components in structural mechanics&lt;SEP&gt;A rod or physical element whose thermal expansion and change in length are described by the equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Body">
  <data key="d0">Material Body</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A physical object that undergoes relative elongation or contraction under mechanical loading, quantified by engineering strain.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical object of material, of which the differential element is a representative part.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-153ac624cdef095260ab75841c027b8f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Loading">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The application of forces or loads that causes a material body or bar element to deform.&lt;SEP&gt;Purely mechanical loading, from which thermoelastic coupling represents a critical extension.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Quantity">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Quantity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as kinematische Größe, a classification of physical variables that describe the geometry of deformation, such as strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force-Related Quantity">
  <data key="d0">Force-Related Quantity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Kraftgröße, a classification of physical variables related to forces, such as stress, which complements kinematic quantities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Limit">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted by σ_p, the maximum stress value up to which stress and strain remain linearly proportional, bounding the linear-elastic regime.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress point, marked as $\sigma_P$, representing the maximum stress at which stress remains directly proportional to strain.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum stress (σ_p) at which stress remains directly proportional to strain, relevant to Hooke's Model.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress threshold, denoted as σp, up to which the material exhibits linear-elastic behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress limit (σ ≤ σ_p) within which the superposition principle holds.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress limit (σ_p) used in engineering design verification to ensure linear-elastic behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress limit within which the thermoelastic bar equation and the superposition principle are valid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The phenomenon where a material returns to its original undeformed shape upon unloading, distinguished from permanent plastic strain in a stress-strain diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Bar Truss System">
  <data key="d0">Three-Bar Truss System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A statically indeterminate structural system with two inclined bars (bars 1 and 3) and one vertical bar (bar 2), exhibiting geometric and material symmetry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EA₂">
  <data key="d0">EA₂</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Axial rigidity of the vertical bar (bar 2), defined as Young's modulus E multiplied by cross-sectional area A.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="E (Young's Modulus)">
  <data key="d0">E (Young's Modulus)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material elastic modulus that, when multiplied by cross-sectional area A, gives the axial rigidity of the bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A (Cross-Sectional Area)">
  <data key="d0">A (Cross-Sectional Area)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Geometric property of the bars that, when multiplied by Young's modulus E, gives the axial rigidity EA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="α">
  <data key="d0">α</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Angle representing the inclination of bars 1 and 3 relative to the horizontal axis; when α approaches 90°, the force in diagonal bars approaches zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latex">
  <data key="d0">Latex</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The typesetting format used to represent the mathematical equation in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Lengthening">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Lengthening</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The deformation or change in length of the bars under load, defined by the formula Δl = Sl/EA, which is substituted into the compatibility equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Length Relation">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Length Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric constraint combined with kinematics that contributes to the numerator's cos² α term by projecting node K's displacement onto the inclined bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="N(x) Equation">
  <data key="d0">N(x) Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation N(x) = EA·u'(x) = G(1 - x/l) representing the axial force distribution in a vertically hanging rod under self-weight, combining Hooke's constitutive relation with the equilibrium solution derived from the governing differential equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Force Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Axial Force Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal axial force N(x) at position x along the rod, exhibiting a linear distribution from maximum value G at the top support to zero at the free end.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertically Hanging Rod Under Self-Weight">
  <data key="d0">Vertically Hanging Rod Under Self-Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural system consisting of a rod hanging vertically subjected to its own weight, classified as statically determinate, where support reactions are determined from equilibrium alone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Function">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The function u(x) describing the deformation of the rod along its length, whose first derivative u'(x) represents the axial strain ε(x).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Top Support">
  <data key="d0">Top Support</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The fixed point at the top of the rod (x = 0) where displacement is zero (u(0) = 0) and the internal axial force is at its maximum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">Governing Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation for axial deformation of bars, derived by combining the differential form with kinematic and elasticity laws.&lt;SEP&gt;The mathematical equation combining constitutive relations and equilibrium conditions to determine the structural behavior of the rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compatibility Equation Δl₁ = Δl₂ Cos α">
  <data key="d0">Compatibility Equation Δl₁ = Δl₂ Cos α</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A compatibility equation used in solving the three-bar system, relating the elongations of bars 1 and 2 through the cosine of angle α.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Displacement Δl₂">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Displacement Δl₂</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vertical displacement associated with bar 2 of the three-bar system, determined as part of the solution steps on page 25.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bar Forces S₁,₃">
  <data key="d0">Bar Forces S₁,₃</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The forces in bars 1 and 3 of the three-bar system, determined as part of the solution steps on page 25.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
