<node id="Failure Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Failure Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Failure Analysis is an engineering field and analytical process enabled by structural mechanics that is dedicated to predicting and evaluating material failure. It fundamentally depends on understanding the full stress state of a material and serves as a practical application of stress transformations used to assess structural components and materials. Within this field, the determination of principal angles, principal stresses, and maximum shear stresses is a critical concept. The computed normal stress component σξ is essential to this engineering application, and the principal stresses are specifically utilized to determine the critical loading directions for material failure assessment.

To predict and analyze material failure, Failure Analysis connects stress states to physical failure through established yield criteria and theories, such as the Tresca and von Mises criteria. It also encompasses the analysis of failure due to crack formation (*Versagen durch Rissbildung*), a specific focus area in which the equilibrium equation serves as a fundamental principle.

In practical applications, Failure Analysis involves the evaluation of critical cross-sections prone to failure. A typical focus is the fixed support of a hanging rod, which is analyzed because it is the location where maximum stress occurs. Furthermore, a specific evaluation detailed in the literature (documented on page 29 of its associated text) demonstrates that when assessing pressure vessels, the analysis indicates that rupture occurs in the longitudinal direction of the vessel.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of predicting structural failure in materials, particularly ductile materials, using strength hypotheses like the shear stress hypothesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
