  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rod Elements">
  <data key="d0">Rod Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical elements analyzed under deformation, specifically concerning their location-dependent variable cross-sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a438fa813ee176d3e3d64cd5a7e2dba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nominal/Engineering Area">
  <data key="d0">Nominal/Engineering Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original cross-sectional area of a material, used in contrast to the true cross-sectional area during deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a438fa813ee176d3e3d64cd5a7e2dba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="N = -B = -C">
  <data key="d0">N = -B = -C</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation expressing the relationship between internal normal force N and support reactions B and C, indicating that the normal force is compressive.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Bar">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A rod fixed at both ends ('beidseitig eingespannter Stab') consisting of two sections with cross-sectional areas A1 and A2, subjected to thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Force N">
  <data key="d0">Normal Force N</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal normal force acting on the left face of the infinitesimal element, labeled as N in the diagram, representing the normal force at that cross-section.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal axial force in the statically indeterminate bar, which is constant throughout its length and compressive due to constrained thermal expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;The compressive normal force induced in the rod due to constrained thermal expansion, which is represented by F_x.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Support Reaction B">
  <data key="d0">Support Reaction B</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The reaction force at the left support of the statically indeterminate bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Support Reaction C">
  <data key="d0">Support Reaction C</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The reaction force at the right support of the statically indeterminate bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force Method">
  <data key="d0">Force Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Kraftgrößenverfahren, it is a method for solving statically indeterminate problems by supplementing equilibrium with compatibility conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Alternative solution method (Kraftgrößenverfahren) using superposition of a statically determinate '0'-system (pure thermal expansion) and a '1'-system (unit load)&lt;SEP&gt;A structural analysis technique (Kraftgrößenverfahren) for solving statically indeterminate problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area A1">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area A1</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area of section 1 of the statically indeterminate bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-sectional area of segment 1 of the doubly-clamped rod in m².&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-sectional area of rod segment 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area A2">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area A2</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area of section 2 of the statically indeterminate bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-sectional area of segment 2 of the doubly-clamped rod in m².&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-sectional area of rod segment 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Change Delta T">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Change Delta T</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The uniform temperature change by which section 1 of the statically indeterminate bar is heated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Free Thermal Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Free Thermal Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The unconstrained expansion of a statically determinate system under thermal loading.&lt;SEP&gt;The unconstrained dimensional increase of a material due to a rise in temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Redundant Constraint Force C">
  <data key="d0">Redundant Constraint Force C</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The corrective force accounting for the redundant constraint in the superposition solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hookesches Modell">
  <data key="d0">Hookesches Modell</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A model representing elasticity discussed within the material.&lt;SEP&gt;Hooke's model relating strain to stress using parameters including elastic modulus E, Poisson's ratio ν, shear modulus G, thermal expansion coefficient αT, and temperature change ΔT, with the relationship G = E / (2(1+ν))&lt;SEP&gt;Hooke's model describing linear elastic material behavior, valid in any Cartesian coordinate system, with the same principal directions as the stress tensor, also applicable in 3D under temperature changes&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for Hooke's Model, referring to material behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Hookesches Modell</data>
</node>
<node id="Elongation Equation">
  <data key="d0">Elongation Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation representing kinematic compatibility relations for elongation of two serially-connected rod segments in a statically indeterminate system, combining mechanical and thermal elongation components</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Segment 1">
  <data key="d0">Segment 1</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">First rod segment subject to both mechanical elongation (Nl/(EA₁)) and thermal elongation (αT·ΔT·l), with cross-sectional area A₁&lt;SEP&gt;The left segment of the horizontal bar, colored light gray and subjected to a uniform temperature increase.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df979c16e8c52bf800fb84c3a4a4bdea</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle Of Superposition">
  <data key="d0">Principle Of Superposition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principle allowing decomposition of total strain into independent mechanical and thermal components: ε = σ/E + αT·ΔT&lt;SEP&gt;An alternative solution method decomposing the thermal loading problem into a free-expansion case (state 0) and a force-correcting case (state 1).&lt;SEP&gt;A principle used to extend the uniaxial stress-strain relationship to account for multiaxial stress states.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle in linear elasticity stating that the total strain in one direction is the sum of the axial strain from direct normal stress and the transverse strain from perpendicular normal stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Elongation">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Elongation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The component of total elongation derived from Hooke's Law for axial loading, represented mathematically as Nl/(EA)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Elongation">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Elongation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The linear thermal strain contribution to total elongation, represented mathematically as αT·ΔT·l</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Support Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Support Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The unknown forces at the fixed supports of the compound rod, denoted as B and C, constrained by the equilibrium condition B − C = 0</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="'0'-System">
  <data key="d0">'0'-System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A statically determinate system representing pure thermal expansion, used as a component in the force method solution</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="'1'-System">
  <data key="d0">'1'-System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit load system used as a component in the force method solution</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Generalized Hooke's Law, also known by its German designations as Elastizitätsgesetz, verallgemeinertes Hookesches Gesetz, or generalisiertes Hookesches Gesetz, is the foundational constitutive equation of linear elasticity theory. It serves as a physical law defining the linear elastic relationship between stress and strain tensors, specifically for homogeneous and isotropic materials.

This fundamental principle is highly versatile and is applied across various dimensional states and coordinate systems. In a three-dimensional, isotropic, linearly elastic continuum, the law defines both normal and shear relationships. It explicitly relates principal strain components to principal normal stresses under a 3D state of stress and outlines the shear stress-shear strain relationships. When formulated within the principal axis system, it provides a direct constitutive relation expressed entirely in terms of these principal stresses and strains. Additionally, the law is adapted for two-dimensional scenarios, such as a biaxial state of stress. In these cases, it calculates normal strain in a specific direction—for example, in the x-direction—as a function of applied normal stresses using material properties like Young's modulus (E) and Poisson's ratio (ν), mathematically represented by the equation ε_x = (1/E)(σ_x − ν·σ_y).

Beyond standard mechanical loading, Generalized Hooke's Law is extended to incorporate thermal effects for a more comprehensive analysis of material behavior. This formulation allows the constitutive equation to compute normal strain components, such as the principal strain in the x-direction, for a three-dimensional, isotropic, linear-elastic material that is subjected to both mechanical forces and temperature variations simultaneously.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Elasticity Theory">
  <data key="d0">Linear Elasticity Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Linear Elasticity Theory is a fundamental theoretical framework and branch of continuum mechanics that describes how solid objects deform and become stressed under applied loads. It specifically studies the behavior of elastic materials under loading, relying on a linear behavioral model that is centrally dependent on the small-strain assumption.

Within this framework, constitutive equations are used to relate stress and strain in a strictly linear manner. The generalized Hooke's Law serves as the foundational constitutive equation for this theory. Linear Elasticity Theory establishes the broader framework within which key mechanical relationships are formulated, including the constitutive relationship for uniaxial normal stress and the defining relationship between Young's Modulus and Shear Modulus. 

Additionally, the theory provides the necessary analytical basis for understanding and evaluating specific physical phenomena and structural changes. It is extensively utilized to describe general material deformation, the Poisson effect, and to apply the geometric analysis of shear deformation in solid materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64914dffaa71b279b32225287f51b6fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b73317ca6218a3794fd92d7cfec3e4db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999248</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cauchy Stress Tensor, also referred to as the Spannungstensor, is a fundamental second-rank tensor utilized in continuum mechanics and solid mechanics to comprehensively characterize the state of stress at a specific point within a deformable body or material under load. Because stress at a point cannot be fully described by a single vector, this mathematical construct maps the unit normal vector of any cutting plane to the corresponding traction vector, representing internal force intensities typically measured in units of MPa or N/mm². It is generally represented as a 3×3 symmetric matrix in three-dimensional space or as a 2×2 symmetric tensor for two-dimensional plane stress states.

A defining characteristic of the Cauchy Stress Tensor, commonly denoted as σ with components σ_ij, is its inherent symmetry. Established through moment equilibrium conditions about the coordinate axes through a given point, the tensor satisfies the matrix condition σ = σ^T. This means that the off-diagonal shear components are equal across the main diagonal (e.g., σ_xy = σ_yx, σ_xz = σ_zx, and σ_yz = σ_zy). This symmetry property effectively reduces the number of independent stress components from nine to six.

The mathematical properties of the Cauchy Stress Tensor dictate how stress components transform between different orthogonal Cartesian coordinate systems. Its transformation behavior, which gives rise to double-angle formulations under coordinate rotation, is governed by specific geometric rules and stress transformation equations. Solving the eigenvalue problem for this tensor yields its eigenvalues, which correspond to the principal stresses acting on planes where the shear stress completely vanishes. Additionally, the tensor features mathematical invariants, notably its first invariant denoted as I₁.

In specific loading scenarios, the general representation of the stress tensor simplifies into distinct diagonal forms. For example, in a two-dimensional plane stress state under uniaxial tension where the only non-zero normal stress acts along the x-axis (σ_x = σ_0), the tensor reduces to a 2×2 diagonal matrix represented as diag(σ_0, 0), with zero shear components (τ_xy = τ_yx = 0). Similarly, a state characterized by equal principal normal stresses combined with zero off-diagonal shear stress components will also result in a simplified diagonal matrix representation.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical tensor used in continuum mechanics, whose eigenvalues correspond to the principal normal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical tensor whose eigenvalues correspond to the principal normal stresses acting at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
