<node id="Poisson's Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Poisson's Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Poisson's Ratio is a dimensionless material property and elastic constant that measures the deformation of a material in directions perpendicular to the specific direction of loading. Commonly denoted by the Greek letter ν (nu) or 'v', and also known in German as "Querkontraktionszahl," it fundamentally defines the ratio of transverse strain (lateral contraction or expansion) to axial strain (longitudinal extension). This parameter captures and scales the "Poisson effect"—or Poisson coupling effect—which describes the lateral contraction induced by an applied normal stress or orthogonal stresses. For stable, homogeneous, and isotropic materials, this dimensionless scalar typically ranges from 0.0 to 0.5.

In the fields of elasticity theory and continuum mechanics, Poisson's Ratio serves as a vital parameter used to relate stresses and strains within the elasticity law. It emerges from the superposition of strains in orthogonal directions and acts as a scalar multiplier in constitutive equations, famously appearing in the generalized Hooke's Law equation through the term −ν·σ_y. Furthermore, it is extensively utilized in the calculation of mechanical strain, principal strains, and the internal energy of a system.

Poisson's Ratio is one of the two independent constants required to fully characterize the elastic behavior of isotropic materials. Within this framework, it is intricately linked to other fundamental material constants. Specifically, when paired with Young's modulus, it is equivalent to the Lamé parameters. Additionally, Poisson's Ratio is actively used to define the isotropic mathematical relationship between Young's modulus and the shear modulus, solidifying its foundational importance in material science and structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1f71b01c1aa5c67c36a16a597748fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddbe1099c718b59c4f95b165b71f72ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b73317ca6218a3794fd92d7cfec3e4db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b7586a6b498b557c286e9f48cc5b98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
