<node id="Cartesian Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cartesian Coordinate System is an orthogonal reference framework utilized extensively in technical, mathematical, and engineering contexts to define spatial points, physical quantities, and graphical relationships. Characterized by perpendicular axes that provide precise spatial orientation, it commonly consists of a horizontal x-axis pointing to the right and a vertical y-axis pointing upward. In two-dimensional applications, this system is often visually highlighted in blue and establishes spatial orientation, defining the geometric constraints of features such as line segments connecting distinct points (e.g., points P and Q). The origin of this system can be centrally located at a specific point, such as point P, or anchored to a bottom-left vertex. In three-dimensional spatial contexts, the Cartesian Coordinate System operates as a right-handed framework comprising x, y, and z axes.

In the fields of material mechanics and stress analysis, the Cartesian Coordinate System is frequently overlaid on differential material elements to specify their location and orientation in space. It is essential for executing coordinate transformations, such as translating data between an original x,y system and a rotated eta,xi system. Furthermore, this coordinate system provides a valid framework for the application of generalized Hooke's Law, linear isotropic elasticity models, and shear stress-shear strain equations in any arbitrary orientation.

Additionally, the Cartesian Coordinate System serves as the mathematical plotting foundation for analytical diagrams, including Mohr's Circle and stress-strain diagrams. When applied to Mohr's Circle, the system utilizes perpendicular axes intersecting at the origin to map the state of stress as a locus of points. The horizontal axis represents the normal stress component (often labeled as sigma), while the vertical axis represents the shear stress component (often labeled as tau). These axes typically feature directional arrows indicating positive values, allowing for a clear, standardized visual representation of complex material behaviors and stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90cfec891927772de90cde9aa609de48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
