<node id="Stress Vector">
  <data key="d0">Stress Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Stress Vector, also referred to in German as the Spannungsvektor, is a foundational concept in continuum mechanics and engineering stress analysis. It represents the internal forces acting across a surface within a material, specifically defining the state of stress—or force per unit area—at a distinct point on a cross-section or sectional plane under loading. The vector is typically constructed for a cutting surface whose normal points in a specific coordinate direction, such as the x-direction or the xi-axis. Once established, the stress vector can be mathematically decomposed into fundamental directional quantities, specifically normal stresses and shear stresses.

Despite its utility, a single Stress Vector is inherently limited in its capacity to describe the complete stress state at an arbitrary point. Because the vector is highly dependent on both its precise location and the chosen cutting direction, relying on only one vector is insufficient for a comprehensive analysis. To overcome this limitation and fully describe the three-dimensional stress state at a point, multiple individual stress vectors are systematically arranged to construct a stress tensor, or matrix. 

This progression from a single directional vector to a comprehensive tensor theory in three-dimensional bodies is a core component of advanced mechanics analysis. The Stress Vector is frequently introduced early in this systematic development within academic literature. For instance, it is formally presented as a complex directional stress quantity in Chapter 11, Section 11.1, of German technical mechanics textbooks, serving as an essential building block for understanding material stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress tensor, also known as the Cauchy stress tensor or Spannungstensor and denoted as σ or σ_ij, is a fundamental second-order, symmetric tensor used in continuum mechanics, solid mechanics, and engineering stress analysis. It serves as a complete mathematical object that characterizes the physical state of stress (Spannungszustand) and the internal force distribution at any given point within a deformable body. Because a single stress vector is insufficient to fully describe the stress state at a point, the stress tensor was developed to represent it using three stress vectors on mutually perpendicular planes.

Structurally, the stress tensor is represented by a 3x3 matrix consisting of nine components. It utilizes a double-index notation, where the first index denotes the axis normal to the plane and the second indicates the direction of the stress vector. The main diagonal of the matrix contains the normal stresses (σ_xx, σ_yy, σ_zz), while the off-diagonal components represent the shear stresses (τ_ij). Due to the principle of symmetry (σ_ij = σ_ji), these nine components are coupled through equilibrium requirements, reducing the independent entries to six for the general case. In specific scenarios, such as plane stress analysis, the tensor is often represented by a 2x2 matrix (e.g., [σx, τxy; τxy, σy]) with only three independent components.

Mathematically, while the individual component values change under coordinate rotation according to a specific rotational prescription, the underlying physical stress state remains invariant. The tensor possesses specific mathematical invariants, including its trace (the sum of its normal stresses) and its determinant. The eigenvalues of the stress tensor correspond to the principal stresses, and its eigenvectors represent the principal directions. When expressed in this principal coordinate system, the stress tensor simplifies into a diagonal matrix. Additionally, the total stress tensor can be decomposed into hydrostatic and deviatoric components to analyze volumetric and distortional effects separately.

The stress tensor shares a direct structural analogy and identical transformation equations with the strain tensor. It is fundamentally connected to the strain tensor through the constitutive equation or constitutive matrix in continuum mechanics. In linear elastic materials, such as those described by Hooke's Model, the stress tensor shares the same principal directions as the strain tensor. Furthermore, the mathematical construct of the stress tensor shares the same principal axes as the shear constitutive relations in Cartesian coordinate systems. This systematic formulation is a staple in technical mechanics curricula, typically introduced through a progressive build-up from the foundational stress vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ebf6d69a9ef72ba2950206908d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddbe1099c718b59c4f95b165b71f72ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
