<node id="Shear Stress">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear stress, also known as *Schubspannung* in German, is a foundational concept in engineering mechanics that describes the tangential component of a stress vector acting parallel to a surface, cross-section, or material face. It is universally denoted by the Greek letter tau ($\tau$) and often utilizes double-index notation, such as $\tau_{ij}$ or $\tau_{xy}$, to specify the stress component acting on the face normal to $i$ in the direction of $j$. Mathematically, this component is obtained by decomposing the stress vector and can be expressed as $\tau = |t - (t \cdot n)n|$. It is quantified in units of pressure, such as Pascals (Pa) or megapascals (MPa).

Within the stress tensor, shear stress components occupy the off-diagonal positions. Due to moment equilibrium and the inherent symmetry property of the stress tensor, these components are not entirely independent; the six possible shear stresses reduce to three independent pairs (e.g., $\tau_{xy} = \tau_{yx}$). Shear stress components can vary across differential distances based on partial derivatives and are entirely absent in a hydrostatic stress state. Mechanically, shear stress dictates a material's resistance to shear deformation, causing material elements and crystal lattices to skew. In the context of isotropic elastic behavior and the theory of elasticity (*Elastizitätsgesetz*) for plates (*Scheiben*), it forms a fundamental relationship alongside shear strain and the shear modulus.

Shear stress plays a critical role in coordinate transformation analysis, where its magnitude depends on the cutting angle. A defining characteristic of shear stress is its behavior on principal planes (*Hauptrichtungen*); shear stress components vanish to zero precisely at these principal directions, which is where normal stresses reach their extremal values. In contrast, shear stress reaches its own maximum extremal magnitudes on planes oriented exactly 45 degrees to the principal directions. Under specific biaxial stress states, this maximum shear stress is recognized to be equal to the applied normal stress ($\sigma$).

In graphical representations, shear stress is a fundamental element of Mohr's Circle. In this Cartesian coordinate system, shear stress ($\tau$) is plotted along the vertical axis, and its components are used to calculate the circle's radius, which corresponds to the maximum shear stress magnitude. Visually, shear stresses are commonly depicted as horizontal and vertical arrows—often illustrated as pairs of thick red arrows pointing in opposite directions—running parallel to the faces of an infinitesimal material element to indicate the directional, tangential forces applied to the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-011c3fc433230965d7a734e4052f7689&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49d921c1af07b0743c98652a77081e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7d19da473048f7577c45363a2702e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fe71d7fb091b493303186868a143418</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Plane Stress State, also known by its German term "ebener Spannungszustand" (ESZ), is a fundamental concept and idealization in solid and continuum mechanics describing a two-dimensional state of stress. This condition occurs when a body's thickness is negligible compared to its other dimensions, such as in thin plates, disks, or thin-walled vessels, and the loading is confined entirely to the object's own plane. In this state, the stress distribution is constant across the thickness, and the forces or stress components acting in one of the three mutually perpendicular planes—typically the z-direction—are zero. 

Characterized by normal and shear stresses acting within a single plane, the Plane Stress State is primarily defined by the stress components σx, σy, and τxy (where τxy = τyx). These components form a 2x2 symmetric stress matrix that can be mathematically analyzed within a two-dimensional (x, y) coordinate system. For instance, in a specific engineering worked example, this condition is represented by a tensile normal stress σx of 50 MPa, a compressive normal stress σy of -20 MPa, and a shear stress τxy of 30 MPa. Another notable manifestation is the uniaxial tension case, a specific Plane Stress State where the shear stress components are entirely zero.

In engineering mechanics, the Plane Stress State serves as the foundational basis for performing two-dimensional stress transformations and evaluating failure criteria. The transformation of these original stress components is highly relevant to the rotation angle φ and the transformed normal stress σ_ξ. This analytical process frequently utilizes the stress invariant equation and the normal stress transformation law. Engineers commonly employ Mohr's Circle to visually and mathematically analyze these 2D stress conditions, allowing them to accurately determine the principal stresses and maximum shear stresses experienced by infinitesimal elements.

A prominent practical application of the Plane Stress State (ESZ) is found in the structural analysis of thin-walled vessels. Under the ESZ assumption, radial stresses in the vessel wall are considered negligible. Consequently, the analysis is simplified, rendering the longitudinal and circumferential stresses (often denoted as σ_x and σ_φ) as the principal stresses. In this specific state, the vessel wall experiences these principal stresses without the presence of accompanying shear stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional state of stress (ebener Spannungszustand, ESZ) whose generalized elasticity law is derived from the uniaxial Hooke's law.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional state of stress present in materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state concept known as ebener Spannungszustand (ESZ), representing the plane stress condition in homogeneous and isotropic elastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress state known as ebener Spannungszustand (ESZ), for which the constitutive equation is specifically formulated</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ba563b25b03c792bf6e4d826fd0f062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e2cb25fd70f2b5f3b6c22629fe461a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1685984065ed0dcd54dea046d7d4f918&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b73317ca6218a3794fd92d7cfec3e4db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb63bc877af3e54e4e227db32248b763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
