<node id="Coordinate Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Coordinate Transformation, also referred to as "Koordinatentransformation" in German, is a fundamental mathematical procedure and engineering mechanics concept used to convert stress components between different coordinate systems. Primarily applied within continuum mechanics, it focuses on analyzing two-dimensional plane stress states. The procedure involves examining the stresses at a specific point on a disk or plane by rotating the reference frame. For example, it allows for the transformation of stress components from a standard x,y-coordinate system into a rotated η,ξ-system, a process characterized by a specific angle φ when cuts are set arbitrarily and perpendicularly.

Through its associated mathematical derivations and transformation equations for both normal and shear stresses, Coordinate Transformation effectively demonstrates that stress acts as a second-order tensor. A key physical principle of this process is that while the individual stress components change with the rotation of the coordinate system, certain properties remain invariant; notably, the sum of the normal stresses remains constant regardless of the reference frame's orientation. 

In academic and structural contexts, such as the *Technische Mechanik 2* lecture materials, Coordinate Transformation serves as a crucial theoretical framework. Detailed in specific academic sections, the surrounding German mechanics text provides the necessary context for understanding the physical principles and mathematical derivations. This topic typically acts as the foundational theoretical step that directly precedes and informs the discussion of principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure involving rotation by angle φ to transform strain components between coordinate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical procedure for transforming tensor components between different coordinate systems, used to determine principal directions and strains.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical procedure in continuum mechanics for which the symmetric strain tensor's use of half the engineering shear strain is essential.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec30ad8072c74dbe906914b85d9353a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
