<node id="Transformation Equations">
  <data key="d0">Transformation Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation Equations are mathematical formulas utilized for coordinate transformations and rotations, such as those involving an angle to the ξη-system. They are widely applied in tensor transformations, mechanics, and strain analysis. These equations serve the fundamental purpose of relating physical quantities between an original coordinate system and a rotated one, demonstrating that the intrinsic state of these quantities remains unchanged regardless of the observational coordinate frame.

In the context of stress analysis, the Transformation Equations are used to convert stress components between different rotated coordinate systems. By mathematically relating the original and rotated stress components, the equations establish that stresses represent the exact same physical stress state in any given coordinate system. 

Similarly, in strain analysis, these equations are utilized for the transformation of strain components during coordinate rotations. They allow for the precise determination of strain components on different coordinate planes, operating on mathematical principles that are directly analogous to stress transformations. In an academic setting, the application and derivation of these Transformation Equations are thoroughly covered in university lecture content, specifically detailed in section 11.2.1 "Koordinatentransformation" of the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2".</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec30ad8072c74dbe906914b85d9353a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8f3f8c72a4f9a9fdeabe99c8c8ba4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Formation of the equilibrium of moments around an axis parallel to the x-axis through the center, used to establish that shear stresses are not independent of each other&lt;SEP&gt;A physical condition where moments acting on an infinitesimal volume element generate equal and opposite moments about an axis through its center point.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle in classical mechanics where the algebraic sum of all moments (torques) acting on a body equals zero, ensuring a state of static rotational equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical principle (Momentengleichgewicht) about the balance of moments about axes parallel to coordinate axes, from which the symmetry of the stress tensor is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical state where the sum of all moments is zero, used to derive the symmetry of shear stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff</data>
