<node id="Kesselformeln">
  <data key="d0">Kesselformeln</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kesselformeln, commonly referred to as boiler formulas or vessel formulas, are fundamental equations used in the engineering and technical context of pressure vessel design. Derived from elementary equilibrium principles, these formulas are primarily utilized for the stress analysis of thin-walled cylinders and pressure vessels. They are used to derive the stress states within these structures, often corresponding directly to the schematic diagrams used in technical analyses.

The core function of the Kesselformeln is to calculate the longitudinal, circumferential, and tangential stresses acting on the walls of a thin-walled vessel. A crucial mechanical principle established by these boiler formulas is that the hoop stress (sigma_phi) is exactly twice the longitudinal stress (sigma_x). Because they outline the fundamental stress parameters for thin-walled cylinders, these equations frequently serve as the primary heading preceding more detailed derivations in engineering literature.

Furthermore, the Kesselformeln are highly versatile in their application to different loading conditions. While they are standardly applied to vessels under internal pressure, they are equally applicable to externally pressurized vessels. In scenarios involving external pressure, the sign of the pressure variable is simply changed. This adjustment correctly accounts for the reversal in force, allowing the formulas to accurately calculate and obtain the resulting compressive stresses within the vessel walls.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35913765c2d7101f6d284ecd039952ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dünnwandiger Kessel">
  <data key="d0">Dünnwandiger Kessel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A thin-walled vessel analyzed under the assumption of a plane stress state (ESZ), characterized by radius and wall thickness, subjected to internal or external pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin-walled, spherical vessel with a radius subjected to internal pressure, producing tangential and circumferential stresses&lt;SEP&gt;German term for thin-walled vessels, introduced in section 11.2.4 as an engineering application in pressure vessel design.&lt;SEP&gt;A section (11.2.4) of the German stress analysis document covering thin-walled vessel theory, including derivation of longitudinal and circumferential (hoop) stresses, vessel formulas, and principal stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;German for "thin-walled pressure vessels", a topic covered in the section where the footer citation appears.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical mechanics concept referring to thin-walled pressure vessels discussed in the academic material.&lt;SEP&gt;A German term for thin-walled vessel, mentioned as the surrounding context (Section 11.2.4) for the engineering calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903914</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spannungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Spannungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of stress at a point, determined by three stress vectors in three mutually perpendicular sectional areas.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress state at a point of a body, defined by the stress tensor components&lt;SEP&gt;A topic related to stress states covered in the lecture content.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal stress state within a material under load, analyzed at specific points within the block depicted in the schematic diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;German technical term for State of Stress, which is the main focus of Chapter 11.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for stress states, representing the central topic summarized in Chapter Section 11.4 Zusammenfassung.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress state, a stress analysis topic covered in a German-language lecture presentation on technical mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59edb59253a1c7bdee6d99d3f2cc4d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spannungstensor">
  <data key="d0">Spannungstensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">"Spannungstensor" is the German technical term for the stress tensor, most commonly referring to the Cauchy stress tensor. As a fundamental concept and key mechanical principle, it is extensively utilized and discussed within the fields of continuum mechanics and engineering mechanics. It is frequently featured in technical literature, including textbooks such as *Technische Mechanik 2*, and is often visually depicted in related educational graphics.

Mathematically, the Spannungstensor represents the internal stress state of a continuous material. It is formed by arranging the components of the stress vectors derived from three mutually perpendicular sectional areas. In a standard three-dimensional case, this mathematical entity is represented by a 3x3 matrix of components that notably exhibits symmetry. 

The properties of the Spannungstensor are highly applicable to various mechanical and mathematical models. For instance, when evaluating a plane stress state, the tensor's three-dimensional components are appropriately reduced. The behavioral properties of the Spannungstensor in this specific plane stress state can be analogously transferred to the strain tensor. Furthermore, the Spannungstensor shares the same principal directions as Hooke's model, demonstrating its integral role in understanding material deformation and mechanical behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964375</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Spannungstensor</data>
</node>
<node id="Gleichgewichtsbedingungen">
  <data key="d0">Gleichgewichtsbedingungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium conditions that link the components of the stress tensor together through partial differential equations&lt;SEP&gt;A technical mechanics concept referring to equilibrium conditions discussed in the academic material.&lt;SEP&gt;Equilibrium conditions in solid mechanics that the mathematical equation is designed to satisfy, ensuring static equilibrium within an infinitesimal element.&lt;SEP&gt;Equilibrium conditions, a topic in technical mechanics covered in a German-language lecture presentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ebener Spannungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Ebener Spannungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ebener Spannungszustand, commonly abbreviated as ESZ, is the German term for the "plane stress state," a key mechanical principle frequently discussed in technical literature such as the textbook *Technische Mechanik 2*. This concept refers to a two-dimensional condition in which the stress components are analyzed exclusively within a single plane. By assuming a plane stress state, the standard three-dimensional stress tensor is simplified and reduced to just three independent components: sigma_x, sigma_y, and tau_xy. This mechanical state is often visualized and analyzed using specific diagrams designed for two-dimensional stress conditions.

In practical engineering applications, the Ebener Spannungszustand is frequently assumed for the structural analysis of thin-walled vessels. In these scenarios, the stresses acting in the radial direction can be safely neglected, provided that the wall thickness of the vessel is significantly smaller than its overall radius. Furthermore, when analyzing this two-dimensional stress condition, the governing elasticity law is typically formulated under the assumption that the material exhibits both homogeneous and isotropic properties.

Despite simplifying the stress state to only two dimensions, the Ebener Spannungszustand ultimately leads to a spatial, three-dimensional state of strain. This mechanical phenomenon occurs through the principle of superposition, linking the simplified 2D stress analysis to its broader 3D physical effects on the material.&lt;SEP&gt;The plane stress state (ESZ), a section within the elasticity law content covering stress-strain relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;The plane stress state of a biaxially loaded plate, which is analyzed to derive the mathematical relationship between G, E, and nu.</data>
