<node id="Principal Directions">
  <data key="d0">Principal Directions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Directions, also widely referred to as "Hauptrichtungen" in German, are orthogonal sectional directions or planes along which principal stresses act. In the study of technical mechanics, these directions are identified by examining the extremal values of normal and shear stresses. Specifically, Principal Directions are the two perpendicular orientations, separated by an angle of π/2, at which normal stresses attain their maximum and minimum extreme values. Concurrently, the shear stress completely vanishes along these orientations.

Mathematically, the angles defining these directions, typically denoted as φ* (and separated by π/2 due to the periodicity of the tangent function), are determined by the equation tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x - σ_y). Because the shear stress τξη drops to zero at these specific angles, Principal Directions directly correspond to the eigenvectors of the stress tensor. Additionally, these orientations can be determined graphically using Mohr's circle construction. 

A practical example of this concept is observed in cases of uniaxial tension, where the Principal Directions are oriented at φ* = 0° and φ** = 90°. This indicates that the principal stress directions align directly with the applied loading direction. Whether derived through trigonometric extremal values, stress tensor eigenvectors, or geometric constructions, these directions are fundamental for analyzing stress states in technical mechanics lecture documents (such as Section 11.2.2).&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as 'Hauptrichtungen', a critical concept in plane strain analysis covered on page 8.&lt;SEP&gt;Specific orientations related to strain state that are discussed in the mechanics document.&lt;SEP&gt;Specific orientations (Hauptrichtungen) identified through coordinate transformation, in which principal strains occur with no shear component.&lt;SEP&gt;Orientations corresponding to the principal strains, also known as Hauptrichtungen.&lt;SEP&gt;Eigenvectors of the strain tensor, also referred to as Hauptrichtungen.&lt;SEP&gt;The directions along which the normal strains are analyzed in the geometric derivation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3ee9641e5bb1298b56301c99bff5e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999089</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Axis System">
  <data key="d0">Principal Axis System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Principal Axis System, also known by its German name Hauptachsensystem, is a specific coordinate system utilized extensively in technical mechanics. Its defining characteristic is that its axes are aligned strictly parallel to the principal directions of stress. For instance, the system's 1-axis and 2-axis directly align with the principal stress directions, denoted as σ1 and σ2. A fundamental property of this framework is that within it, all shear stresses are zero. 

Because of these properties, the Principal Axis System serves as an essential reference framework for stress analysis and is a central topic within stress transformation theory. It is frequently detailed in technical mechanics literature—often discussed in dedicated sections regarding principal stresses, such as section 11.2.2—to facilitate the evaluation of stress states.

Beyond stress analysis, the Principal Axis System is fundamental to the study of material elasticity and solid mechanics. It provides the specific coordinate framework in which the constitutive equations for linear elastic materials are presented and formulated. Within this system, the strain equation and the generalized Hooke's Law are mathematically expressed, and the derivations of the relationships between various elastic constants are performed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Shear Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Principal Shear Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The extreme values of shear stresses, calculated from the derivative of the shear stress equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Referred to as Hauptschubspannungen, these are extremal values of shear stresses calculated during stress transformation analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stresses calculated during stress transformation, used to predict material yielding or fracture.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum and minimum shear stresses found through geometric construction of Mohr's circle, alongside principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Extreme shear stress values (Hauptschubspannungen) identified using Mohr's Stress Circle construction methodology.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stresses at a point, referred to as Hauptschubspannungen in German, determined graphically using Mohr's Stress Circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903843</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Stress Circle">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Stress Circle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohr's Stress Circle, also known by its German name *Mohrscher Spannungskreis*, is a graphical method named after Otto Mohr that is widely utilized in continuum mechanics and engineering stress analysis. It serves as a visual and analytical framework for representing, computing, and transforming the state of stress at a given point. The method is primarily applied to two-dimensional or plane stress states, allowing for the graphical determination of principal stresses, principal directions, extreme or maximum shear stresses, and overall stress tensor transformations. 

The technique operates by constructing a circle within the sigma-tau ($\sigma$-$\tau$) plane, which represents normal stress and shear stress, respectively. The circle is defined by specific geometric properties—namely its center (such as a mean stress, $\sigma_m$, which may be located at a specific value like 15 MPa in given problem contexts) and its radius—which are derived from known initial normal and shear stresses. A fundamental geometric principle of this method is that physical angles of rotation ($\phi$) in the material's cross-section are represented as double the angle ($2\phi$) with an opposite sign on the circle. Through this geometric rotation and construction, engineers can evaluate stresses acting on arbitrary cross-sections and identify extreme values.

In educational and instructional contexts, such as the German engineering mechanics textbook *Technische Mechanik 2* (often discussed in dedicated sections like topic 11.2.3), Mohr's Stress Circle is taught as a foundational step-by-step procedural methodology. The curriculum covers the general theory of biaxial stress states alongside specific special cases, providing a comprehensive framework for analyzing uniaxial tension, pure shear stress states, and hydrostatic stress states.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical representation in (σ, τ) space depicting stress transformation, serving as the structural analog for Mohr's strain circle.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical method for analyzing stress states referenced as a direct analogy for constructing Mohr's strain circle.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical representation for stress transformation plotted with coordinates (σ, τ).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165c1196600ad5f85ab89d1be16e9bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e8c155e741c754b0f477a1a09ae9dc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
