<node id="Page 13 - Coordinate Transformation Stress Equations (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 13 - Coordinate Transformation Stress Equations (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '13' located at bottom-left of page 12 (index). Situated within section 11.2.1 on coordinate transformation for plane stress states. The page presents key stress transformation formulas including σ_ξ calculation using Mohr's circle trigonometric relations and the method for determining σ_η through π/2 rotation. Serves as reference point for the mathematical derivation of stress components under arbitrary section angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b5475536a7fccb42b9f6f9e39d32b77</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Equation for Stress Transformation in the ξ-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Equation for Stress Transformation in the ξ-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This static equilibrium equation balances the force components acting along the rotated ξ-axis on an infinitesimal triangular element. By projecting the known x-y stress components onto the inclined surface, it mathematically derives the 2D coordinate transformation for normal stress. It explicitly demonstrates that stress components transform according to specific trigonometric rules, thereby proving that the state of stress is a second-order tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress Equilibrium Equation in Rotated Coordinates (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Equilibrium Equation in Rotated Coordinates (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Force equilibrium in the η-direction for an infinitesimal triangular element extracted from a 2D stress body. Projects all stress components from the x,y-system onto the rotated ξ,η-system. This equation is the key intermediate step in deriving the tensor transformation law for plane stress, demonstrating that stresses transform as second-order tensors under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Infinitesimal Wedge Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Infinitesimal Wedge Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram depicts an infinitesimal triangular element used to derive the coordinate transformation equations for plane stress. It shows the relationship between stress components in the original $x,y$ coordinate system ($\\sigma_x, \\sigma_y, \\tau_{xy}$) and those in a rotated $\\xi,\\eta$ system ($\\sigma_\\xi, \\tau_{\\xi\\eta}$) defined by the angle $\\varphi$. The visual serves as a fundamental proof that stress is a second-order tensor whose components change predictably with rotation, maintaining the same physical state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress Transformation Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Transformation Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation transforms the shear stress component τ_xy from the original (x,y) coordinate system to the shear stress τ_ξη in a rotated (ξ,η) coordinate system at angle φ. It is one of the three stress transformation equations demonstrating that stress is a second-rank tensor. The relation is essential for determining principal stresses, principal directions, and maximum shear stresses in plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation for Orthogonal Normal Stress (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation for Orthogonal Normal Stress (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal stress $\sigma_{\eta}$ on an orthogonal plane by applying the 2D stress transformation formula at a rotation angle of $\varphi + \pi/2$. It demonstrates how stress components transform under coordinate rotation, serving as fundamental proof that the state of stress is a second-order tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Equation for $\sigma_{\eta}$ (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Equation for $\sigma_{\eta}$ (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal stress component $\sigma_{\eta}$ in a rotated coordinate system by applying a $\pi/2$ angular shift to the formula for $\sigma_{\xi}$. It uses basic trigonometric identities to demonstrate how stress components transform under rotation, establishing the mathematical foundation for the stress tensor in a 2D plane stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
