<node id="Normal Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation (2D Stress Tensor Transformation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation (2D Stress Tensor Transformation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This fundamental equation from continuum mechanics describes how the normal stress component transforms under rotation of the coordinate system in 2D. It demonstrates the tensor nature of stress, decomposing the transformed stress into invariant hydrostatic and direction-dependent deviatoric components, forming the basis for principal stress analysis and Mohr's circle constructions in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Equation (σ_η Component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Equation (σ_η Component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation transforms the normal stress component σ_η under a 2D coordinate rotation by angle φ. As part of the Cauchy stress tensor transformation, it demonstrates the second-rank tensor property of stress and connects to invariants like the trace σ_x + σ_y. Essential for principal stress analysis and Mohr's circle construction in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="First Invariant of the Stress Tensor (Trace) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">First Invariant of the Stress Tensor (Trace) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the first invariant of the 2D stress tensor, demonstrating that the trace (sum of normal stresses) remains unchanged during a coordinate transformation. It mathematically proves that stress is a second-rank tensor because its physical manifestation is independent of the observational coordinate system's rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 16 - Koordinatentransformation Sonderfall (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 16 - Koordinatentransformation Sonderfall (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 16 in a German mechanics textbook, located within section 11.2.1 on coordinate transformation. This page introduces a special case (Sonderfall) of the hydrostatic stress state where normal stresses are direction-independent and shear stresses vanish. It follows discussion of stress tensor invariants and represents a key transition from general transformation theory to applied cases in stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Direction Derivation Step (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Direction Derivation Step (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A garbled equation likely representing intermediate steps in deriving principal stress directions through differentiation of stress transformation equations. The context establishes the condition for extremal normal stresses yielding two perpendicular Hauptrichtungen via tangent function periodicity, fundamental to eigenvalue analysis of the 2D stress tensor in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
