<node id="Principal Stress Direction Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Direction Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A severely garbled mathematical expression that, based on context, represents the tangent formula for determining principal stress directions. The true formula, $\tan(2\varphi^*) = 2\tau_{xy}/(\sigma_x - \sigma_y)$, calculates the angles of the planes where normal stresses are extremal and shear stresses vanish.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 17 - Introduction to Principal Stresses (Hauptspannungen) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 17 - Introduction to Principal Stresses (Hauptspannungen) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 17 marks the beginning of section 11.2.2 on principal stresses in a German mechanics textbook. The page introduces how stresses depend on cutting angle φ and begins investigating extremal values, leading to the concept of principal directions where two perpendicular cutting directions yield maximum/minimum normal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
