  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic source citation attributing page content to Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), Technische Mechanik 2, Springer Vieweg. Located on page discussing stress tensor coordinate transformation, tensor invariants, hydrostatic stress states, and principal stress theory. Establishes academic provenance for the continuum mechanics content presented in sections 11.2.1 and 11.2.2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Extremum Condition for Principal Stresses (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Extremum Condition for Principal Stresses (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This stationarity condition (dσ/dφ = 0) identifies the cutting angles φ* at which normal stresses reach extremal values—the principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂. It connects to the eigenvalue problem of the stress tensor, where shear stresses simultaneously vanish, yielding the principal directions central to failure analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Equation (Ebener Spannungszustand) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Equation (Ebener Spannungszustand) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Transforms normal stress σ_ξ under coordinate rotation by angle φ in plane stress. Combines mean stress with deviatoric and shear contributions via trigonometric functions. Fundamental for deriving principal stresses and directions, forming the analytical basis for Mohr's circle and failure analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stationarity Condition for Principal Stress Directions (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stationarity Condition for Principal Stress Directions (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation sets the derivative of the transformed normal stress σ_ξ with respect to the rotation angle φ to zero, yielding the principal directions (Hauptrichtungen). Solving tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x - σ_y) gives the orientations where normal stresses reach extrema (σ_1, σ_2) and shear stress vanishes, forming the analytical core of Mohr's circle theory in plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Numerical Stress Component Ratio for Principal Direction Calculation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Numerical Stress Component Ratio for Principal Direction Calculation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation displaying fractional tuples (2/5, 3/5) likely representing normalized trigonometric or stress components used in computing principal stresses (Hauptspannungen) and principal directions (Hauptrichtungen). The notation appears non-standard, possibly denoting the ratio tan(2φ*) = 2τxy/(σx−σy) within Section 11.2.2 of the stress transformation framework, where shear stresses vanish at extremal normal stress values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Direction Equation (tan 2φ*) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Direction Equation (tan 2φ*) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation identifies the principal angle φ* at which normal stresses reach extremal values (principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂) and shear stresses vanish. It forms the mathematical foundation for coordinate transformation in plane stress analysis, connecting the general stress state (σ_x, σ_y, τ_xy) to the principal stress directions in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
