<node id="Principal Normal Stress Transformation Equation at Critical Angle (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stress Transformation Equation at Critical Angle (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This plane stress transformation equation evaluates the normal stress σ_ξ at the principal angle φ*, yielding the extremal normal stresses (principal stresses σ₁, σ₂). It combines the invariant hydrostatic stress with orientation-dependent deviatoric terms using double-angle trigonometry, forming the basis for principal stress and Mohr's circle analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Minor Principal Stress (σ₂) Transformation Equation at Principal Angle (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Minor Principal Stress (σ₂) Transformation Equation at Principal Angle (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluates the transformed normal stress σ_η at the principal angle φ* in plane stress analysis, yielding the minor principal stress σ₂. Combines the isotropic stress average with deviatoric orientation-dependent terms. Fundamental to determining principal directions where shear stress vanishes, essential for failure analysis and Mohr's circle construction in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Angle Tangent Condition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Angle Tangent Condition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An OCR-corrupted mathematical representation of the trigonometric condition $\tan(2\varphi^*) = \frac{2\tau_{xy}}{\sigma_x - \sigma_y}$ and the periodic property $\tan(2\varphi + \pi) = \tan(2\varphi)$. In the context of stress analysis, it defines the principal directions where shear stress becomes zero and normal stresses reach their maximum and minimum values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Numerical Stress Analysis Computation Step (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Numerical Stress Analysis Computation Step (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical evaluation step within principal stress (Hauptspannungen) analysis, computing a dimensional quantity by combining a geometric measurement (14 mm = 14×10⁻³ m) with unit conversion factors. The 1/2 coefficient indicates involvement in stress transformation formulas such as Mohr's circle relations, serving as an intermediate calculation for principal stresses σ₁, σ₂ or the principal angle φ* in a 2D stress state analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d</data>
