<node id="Plane Stress State Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress State Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates a differential square element subjected to a general plane stress state within an x-y coordinate system. It visually defines the normal stress components ($\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_y$) and shear stress components ($\\tau_{xy}$, $\\tau_{yx}$) acting on the element's faces. This representation provides the necessary geometric context for the calculations of principal stresses and maximum shear stresses discussed in section 11.2.2 of the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Principal Stress State and Orientation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Principal Stress State and Orientation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the principal stress state (Hauptspannungen) of a material element as described in Section 11.2.2. It shows a square element rotated by an angle $\\varphi^*$ into the principal coordinate system (axes 1 and 2). In this orientation, shear stresses are zero, and the normal stresses acting perpendicular to the faces are at their extreme values, labeled as the principal stresses $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2$. The visual clearly distinguishes between the principal axes (blue), the stress vectors (red), and the principal angle (green).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Extremalbedingung für Hauptschubspannungen (Extremization Condition for Principal Shear Stresses) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Extremalbedingung für Hauptschubspannungen (Extremization Condition for Principal Shear Stresses) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation differentiates the shear stress transformation formula and sets the derivative to zero to find the angle φ** at which shear stress reaches its extreme values (Hauptschubspannungen). The resulting condition tan 2φ** = -(σ_x - σ_y)/(2τ_xy) shows that maximum shear planes are at 45° to principal stress directions, a key result in plane stress analysis and Mohr's circle theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Verification of Trigonometric Normalization in Principal Stress Derivation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Verification of Trigonometric Normalization in Principal Stress Derivation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An identity verification within the principal stress (Hauptspannungen) derivation, demonstrating that the defined sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*) expressions satisfy the Pythagorean normalization sin²+cos²=1, ensuring consistency of the principal direction angle φ* where shear stress τξη vanishes and normal stresses σ₁, σ₂ attain extremal values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 20 - Principal Stresses Section (aside_text)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 20 - Principal Stresses Section (aside_text)</data>
  <data key="d1">aside_text</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '20' serving as a pagination marker within section 11.2.2 on Hauptspannungen (principal stresses). This page covers the determination of extremal shear stress values (Hauptschubspannungen) and their associated normal stresses at critical section angles (φ**), continuing the multi-page discussion on two-dimensional stress state analysis in a German mechanics textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
