<node id="Invalid/Corrupted Stress Transformation Expression (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Invalid/Corrupted Stress Transformation Expression (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically invalid expression in the context of Mohr's stress circle analysis. The left-hand side evaluates to 0, not 1.5. It does not correspond to any standard stress transformation formula and likely represents a transcription error or corrupted equation within the discussion of Mohr's circle construction for plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Spannungskreis Konstruktionsdiagramm (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Spannungskreis Konstruktionsdiagramm (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a step-by-step graphical representation for constructing Mohr's Circle in mechanics. It visually demonstrates how to plot the stress states of two perpendicular faces (points P and P') using normal stresses ($\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_y$) and shear stress ($\\tau_{xy}$) to find the circle's center ($\\sigma_M$) and radius ($r$). This construction is essential for determining principal stresses and maximum shear stresses in a material under load, as detailed in the accompanying section on stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Formula for Mohr's Circle Radius and Stress Invariants (image)">
  <data key="d0">Formula for Mohr's Circle Radius and Stress Invariants (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image presents the mathematical formula for calculating the squared radius of Mohr's Circle of stress. It explicitly identifies the stress invariants—the components of the stress tensor that do not change with rotation—by using braces to label parts of the equation as 'Invariante'. This provides the technical basis for constructing the circle once the initial stress components ($\\sigma_x, \\sigma_y, \\tau_{xy}$) are known, as outlined in section 11.2.3 of the source material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 23 - Mohr's Stress Circle Analysis (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 23 - Mohr's Stress Circle Analysis (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 23 from a German mechanics textbook, located within section 11.2.3 on Mohr's Stress Circle. This page covers stress state analysis using Mohr's circle, including stress transformation in rotated coordinate systems, principal stresses, and principal shear stresses. It builds on construction methods from page 21.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
