<node id="Trigonometric Identities">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Identities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trigonometric identities are mathematical equations and relations that are extensively utilized in the field of mechanics, particularly for deriving and simplifying stress transformation equations during coordinate rotations. By applying these mathematical relations, complex stress equations can be systematically reduced into standard, manageable formulas used to calculate shear and normal stresses. 

In the context of stress transformations, specific algebraic and angle-based trigonometric identities are substituted into derivations to simplify the equations. For instance, angle-shift identities such as cos(phi + pi/2) = -sin(phi) are used to simplify equations into the standard formula for normal stresses, such as Sigma Eta. Additionally, fundamental power-reduction and double-angle identities—namely cos²φ = (1+cos2φ)/2, sin²φ = (1-cos2φ)/2, and 2sinφcosφ = sin2φ—are applied to derive the standard double-angle forms of the stress transformation equations.

Furthermore, trigonometric identities involving sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*) are integral to the subsequent derivation steps needed to obtain explicit expressions for principal stresses, specifically σ_1 and σ_2. These identities allow for sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*) to be expressed as rational functions of the base stress components. By translating these trigonometric terms into rational functions, the identities provide a comprehensive mathematical bridge to accurately derive principal stresses directly from standard normal and shear stress components.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical formulas that the Tangent Equation bridges with continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd1f71b01c1aa5c67c36a16a597748fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point P">
  <data key="d0">Point P</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Point P refers to a specific spatial location within a deformable solid body, frequently utilized as a fundamental reference point in continuum mechanics and stress analysis. In its physical context, Point P is identified within the cross-section A of a solid body and represents a neighboring material point at coordinates (x, y) within a continuous body's reference, undeformed configuration. It commonly serves as the origin for local Cartesian coordinate systems and the undeformed material element, visually marked by an open circle at the rear-bottom-left corner of the volume element. Through this reference point, coordinate axes pass to derive moment equilibrium conditions, which establish the symmetry of shear stresses. The state of stress at Point P is fully characterized by evaluating the stress components across all possible cutting planes. Additionally, Point P acts as the starting corner point for original undeformed geometric elements, such as the square element PQRS and the line element PQ. It also functions as the initial point connected by a displacement vector.

Alternatively, within the context of graphical stress analysis, Point P designates a specific coordinate point plotted on Mohr's Circle. In this mathematical representation, Point P is located in the upper-right quadrant of the circle. It represents the stress state acting on the x-face of the physical material, possessing coordinates of (σ_x, τ_xy). On this graph, Point P is distinctly connected by a vertical line extending down to the normal stress value σ_x located on the horizontal axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13b9a52b59b74e8f8b6c3180f71ccba2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf382350c1415b7ddd1f82959df3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90cfec891927772de90cde9aa609de48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998988</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point P'">
  <data key="d0">Point P'</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A point located in the lower-left quadrant of Mohr's Circle with coordinates (σ_y, -τ_xy), connected by a vertical line to σ_y on the horizontal axis.&lt;SEP&gt;A point on Mohr's Circle representing the stress state on the perpendicular y-face.&lt;SEP&gt;The displaced position of Point P.&lt;SEP&gt;The deformed position of Point P after displacement.&lt;SEP&gt;The final, deformed position of the reference origin point P.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf382350c1415b7ddd1f82959df3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90cfec891927772de90cde9aa609de48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
