<node id="Radius">
  <data key="d0">Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The term Radius refers to distinct geometric concepts depending on the engineering and mathematical context, specifically relating to either Mohr's Circle or thin-walled pressure vessels. 

In the context of stress analysis and Mohr's Circle, the Radius (commonly denoted as $r$ or $R$) is defined as the distance or line segment extending from the center of the circle, designated as $\sigma_M$, to its circumference, such as point P. This geometric property is highly significant because it directly corresponds to the magnitude of the maximum shear stress ($\tau_{max}$). The radius of Mohr's stress circle is mathematically calculated from the state's stress components, which can yield specific numerical values in practical applications, such as approximately 46.1 MPa.

Alternatively, in the context of structural mechanics, the Radius (denoted as $r$) refers to the physical geometric dimension of a thin-walled pressure vessel. This applies to both cylindrical and spherical pressure vessels. In these cases, the radius represents the physical distance from the central axis of the vessel to the edge of its spherical shell or cylindrical wall, serving as a fundamental parameter for the physical structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35913765c2d7101f6d284ecd039952ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
