<node id="Tensor Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical framework applied to mechanical stress states, enabling the complete characterization of internal force distributions in deformable bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical framework for analyzing second-rank tensors in 2D continuum mechanics</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-rank Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Second-rank Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Tensor 2. Stufe, a mathematical object representing physical quantities like stress that transform according to specific tensorial rules and are rotation-invariant.&lt;SEP&gt;Type of tensor that stress represents, transforming according to specific rules under coordinate rotation while representing the same physical stress state&lt;SEP&gt;Type of mathematical object (Tensor 2. Stufe) defined by the transformation behavior exhibited by stress components under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Transformation Rule">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Transformation Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">General tensor transformation rule σ'_ij = a_ik · a_jl · σ_kl, where a_ij are direction cosines</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direction Cosines">
  <data key="d0">Direction Cosines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coefficients a_ij used in the general tensor transformation rule</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrostatic stress, also commonly referred to as mean stress, is a fundamental concept in continuum mechanics representing the arithmetic mean of normal stresses. In a two-dimensional stress state, it is mathematically defined as half the sum of the normal stresses, calculated as (sigma_x + sigma_y) / 2. In a three-dimensional or triaxial stress state, it is defined as the arithmetic mean of the three principal stresses, (sigma_1 + sigma_2 + sigma_3) / 3, which equates to one-third of the trace of the stress tensor (1/3 Tr(stress tensor)). Under uniform triaxial conditions where the principal stresses are equal (sigma_1 = sigma_2 = sigma_3 = sigma), hydrostatic stress defines a uniform, triaxial normal stress state that acts equally in all spatial directions.

A defining characteristic of hydrostatic stress is its invariance under coordinate rotation. In specific two-dimensional scenarios where the normal stresses are equal (sigma_x = sigma_y) and the shear stress is zero (tau_xy = 0), the stress equation reduces so that the normal stress (sigma_eta) is equivalent to both sigma_x and sigma_y. This confirms its complete independence from rotational orientation. Graphically, on a Mohr's Circle representation, the hydrostatic stress corresponds to the center of the circle and represents the normal stress acting on the plane of maximum shear. Physically, this scalar stress component is strictly responsible for volumetric changes within a material. Because it acts uniformly, it does not generate shear stresses and does not cause plastic deformation.

In the broader context of continuum mechanics, hydrostatic stress plays a vital role in the derivation of equivalent stress, which is historically referred to in German as Vergleichsspannung. During this derivation process, the hydrostatic stress components are mathematically subtracted from the total stress state. This critical step is undertaken to isolate the deviatoric, or shape-altering, energy. By removing the hydrostatic stress—which only contributes to volume change—engineers and physicists can accurately analyze material yielding and structural distortion without the influence of volumetric expansion or compression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
