<node id="Provided Equation">
  <data key="d0">Provided Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A finite scalar product expressed in LaTeX format where each multiplicand takes the form of an additive inverse (1/2 - 1/2), causing the entire expression to evaluate exactly to zero by the zero-product property.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical expression consisting of a chain of equalities utilizing basic arithmetic operations on rational constants, identified as mathematically inconsistent and likely an artifact of severe Optical Character Recognition corruption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Optical Character Recognition">
  <data key="d0">Optical Character Recognition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Optical Character Recognition (OCR) is a digitization technique and text recognition technology utilized to convert images of text into machine-encoded and machine-readable text. While designed for accurate digitization, the provided descriptions specifically highlight the technology's severe limitations and parsing errors when processing complex mathematical formulations.

In the analyzed contexts, Optical Character Recognition was identified as the primary source of severe data corruption, ultimately rendering a mathematical equation nonsensical. This particular equation was originally a valid transformation equation for a 2D plane stress state. The failure of the OCR process stems from its inability to accurately interpret mathematical formatting, leading to the misinterpretation of fractions and subscripts. For example, the technology failed to correctly parse specific subscripts like sigma_1 and sigma_2, which directly resulted in corrupted constants within the final text. Furthermore, Optical Character Recognition demonstrated character misidentification by confusing the Greek sigma symbol for a bold theta.

Beyond simple misidentification, the errors produced by Optical Character Recognition are characterized by specific, recurring parsing artifacts and structural deformations. When attempting to digitize mathematical text or LaTeX equations, the technology introduced recurring, nonsensical artifacts into the output, such as the term "nuptermation." Additionally, the parsing errors produced by this technology frequently exhibit repetitive redundancy, further degrading the integrity and accuracy of the resulting machine-encoded mathematical formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68f46a2f336e00af32ab51674b243da4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
