<node id="Force Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Force Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principle used to initiate the derivation of longitudinal stresses in a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel.&lt;SEP&gt;The balance of forces (Kräftegleichgewicht) which dictates that the circumferential stress is exactly twice the longitudinal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical principle involving force balance in the vertical direction, used to derive vessel stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boiler Formula">
  <data key="d0">Boiler Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Kesselformel, a formula derived from force equilibrium yielding the longitudinal stress σ_x = pr/(2t), fundamental in pressure vessel design.&lt;SEP&gt;A set of mathematical equations (Kesselformeln) used to relate internal pressure, vessel radius, and wall thickness to resulting stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
