<node id="Uniaxial Tension">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uniaxial Tension, also referred to in German as "einachsiger Zug" or designated as "Sonderfall 1" (Special Case 1), is a specific loading condition and fundamental stress state in continuum mechanics and the strength of materials. In this state, an applied normal tensile stress acts exclusively along a single axis. As a result, the principal stress in one direction is nonzero, while the principal stress in the perpendicular direction is zero.

Due to its fundamental nature, Uniaxial Tension serves as a crucial theoretical foundation and reference state used to demonstrate broader stress state concepts. It is frequently analyzed as a special case within the context of Mohr's Stress Circle. For example, in specific instructional contexts, this loading condition is introduced as a theoretical baseline before progressing to worked examples, such as on Page 24 of a referenced text. Within the framework of Mohr's Circle, Uniaxial Tension is particularly valuable for illustrating the depicted stress states and demonstrating its analytical relationship with pure shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sign Conventions">
  <data key="d0">Sign Conventions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rules used in stress analysis for determining the positive or negative direction of stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventions used for determining the positive or negative directions of stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;The rules establishing that positive stresses on positive faces point in positive coordinate directions.&lt;SEP&gt;Definitions and rules presented in the main text of the academic document that are authenticated by the source literature.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical conventions that define the positive and negative directions for stresses, summarized in the document's summary section.&lt;SEP&gt;Standardized rules used in plane stress analysis to determine the positive or negative direction of stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalspannungen">
  <data key="d0">Normalspannungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German language term for normal stresses used in the continuum mechanics content.&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stresses in the mechanics of materials. A key theoretical point noted in the surrounding material states that normal stresses generally do NOT vanish at sections of extremal shear stress ('In den Schnitten extremaler Schubspannungen verschwinden die Normalspannungen im allgemeinen nicht!').</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sonderfall 1">
  <data key="d0">Sonderfall 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A labeled special case in the Mohr's stress circle analysis framework corresponding specifically to the uniaxial tension stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Case 1: Einachsiger Zug (Uniaxial Tension), a special case in Mohr's stress circle analysis representing a uniaxial stress state where only σ_x (or σ_1) is non-zero, reducing the stress tensor to σ = [[σ_1, 0], [0, 0]]&lt;SEP&gt;The designation for the uniaxial tension stress state within the Mohr's Circle analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_0">
  <data key="d0">σ_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the magnitude of the applied tensile stress in the uniaxial tension stress state, determining the center position (σ_0/2, 0) and radius (R = σ_0/2) of the corresponding Mohr's circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Stress refers to the maximum and minimum normal stresses that occur at a specific point within a given stress state, representing a fundamental concept in stress analysis that can be traced through primary literature. In some contexts and historical literature, this concept is referred to as 'Hauptspannungen'. Principal stresses represent the extremal values of normal stress—typically denoted as σ₁, σ₂, and σ₃—which act on specific planes oriented at the principal angle. On these unique planes, the shear stress completely vanishes.

Mathematically, Principal Stresses are determined as the eigenvalues resulting from stress transformation analysis. These values can be evaluated through rigorous eigenvalue analysis or determined graphically via Mohr's circle construction. 

In practical engineering applications, principal stresses define critical loading parameters within engineering formulas. For example, in the specific context of uniaxial tension, the principal stresses are defined as σ₁ equal to σ₀ (representing the maximum principal stress) and σ₂ equal to zero. In this scenario, the standard x-y coordinate axes are perfectly aligned with the principal stress directions. Furthermore, principal stresses are essential for understanding material deformation, as they directly relate to the principal strains in accordance with the generalized Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999541</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Testing">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A material testing method that commonly encounters the uniaxial tension stress state, used to measure material properties under applied tensile loads.&lt;SEP&gt;An experimental testing method used for the determination of Young's modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999272</data>
