  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Directions">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Directions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Stress Directions, also referred to by the German term Hauptrichtungen, are the specific orientations in a stressed material where normal stresses reach their extremal maximum and minimum values. In these directions, the stress state becomes purely normal, meaning there is absolutely zero shear component. Mathematically, these two perpendicular directions are obtained by setting the shear stress τ_ξη to zero in the shear stress transformation equation. Due to the π-periodicity of the tangent function, this yields two orthogonal angles defined by the equation tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x - σ_y).

In the broader context of solid mechanics and stress analysis, Principal Stress Directions are frequently discussed alongside Mohr's circle construction. They serve as a fundamental basis for executing subsequent stress transformation calculations at arbitrary cutting angles. Furthermore, these orientations are critically linked to material failure mechanisms; for example, the maximum shear stress within the material occurs at an angle of 45 degrees relative to the principal directions. Consequently, Principal Stress Directions act as the primary reference orientations relative to which characteristical slip or failure planes are situated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68f46a2f336e00af32ab51674b243da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8f3f8c72a4f9a9fdeabe99c8c8ba4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation Equations for Plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Transformation Equations for Plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The key equations transforming stresses σ_ξ, σ_η, and τ_ξη as functions of the section angle φ, using double-angle trigonometric identities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
