  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A special case in Mohr's stress circle analysis representing hydrostatic stress, where σ_1 equals σ_2&lt;SEP&gt;A special case of Mohr's stress circle representing hydrostatic stress state (hydrostatischer Spannungszustand), covered on page 24 of Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific designation given to the hydrostatic stress state within the Mohr's Circle analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_2">
  <data key="d0">σ_2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second principal stress derived in the document.&lt;SEP&gt;The minimum principal stress (Minor principal stress) obtained from the eigenvalue decomposition of the stress tensor</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_1">
  <data key="d0">σ_1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The first principal stress derived in the document, defined such that σ_1 is greater than σ_2.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum principal stress in the stress tensor, which in uniaxial tension is the only non-zero principal stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_max">
  <data key="d0">τ_max</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The symbol representing maximum shear stresses acting tangentially along the faces of the material element, arranged in opposing pairs on adjacent faces to satisfy static equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stress, equal to σ_1/2 in the uniaxial tension case, occurring at 45° to the principal directions&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stress value indicated at the highest point of the circle's circumference, corresponding to the radius of the circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_0">
  <data key="d0">τ_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress parameter appearing in the pure shear stress state (Sonderfall 2), where the stress tensor is σ = [[0, τ_0], [τ_0, 0]]</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ-axis">
  <data key="d0">τ-axis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress axis on the Mohr's circle diagram, which the Mohr's circle for uniaxial tension is tangent to at the origin</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Angle">
  <data key="d0">Principal Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle φ* that defines the principal stress directions ('Hauptrichtungen'), found using the trigonometric relation tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x - σ_y).&lt;SEP&gt;The angle φ* determined by the tangent equation tan(2φ*) = 2τxy/(σx - σy), defining the orientation of principal directions&lt;SEP&gt;The angle 2φ* determined via a specific mathematical relation involving stress components, used in stress transformation equations.&lt;SEP&gt;An angle, conventionally denoted as $\varphi^*$ or $\varphi^{**}$, at which normal or shear stresses are maximized, found by setting the derivative to zero.&lt;SEP&gt;The physical angle (φ*) between the x-axis and the direction of maximum/minimum principal stress, identified through Mohr's circle analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle of the principal axes, denoted as φ*, measured in radians [rad].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Planes">
  <data key="d0">Principal Planes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The orthogonal planes separated by 90 degrees on which shear stress becomes zero and normal stresses reach their extremal values.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific planes corresponding to principal directions where shear stress is zero (shear-free)&lt;SEP&gt;Planes that experience only normal stresses without shear, established when the shear stress τ_ξη vanishes.&lt;SEP&gt;Planes where shear stress vanishes, contrasting with planes of extremal shear stress where normal stresses generally do not vanish.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific planes in a stress state where the shear stress is zero and the extreme normal stresses (principal stresses) act</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Stress Transformation">
  <data key="d0">2D Stress Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical procedure in continuum mechanics used to determine how stress components change with coordinate rotation, specifically used to find the principal angle.&lt;SEP&gt;A key analytical process in continuum mechanics where principal stresses, conventionally denoted as sigma_1 and sigma_2, are calculated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Plane Stress Conditions, also widely referred to as ebener Spannungszustand or ESZ, describe a two-dimensional state of stress where the normal stress in one dimension is approximately or exactly zero. This condition is typically applied to thin structures, meaning the stress components acting in one direction are negligible. It represents the state of an infinitesimal element extracted from a two-dimensional body, such as a plate or disc.

Under Plane Stress Conditions, a structural element can be conceptualized as a triangular or infinitesimal component extracted from a larger, often biaxially loaded, plate. This specific state forms the necessary geometric and physical basis for analyzing complex mechanical behaviors. It is the fundamental framework under which Hooke's law is generalized for two-dimensional applications. Furthermore, it dictates the conditions under which the shear stress-shear strain constitutive relation is deemed valid, particularly for isotropic materials.

In the broader context of solid mechanics, Plane Stress Conditions are essential for evaluating material behavior and structural integrity. They are frequently utilized in the analysis of stress tensor transformations and serve as the underlying premise for constructing Mohr's stress circle. Additionally, this two-dimensional stress state is highly relevant when analyzing lateral contraction phenomena, ensuring accurate predictions of deformation and structural response in homogeneous, isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-235ab6bb09c88f34c57e39b028978709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999227</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Cartesian Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">2D Cartesian Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system with a horizontal x-axis pointing right and a vertical y-axis pointing upward, used as the reference frame for the stress diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The spatial reference system in which the Mohr's Circle diagram is set, consisting of a horizontal and vertical axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999391</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equations Of Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equations Of Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Governing partial differential equations derived by summing forces in x and y directions to zero for static equilibrium: ∂σ_x/∂x + ∂τ_yx/∂y + f_x = 0 and ∂τ_xy/∂x + ∂σ_y/∂y + f_y = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dy">
  <data key="d0">Dy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The infinitesimal height of the rectangular mechanical element, measured in the vertical y-direction of the 2D Cartesian coordinate system.&lt;SEP&gt;The original infinitesimal length of the line segment in the undeformed configuration, aligned along the y-axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sign Convention">
  <data key="d0">Sign Convention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A convention defining tensile normal stresses and positive shear stresses as those pointing in positive coordinate directions on positive faces of the volume element&lt;SEP&gt;A convention (Vorzeichenkonvention) where positive normal stresses acting on positive cut faces in the positive coordinate direction indicate tensile loading, while negative values indicate compressive loading.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific rule applied in the diagram where positive stresses act in positive coordinate directions on positive faces (faces whose outward normals point in the positive x or y directions).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma_0">
  <data key="d0">Sigma_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greek letter sigma with a subscript zero, representing the magnitude of the applied tensile normal stress in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses acting horizontally outward on the differential material element, represented by prominent red arrows originating from the left and right vertical faces.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress pulling the material apart on the vertical axis, labeled as '$\\sigma_y = \\sigma$'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rectangle Cross-Section">
  <data key="d0">Rectangle Cross-Section</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A large rectangle representing a two-dimensional cross-section of a solid body or block, serving as the central element of the schematic diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Height Arrow h">
  <data key="d0">Height Arrow h</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A double-headed vertical arrow labeled 'h' indicating the total height of the object, located to the right of the block.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Load">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A uniformly distributed compressive load or pressure applied to the top surface of the block, indicated by several downward-pointing arrows of equal length along the top edge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Downward-Pointing Arrows">
  <data key="d0">Downward-Pointing Arrows</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Several arrows of equal length lining the top edge of the rectangle, indicating a uniformly distributed compressive load applied to the surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diagonal Hatching Lines">
  <data key="d0">Diagonal Hatching Lines</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Lines featured on the bottom edge of the rectangle, serving as standard engineering notation for a fixed, rigid support or boundary condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutting Direction">
  <data key="d0">Cutting Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The orientation of the cutting plane through a point, referred to as Schnittrichtung in German, on which the stress state depends.&lt;SEP&gt;Represented by the angle φ, it is the direction on which normal stresses and shear stress depend.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-Direction Equilibrium Equation">
  <data key="d0">X-Direction Equilibrium Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation representing the force balance (ΣFx = 0) in x-direction for an infinitesimal rectangular element in plane stress state, derived from Newton's first law applied to a differential element of dimensions dx × dy with thickness t</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress τ_yx">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress τ_yx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress acting on the horizontal face of the triangular element, which is resolved into the ξ-direction.&lt;SEP&gt;Shear stress component on the y-face acting in x-direction, with spatial gradient ∂σ_yx/∂y&lt;SEP&gt;A surface force arising from shear stresses acting on element boundaries in the y-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Volume Force Density f_x">
  <data key="d0">Volume Force Density f_x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Body force density per unit volume in x-direction, such as weight, acting on the element volume dx·dy·t</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Equilibrium Equations">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Equilibrium Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of two differential equilibrium equations in 2D continuum mechanics that govern the stress field&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical equations representing equilibrium requirements where the net forces must vanish (e.g., ∂σx/∂x + ∂τyx/∂y + fx = 0).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory of Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Theory of Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Framework within continuum mechanics for studying stress and strain in elastic bodies, including equilibrium conditions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order approximation technique used to represent stress field variation across an infinitesimal element&lt;SEP&gt;A first-order expansion of the displacement field u(x) expressing the deformed length as dx + (∂u/∂x)dx, with higher-order terms neglected under the small-deformation assumption</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0deb8f7c8c663233e195e68e3b5afeea</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999034</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Value Problems">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Value Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problems in solid mechanics solved using equilibrium equations and constitutive relations</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Relations">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Relations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress-strain laws that, together with equilibrium equations, form the basis for solving boundary value problems in solid mechanics&lt;SEP&gt;A system of three coupled equations representing the relationship between principal stresses and principal strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equilibrium Equation">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equilibrium Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Simplified governing equation ∂σ_x/∂x + ∂τ_yx/∂y + f_x = 0 obtained by algebraically simplifying the x-direction force balance</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Rectangular Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Rectangular Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Differential element of dimensions dx × dy with thickness t extracted from a two-dimensional plate or disk, used as the basis for deriving the x-direction equilibrium equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Gradients Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Gradients Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Spatial derivatives ∂σ_x/∂x and ∂σ_yx/∂y describing how stress components vary across the infinitesimal element, enabling Taylor expansion approximation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
