<node id="Difference Quotient">
  <data key="d0">Difference Quotient</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical technique used in the equation where fractions such as 0.02/0.02 represent discretized spatial gradients of normal and shear stresses (Δσ/Δx and Δτ/Δy).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Partial Derivatives">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Partial Derivatives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Theoretical derivatives that are discretized into difference quotients to solve practical engineering problems involving differential equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mean Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Mean Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress component identified by the label σ_M = σ_0 / 2 in the stress diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The mean value of normal stress, denoted as sigma subscript M, which equals zero for a pure shear stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Often denoted as $\sigma_m$ or $\sigma_h$, it is the arithmetic mean of the three principal stresses in a three-dimensional continuum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
