<node id="Stationarity Condition For Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Stationarity Condition For Normal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical condition dσ_ξ/dφ = 0 bzw. dσ_η/dφ = 0 representing the extremum condition for normal stresses under coordinate rotation in plane stress analysis, used to determine principal stress directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_ξ">
  <data key="d0">σ_ξ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A normal stress component derived using the normal stress formula involving σ_x, σ_y, and τ_xy under a coordinate system rotation by angle φ.&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stress in the rotated ξ-direction of the (ξ,η)-coordinate system, obtained by rotating the original (x,y)-axes by angle φ.&lt;SEP&gt;The normal stress acting on a plane oriented at the principal angle φ* relative to the original x-axis coordinate system.&lt;SEP&gt;The transformed normal stress whose derivative with respect to φ yields the condition for extremal values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b5475536a7fccb42b9f6f9e39d32b77</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="(ξ,η)-Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">(ξ,η)-Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rotated coordinate system obtained by rotating the original (x,y)-axes by angle φ in plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="(x,y)-Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">(x,y)-Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Original coordinate system in plane stress analysis from which the rotated (ξ,η)-coordinate system is derived.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stresses σ₁ And σ₂">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stresses σ₁ And σ₂</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The extremal normal stress values σ₁ &gt; σ₂ that are the eigenvalues of the Cauchy stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Invariant Of Stress Tensor (I₁)">
  <data key="d0">First Invariant Of Stress Tensor (I₁)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The quantity σ_x + σ_y = σ_ξ + σ_η, which remains constant under coordinate rotation and equals the trace of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Failure Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Failure Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure criterion used in structural design whose theoretical basis involves principal stresses derived from this analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Failure Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Failure Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure criterion used in structural design whose theoretical basis involves principal stresses derived from this analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Two-dimensional stress state analysis in which stresses act only in a single plane, the context for the stationarity condition.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of stress analysis focusing on stresses acting within a single plane, to which the shear stress extremization equation applies.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive stress analysis method encompassing stress tensor representation, sign conventions, transformation relationships, principal stresses and directions, maximum shear stress, Mohr's stress circle, and equilibrium conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eigenvalue Problem Of Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Eigenvalue Problem Of Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical problem connected to the extremum condition for normal stresses, where principal stresses are eigenvalues and principal directions are eigenvectors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beziehungsweise (bzw.)">
  <data key="d0">Beziehungsweise (bzw.)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A German word meaning "or equivalently", used in the equation to indicate that setting the derivative of σ_ξ or σ_η to zero yields equivalent conditions due to the first invariant of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Dimensional Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Two-Dimensional Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of stress analyzed in a 2D plane, represented by stress components acting on an infinitesimal element.&lt;SEP&gt;A plane stress state where stresses act only in two dimensions, for which the principal stress directions are determined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation ∑F_y = 0">
  <data key="d0">Equation ∑F_y = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the fundamental equilibrium condition in the y-direction (vertical force balance), ensuring no translational acceleration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Newtonian Static Equilibrium Equations">
  <data key="d0">Newtonian Static Equilibrium Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The three equations required for a rigid body or infinitesimal element to be in mechanical equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-walled Spherical Vessel">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Spherical Vessel</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A vessel structure analyzed for stress under internal pressure.</data>
