<node id="Von Mises Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure analysis criterion dependent on the full stress state of a component.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion that uses principal stresses to assess material yielding; central to structural design.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure theory where stress transformation is applied for predicting yielding behavior&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion in continuum mechanics used for structural design, for which the principal stress analysis is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion used in strength assessment that relies on principal stresses to determine critical loading directions&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion evaluated using the principal direction formula.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Tresca Criterion, also known as the shear stress hypothesis or Schubspannungshypothese, is a fundamental material failure theory and yield criterion utilized in continuum mechanics. It serves as a critical tool in structural design, failure analysis, and overall strength assessment, frequently employed as an alternative to the von Mises yield criterion. 

At its core, the Tresca Criterion posits that the yielding behavior of a material is primarily characterized by the maximum shear stress it experiences. To evaluate this, the criterion is highly dependent on the full stress state of a given component. Its foundational methodology involves applying stress transformation to isolate and analyze principal stresses. By evaluating these stresses and utilizing the principal direction formula, engineers can accurately predict yielding behavior and determine the critical loading directions that may lead to material failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
