</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Second Law">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Second Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A law establishing the symmetry of the stress tensor, which reduces the nine components of the stress matrix to six independent ones.&lt;SEP&gt;The physical principle of angular momentum conservation which states that the stress tensor is symmetric.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental principle dictating the symmetry of the stress tensor, meaning the shear stress components are equal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium Condition">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition (τ_xy = τ_yx, etc.) that reduces the nine tensor components to six independent ones via the symmetry of the stress tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;An algebraic equation representing the moment equilibrium about an axis parallel to the x-axis passing through the center of an infinitesimal cubic element.&lt;SEP&gt;A rotational equilibrium condition about coordinate axes through a body's centroid that establishes the symmetry property of the stress tensor</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf36ff85412f3dea295a176bbfb87119</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Element Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Finite Element Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Finite Element Analysis (FEA) is a practical computational and numerical method widely utilized in engineering, solid mechanics, structural mechanics, and mathematical physics to solve complex problems. Abbreviated as FEA, this technique is extensively applied in structural engineering within mechanical and civil disciplines to analyze and evaluate load-bearing and mechanical components, including thin-walled structures. Its primary applications include predicting deformation patterns, calculating local strains, determining material failure points, and predicting surface shape changes under load. Furthermore, it is used to compute structural deformations, evaluate multi-axial stress states, and calculate equivalent stresses, strains, and safety factors under complex loading conditions.

The theoretical framework of Finite Element Analysis relies on essential fundamental kinematic relations, equilibrium equations, and strain-displacement equations. It utilizes a three-dimensional displacement field and incorporates mathematical formulations such as Taylor expansions, frequently operating under the assumption of small deformations. To accurately analyze stress and strain distributions, FEA relies on the symmetric stress tensor, applies simplified single-index notation for normal stresses, requires stress transformation, and utilizes essential normal strain measures like ε_x. The method also decouples hydrostatic stresses from deviatoric stresses to effectively evaluate complex mechanical scenarios.

Material behavior in Finite Element Analysis is governed by constitutive equations and derived strain quantities, which utilize specific material properties as input parameters. It fundamentally applies constitutive relationships derived from linear elasticity theory, including the shear stress-shear strain relationship, the full 6x6 stiffness/compliance matrix assembled from shear constitutive relations, and the fundamental relationships between elastic moduli and elastic constants. Additionally, FEA serves as an important numerical simulation technique and application domain for plasticity theory. Within this context, it heavily utilizes the von Mises yield criterion to predict the onset of plastic deformation, thereby ensuring the accurate evaluation and safe design of mechanical components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0deb8f7c8c663233e195e68e3b5afeea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
