<node id="First Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">First Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as σ1 = τ0, the tensile principal stress located to the right of the origin on the horizontal σ-axis of Mohr's circle in the pure shear stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Second Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as σ2 = -τ0, the compressive principal stress located to the left of the origin on the horizontal σ-axis of Mohr's circle in the pure shear stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Origin (0, 0)">
  <data key="d0">Origin (0, 0)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The center point of the Cartesian coordinate system where the center of Mohr's circle is exactly located in a pure shear stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hauptrichtungen">
  <data key="d0">Hauptrichtungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal directions, the angles φ* at which normal stresses attain their extremal values and shear stresses vanish&lt;SEP&gt;Principal stress directions, which are the two perpendicular section directions where extremal normal stresses occur.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal directions defined by the principal angle in a plane stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Components">
  <data key="d0">Stress Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical quantities representing internal forces within continuum mechanics or strength of materials, often represented using double-index notation.&lt;SEP&gt;The individual scalar values comprising the stress tensor, whose number of independent variables is reduced to six due to the symmetry of the tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantities representing the internal forces per unit area within a material, derived in section 11.2.1 using rotated coordinate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Components σx, σy, and τxy used in stress transformation equations to compute principal stresses and directions&lt;SEP&gt;The individual elements of the stress tensor, specifically σx, σy, and τxy, defining internal forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49d921c1af07b0743c98652a77081e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pythagorean Identity">
  <data key="d0">Pythagorean Identity</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, utilized to express sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*) as rational functions of stress components&lt;SEP&gt;The fundamental identity sin²(2φ*) + cos²(2φ*) = 1 that must hold for the trigonometric expressions defining sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*) to be self-consistent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pythagorean Triple">
  <data key="d0">Pythagorean Triple</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical triple such as (3/5, 4/5) that satisfies the unit-norm condition, unlike (2/5, 3/5) which yields 13/25 ≠ 1</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section 11.2.2">
  <data key="d0">Section 11.2.2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A section titled Hauptspannungen whose mathematical framework is concerned with determining the extremal values of normal stresses and the associated angles&lt;SEP&gt;A section in the technical mechanics lecture document covering the derivation of principal stresses (Hauptspannungen), with content sourced from the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2'.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific section within the mechanics/engineering document discussing principal stresses and related calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A section of an academic document whose theoretical framework, derivations, and notational conventions originate from Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c</data>
