</node>
<node id="Unit Vector">
  <data key="d0">Unit Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector with a norm of one, which the numerical tuples in the equation resemble components of</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Stress Theorem">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Stress Theorem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental theorem expressed as t = σ·n, stating that the traction vector t depends on the stress tensor σ and the unit normal vector n to the cutting surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traction Vector">
  <data key="d0">Traction Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector t representing stress acting on a cutting surface, dependent on the stress tensor and the unit normal vector according to Cauchy's stress theorem.&lt;SEP&gt;The force per unit area acting on a specific cutting plane, derived from the stress tensor and unit normal vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Normal Vector">
  <data key="d0">Unit Normal Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector n perpendicular to the cutting surface, used in Cauchy's stress theorem and in the decomposition of stress into normal and shear components.&lt;SEP&gt;A vector perpendicular to a cutting plane that is mapped by the stress tensor to find the traction vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Average Stress">
  <data key="d0">Average Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mean stress over a finite area element, referred to as mittlere Spannung in German, which transitions to pointwise stress through the limit process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuum Hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">Continuum Hypothesis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The foundational assumption that ΔF/ΔA approaches a finite limit as ΔA approaches zero, essential for the mathematical well-definedness of stress at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Force">
  <data key="d0">Differential Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The infinitesimal force ΔF acting on an area element, used in the limit definition of stress at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Area Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Area Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The differential area ΔA on which the differential force acts, approaching zero in the limit definition of stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutting Surface">
  <data key="d0">Cutting Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The imaginary surface through a point in a body along which internal forces are analyzed, characterized by its unit normal vector n.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Location">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Location</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The position in space, referred to as Ort in German, on which the stress state at a point depends.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Loads">
  <data key="d0">External Loads</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Forces and surface loads, represented by red arrows, that act upon the outer boundary of a solid body.&lt;SEP&gt;Forces denoted as F_i acting on a deformable body, contributing to the internal force distribution characterized by the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2616f80e6ffe22a7bb18ebfbbc1b015a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Loads">
  <data key="d0">Surface Loads</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Forces denoted as p acting on the surface of a deformable body, contributing to the internal stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sonderfall 2: Reiner Schubspannungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Sonderfall 2: Reiner Schubspannungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific case demonstrating that a state of pure shear is equivalent to equal tensile and compressive principal stresses acting on planes inclined at 45 degrees to the original shear planes.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Case 2: Pure Shear Stress State, the second special case in a series of stress states analyzed using Mohr's circle, introduced on page 24.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05f0cba91a41b2018384acbb7174e398</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$\sigma_1 = \tau_0$">
  <data key="d0">$\sigma_1 = \tau_0$</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A label indicating a tensile principal stress equal to the initial shear stress magnitude.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$\sigma_2 = -\tau_0$">
  <data key="d0">$\sigma_2 = -\tau_0$</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A label indicating a compressive principal stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="45 Degrees">
  <data key="d0">45 Degrees</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle of rotation of the material element from the horizontal reference axis in the analyzed diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The explicit value of angle alpha, marking the orientation of the deformed edges relative to the original horizontal axis.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific angular measurement at which slip planes or failure planes are characteristically oriented relative to the principal stress directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68f46a2f336e00af32ab51674b243da4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Reference Axis">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Reference Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The base line from which the material element is rotated by an angle of 45 degrees.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of principal stress indicated by the label $\sigma_1 = \tau_0$, acting on the upper-right face of the material element.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state labeled as 'σy = σ' in the diagram, corresponding to the intersection of the circle to the right of the origin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of principal stress indicated by the label $\sigma_2 = -\tau_0$, acting on the lower-right face of the material element.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state labeled '-σ = σx' in the diagram, representing a compressive stress of equal magnitude on the left of the origin.&lt;SEP&gt;A negative principal stress, denoted as -sigma, acting in one direction of the biaxial stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Initial Shear Stress">
  <data key="d0">Initial Shear Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The magnitude of shear stress ($\tau_0$) to which the tensile principal stress is equal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Direction Equation">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Direction Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The intended mathematical formula `tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x − σ_y)` derived from a corrupted LaTeX equation, used to determine principal stress directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="φ*">
  <data key="d0">φ*</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The critical or principal angle at which the normal stress reaches its maximum or minimum value, and at which the shear stress τ_ξη vanishes identically.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal angle identifying the principal directions.&lt;SEP&gt;A critical section orientation relevant to the relationship between normal stresses and shear stresses discussed in the document.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle defining the principal directions in the stress transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal stress orientation angle from which the orientation of maximum shear stress (φ**) is offset by π/4 radians.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical analysis method that uses the canonical form of stress transformation equations involving double-angle trigonometric expressions for stress visualization and computation.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical method for stress state characterization to which the principal stress equation is central.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical method discussed in the technical mechanics document, related to the evaluation of stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific application domain within solid mechanics where the principal stress equation is applied.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure in stress analysis where the maximum shear stress is determined by setting the derivative of shear stress with respect to the rotation angle to zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corrupted Latex Equation">
  <data key="d0">Corrupted Latex Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation presented in LaTeX format that appears corrupted or improperly rendered, likely due to a parsing error indicated by an '[object Object]' artifact.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematically nonsensical LaTeX string resulting from severe OCR corruption of thin-walled cylinder stress formulas, equating a bracketed subscripted bold vector theta to its double-primed counterpart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Original Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The primary Cartesian coordinate system overlaid on the infinitesimal element, consisting of the horizontal 'x' axis and vertical 'y' axis.&lt;SEP&gt;The reference frame in which the original normal stress components and shear stress component are defined.&lt;SEP&gt;The initial reference frame, denoted as (x, y), relative to which normal and shear stresses are originally defined.&lt;SEP&gt;The reference system in which the normal stress components σx, σy and shear stress component τxy are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sin(2φ*)">
  <data key="d0">Sin(2φ*)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
