  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Condition For Shear Forces">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Condition For Shear Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the equilibrium condition for shear forces acting on an infinitesimal triangular element cut from a two-dimensional stress element (Scheibe), examining force equilibrium in the η-direction tangential to the inclined cut surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_{xy}">
  <data key="d0">τ_{xy}</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear stress component equal to τ_{yx} by symmetry of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_{yx}">
  <data key="d0">τ_{yx}</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear stress component equal to τ_{xy} by symmetry of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="dA">
  <data key="d0">dA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Area of the inclined face; projected areas are dA·cosφ (x-face) and dA·sinφ (y-face).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensoren 2. Stufe">
  <data key="d0">Tensoren 2. Stufe</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">German term for second-order tensors, quantities whose components transform according to a specific rotational prescription, confirming the rotational invariance of the physical stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rotational Invariance">
  <data key="d0">Rotational Invariance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A property confirming that the physical meaning of the stress state remains unchanged regardless of coordinate system orientation.&lt;SEP&gt;Property denoted as 'rotationsunabhängig' in the text, describing the stress tensor's independence from rotation, connecting physical stress to tensor mathematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Physical Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The actual state of stress in a material, whose physical meaning remains unchanged regardless of coordinate system orientation.&lt;SEP&gt;The underlying state of stress that remains invariant (rotationsunabhängig) regardless of changes in the observer's coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coordinate System Orientation">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate System Orientation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial alignment of the coordinate axes (such as x,y or ξ,η), relative to which stress components are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Stress Equation">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Stress Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the force equilibrium in the longitudinal (axial) direction of a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel under internal pressure, expressed as σ_x · (π(r+t)² - πr²) = p · πr².</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inner Radius">
  <data key="d0">Inner Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius r of the internal circular cross-section of the cylindrical pressure vessel, a key geometric parameter in stress calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Wall Approximation">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Wall Approximation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A simplification applied when wall thickness is much less than inner radius (t ≪ r), where the term πt² becomes negligibly small compared to 2πrt, reducing the equilibrium equation to σ_x · 2πrt = p · πr².</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Annular Cross-Sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Annular Cross-Sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area of the vessel wall, computed as π(r+t)² - πr², which when multiplied by longitudinal stress gives the resisting force.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Circular Cross-Section">
  <data key="d0">Internal Circular Cross-Section</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal area πr² of the vessel over which the internal pressure acts to generate the driving force.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driving Force">
  <data key="d0">Driving Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The force applied by the internal pressure acting over the internal circular cross-section, represented on the right-hand side of the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 5">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 5</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A page number element located at the bottom-left of page 5 (page_idx: 4) in a German mechanical engineering textbook, serving as a navigation reference within the pagination system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e</data>
