  <data key="d0">Stress Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of mechanics whose concepts are treated in detail in the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Quader">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Quader</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A 2D cross-section of an infinitesimal cuboid material element centered at Point M, used to analyze the stress state at that point within a Cartesian coordinate system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_z">
  <data key="d0">σ_z</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress component acting vertically on the z-faces (top and bottom) of the infinitesimal quader, pointing upward on the positive z-face and downward on the negative z-face</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Positive Sign Convention">
  <data key="d0">Positive Sign Convention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A standard convention where positive stresses on positive faces point in positive coordinate directions, used to determine the direction of stress components</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Sigma Eta">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Sigma Eta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The transformed normal stress component acting in the eta-direction, perpendicular to xi, calculated for a plane rotated by an angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometric Shift Identities">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Shift Identities</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical identities used in the derivation of the equation, evaluating cos(φ + π/2) = -sin(φ) and sin(φ + π/2) = cos(φ).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian X Y Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian X Y Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original coordinate system in which the initial normal and shear stress components (sigma x, sigma y, tau xy) are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Xi Eta Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Xi Eta Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The rotated coordinate system in which the transformed normal stress components (sigma xi, sigma eta) are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation $2 \cdot \sigma _ { \varphi } \cdot \Delta l \cdot t = p \cdot \Delta l \cdot 2 r$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $2 \cdot \sigma _ { \varphi } \cdot \Delta l \cdot t = p \cdot \Delta l \cdot 2 r$</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the static force equilibrium condition for a thin-walled spherical pressure vessel (Kugelförmiger Kessel) under internal pressure, balancing pressure-induced forces with membrane stresses in the vessel wall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Wall Assumption">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Wall Assumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assumption that wall thickness $t$ is much smaller than radius $r$ ($t \ll r$), allowing stress to be treated as uniformly distributed and radial stresses to be neglected.&lt;SEP&gt;The assumption that wall thickness is much less than the radius (t ≪ r), allowing radial stresses to be neglected.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membrane Theory">
  <data key="d0">Membrane Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theory concerning shell structures under axisymmetric loading, exemplified by the equilibrium condition of the thin-walled spherical pressure vessel.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arc Length Element">
  <data key="d0">Arc Length Element</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as Δl, it is an infinitesimal arc length element used in analyzing the differential section of the spherical shell.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
