<node id="Tresca Yield Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Yield Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum shear stress criterion used for failure analysis, based on the maximum shear stress a material can withstand before yielding&lt;SEP&gt;A yield criterion, also known as Schubspannungshypothese, which postulates that plastic deformation begins when the maximum shear stress reaches a critical value.&lt;SEP&gt;An alternative name for the shear stress hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese), used in the theory of plasticity and failure analysis for ductile materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A yield hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese) where angle equalities can represent the orientation of slip planes or failure planes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68f46a2f336e00af32ab51674b243da4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ductile Materials">
  <data key="d0">Ductile Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Materials for which the Tresca yield criterion is particularly relevant in failure analysis&lt;SEP&gt;A class of materials prone to plastic yielding (plastisches Fließen), evaluated using shear stress and von Mises hypotheses.&lt;SEP&gt;Materials, such as ductile metals, for which the shear stress hypothesis is particularly applicable because maximum shear stress drives dislocation motion.&lt;SEP&gt;Materials for which the shear stress hypothesis and failure analysis are specifically applied, where yielding or failure initiates when shear stress reaches a critical threshold.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of materials whose mechanical failure and yielding can be predicted using the defined strain energy equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A class of materials that do not undergo plastic yielding when subjected to hydrostatic stress because it produces no shear stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of materials for which the Von Mises equivalent stress equation establishes the yield criterion, defining the mathematical onset of plastic deformation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-343794ade2b1d656c68e2c17454713f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
