<node id="Festigkeitshypothesen">
  <data key="d0">Festigkeitshypothesen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Festigkeitshypothesen, the German term for strength hypotheses, failure hypotheses, or failure criteria, represents a fundamental concept within technical mechanics and engineering mechanics. Referring to specific failure theories, Festigkeitshypothesen serves as a critical bridge between theoretical stress analysis and practical engineering design.

These hypotheses are primarily utilized in mechanical engineering for the assessment of material stress and material loading. Their core mechanical function is to evaluate complex, multi-axial, or spatial stress states by mathematically reducing them to a single, scalar equivalent stress value. This scalar value is designed to be directly comparable to the properties derived from a simpler uniaxial loading of a bar, thereby allowing engineers to accurately perform structural failure analysis. The application and calculation of these equivalent stresses are typically discussed and applied under the assumption of an Elastic Stress State (ESZ).

Within academic and educational contexts, Festigkeitshypothesen is a standard topic featured prominently in German engineering mechanics documents and lecture frameworks. For example, within the structured materials of specific textbooks and courses, the topic is introduced in Section 11.4 and further detailed in Section 12.3. In these academic sections, the principles of reducing a spatial stress state to a scalar equivalent stress are methodically explored to instruct on material strength and failure evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fcd058f225c6403a6dd2ac2e4f810f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db9317a3d674bad613adaf9f20eaf18d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b562c6cf8ea640066c4829c2dbc8a288&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427b2f7911554c401a806ff733b5975e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999596</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
