<node id="Schubspannungshypothese">
  <data key="d0">Schubspannungshypothese</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strength hypothesis where material stress is characterized by the maximum shear stress&lt;SEP&gt;Shear stress hypothesis or Tresca criterion, the second main strength hypothesis which states that material stress is characterized by the maximum shear stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The shear stress hypothesis, a type of failure hypothesis transitioned to in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The German language term for the Shear Stress Hypothesis, explicitly referenced in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the Shear Stress Hypothesis, discussed in the surrounding technical mechanics documentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Shear Stress Hypothesis, also known as Tresca, one of the specific failure theories presented under Festigkeitshypothesen</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b562c6cf8ea640066c4829c2dbc8a288&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5103d6213d27573d3ccf1c58deb496d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypothese Der Gestaltänderungsenergie">
  <data key="d0">Hypothese Der Gestaltänderungsenergie</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strength hypothesis where material stress is characterized by the energy portion necessary for changing the shape, derived by subtracting the hydrostatic portion from total internal energy&lt;SEP&gt;The third hypothesis, also known as the Distortion Energy Hypothesis, discussed in Section 12.3.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the Distortion Energy Hypothesis, representing a failure criterion whose derivation is attributed to the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The Distortion Energy Hypothesis, also known as the von Mises yield criterion, which isolates distortional energy by subtracting hydrostatic energy from total energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the von Mises yield criterion, mentioned explicitly in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Distortion Energy Hypothesis, also known as von Mises, one of the specific failure theories presented under Festigkeitshypothesen&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as the Shape Change Energy Hypothesis, attributed to Richard von Mises, relating to the mathematical formulation of the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vergleichsspannung">
  <data key="d0">Vergleichsspannung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A scalar equivalent stress value derived from reducing the spatial stress state, comparable with uniaxial loading of a bar&lt;SEP&gt;A scalar equivalent stress value that can be compared to uniaxial material limits.&lt;SEP&gt;Equivalent stress derived from principal stresses and distortion energy principles in German engineering mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;The uniaxial equivalent stress state to which the strain energy, expressed strictly in terms of principal stresses, is equated.&lt;SEP&gt;Equivalent stresses derived mathematically under the Festigkeitshypothesen framework, fundamental for predicting material yielding and structural failure</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a498a78500a61b206c4b1205b49030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
