<node id="Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall">
  <data key="d0">Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The authors of the book Technische Mechanik 2&lt;SEP&gt;Authors of the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' published in 2017.&lt;SEP&gt;The authors of the book Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verzerrungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Verzerrungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Verzerrungszustand is the German term for "strain state" or "strain analysis," representing a fundamental concept within the field of continuum mechanics. It describes the state of strain in a material and the overall kinematics of deformation, specifically detailing how small deformations alter the lengths and angles of a material body. Conceptually, the strain state is composed of elongation (Dehnung) and shear (Gleitung), and it is strictly defined by kinematic relationships involving displacements. The concept is frequently illustrated through the two-dimensional deformation of differential material elements and serves as the surrounding context for foundational strain tensor definitions and related mathematical equations.

To transition from physical deformation descriptions into rigorous mathematical formalism, the analysis of Verzerrungszustand utilizes Taylor series expansions. This mathematical foundation allows for the comprehensive analysis of strain in multi-dimensional systems, providing the necessary framework to evaluate complex geometric changes within a deforming body.

In academic settings, Verzerrungszustand is a core topic extensively covered in continuum mechanics lectures and engineering documents, typically structured around Chapter 12 and specifically Section 12.1. According to lecture materials that reference standard textbooks such as Technische Mechanik 2, this academic section introduces the mathematical basis for strain analysis. The instructional coverage includes the kinematics of uniaxial deformation, the deformation of surface bodies, the behavior of displacement vectors, and the detailed analysis of changes in side lengths and angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd468369fbd1eac6b7662866f9ac8fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f01ffec0f2bc0b862fdc1a6a3126dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9f59f4bf11344385ddf5b19b6c93607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4977643a568de41c3595cee58107e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998798</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verzerrung">
  <data key="d0">Verzerrung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain, defined as the sum of Dehnung (elongation) and Gleitung (shear), linked to displacements through kinematic relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gleitung">
  <data key="d0">Gleitung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear component of strain representing angular change in a plane, also referred to as Scherung.&lt;SEP&gt;A German term used as an alternative name for the total engineering shear strain in the context of the deformation diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for total shear strain, synonymous with γ_xy, representing the sum of rotational angles α and β.&lt;SEP&gt;A German term for the engineering shear strain, representing the total change in an initially right angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylorreihe">
  <data key="d0">Taylorreihe</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Taylor series, a power series used to approximate arbitrary functions at an evaluation or development point, applied to describe displacements of neighboring points.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ebener Verzerrungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Ebener Verzerrungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Plane strain state (EVZ), fully defined at a point by the strain components epsilon_x, epsilon_y, and gamma_xy.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the planar strain state (EVZ).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Querkontraktion">
  <data key="d0">Querkontraktion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transverse contraction effect where a stress sigma_x causes an increase in length and a reduction in width.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Homogen">
