</node>
<node id="Räumlicher Verzerrungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Räumlicher Verzerrungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A spatial (three-dimensional) strain state that is shown to result from a plane stress state through superposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d4e48f780c214c48a682a8ccd2b8aa87-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784964351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Objectives Overview for Strain State and Elasticity (image)">
  <data key="d0">Learning Objectives Overview for Strain State and Elasticity (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image serves as a visual agenda for Chapter 12 ('Verzerrungszustand, Elastizitätsgesetz') of a lecture on Mechanics in Materials Technology II. By using the mnemonic 'LEARN' spelled out on wooden tiles, the slide introduces six fundamental topics: Generalized Kinematics, Displacement Vectors, Strain Tensors, Hooke's Model in multiple dimensions, Plane Strain States, and Strength Hypotheses. It bridges basic kinematic concepts with advanced elastic material behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c00d76308ed09c61ea86f8e0dd91335c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 2 - Strain State Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 2 - Strain State Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '2' on page index 1 of the Elastostatik lecture. This page introduces section 12.1 Verzerrungszustand (Strain State), covering kinematics of uniaxial deformation, displacement and strain definitions, and deformation of planar bodies (Scheiben) with position-dependent displacement vectors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07a2e53cd9218f94891c27beeca5d5c2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Uniaxial Tension and Strain Transformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Uniaxial Tension and Strain Transformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual explanation of the strain state in a plate under uniaxial tension ($\\sigma$). It contrasts the 'undeformierte Scheibe' (undeformed disk) with the 'deformierte Scheibe' (deformed disk) to show how the displacement vector $u$ moves a point from $P$ to $P'$. Crucially, it uses two oriented squares (1 and 2) to demonstrate that while an axis-aligned element only changes side lengths, a 45-degree rotated element undergoes both elongation and angular distortion (shear), illustrating the coordinate-dependent nature of the strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8de9b13ad4938915bf58f062528f166</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 3 - Strain State Analysis (Verzerrungszustand) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 3 - Strain State Analysis (Verzerrungszustand) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 3 of a continuum mechanics textbook covering Section 12.1 on strain states. Discusses kinematics of uniaxial tension rod deformation, deformation of 2D bodies (Scheibe), location-dependent displacement vectors, and infinitesimal strain approximations for side lengths and angle changes. Establishes mathematical framework for strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3662016417f4ba5ebb2bdbe894e29fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="2D Displacement Vector Field Decomposition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">2D Displacement Vector Field Decomposition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the location-dependent displacement vector field of a 2D planar body (Scheibe) in continuum mechanics. By decomposing the displacement into Cartesian components, it establishes the continuous differentiable foundation necessary for applying Taylor series approximations to derive spatial derivatives, which ultimately define the material's strain state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e61a123081a6e18fa8de68f6d10d237</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="2D Differential Element Deformation and Strain Derivation Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">2D Differential Element Deformation and Strain Derivation Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual derivation of strain components for a 2D 'Scheibe' (disk/plate) under small deformation theory. It maps the transformation of a square differential element (PQRS) into a parallelogram-like shape (P'Q'R'S'), defining normal strain through changes in side length (using partial derivatives like ∂u/∂x) and shear strain (γxy) through the change in the internal angle (π/2). This serves as the primary visual aid for the 'Verzerrungszustand' (strain state) section, specifically illustrating the application of Taylor series to displacement fields.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9972595c50fe056a02ee9b5484194d34</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Line Element PQ (dy=0) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element PQ (dy=0) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines an infinitesimal material line element oriented along the x-axis in a 2D continuum. This geometric constraint is the foundational setup for deriving the normal strain component εₓₓ through Taylor series linearization of the displacement field at point P for small deformation analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be4811e4e1e18323bb62d62e6b316fff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Multivariable Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multivariable Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the first-order Taylor series expansion for the displacement field $u(x,y)$ of a 2D body. It relates the displacement of a neighboring point to the partial derivatives of the displacement at the original location. It is a crucial mathematical step for deriving the linearized strain tensor under the assumption of small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d1cfb1732152280c4a01c040b2e6095</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Multivariate Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement Field (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multivariate Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement Field (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the two-dimensional Taylor series expansion of the displacement component v(x,y) at a neighboring point, serving as the mathematical foundation for deriving the infinitesimal strain tensor in continuum mechanics. Under small deformation assumptions, truncation after linear terms yields displacement gradients that characterize both normal and shear strains in deformable bodies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d5426abdda67653714efb16800c3287</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Taylor Expansion Integral (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Taylor Expansion Integral (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An integral formulation of the first-order Taylor series expansion for the displacement vector field v in continuum mechanics. This expression establishes the kinematic relationship between displacement gradients and strain, serving as the foundational derivation for the infinitesimal strain tensor under small deformation assumptions in the analysis of deformable bodies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e06032218e4c1967abbab37ae6641ebd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed Infinitesimal Line Element Length (1D Small-Strain Kinematics) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Infinitesimal Line Element Length (1D Small-Strain Kinematics) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Derives the deformed length P'Q' of an infinitesimal 1D line element using a first-order Taylor expansion of the displacement field. By expressing the length change as $\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}dx$, it provides the kinematic foundation for the infinitesimal engineering strain $\varepsilon = \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}$ and serves as the 1D precursor to the multidimensional strain tensor in linear continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-656aecb913f7c381fb4665742e4f637a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain-Displacement Relation in x-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain-Displacement Relation in x-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the normal strain ε_x as the partial derivative of the x-displacement component u with respect to x. This kinematic relation is fundamental to infinitesimal strain theory, serving as the basis for constructing the strain tensor in continuum mechanics. It connects displacement fields to deformation measures, enabling subsequent analysis of strain transformation, principal strains, Mohr's strain circle, and Hooke's constitutive law within the elasticity framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784979705</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 4 - Verzerrungszustand and Taylor Series Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 4 - Verzerrungszustand and Taylor Series Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 4 transitions from strain state analysis (Verzerrungszustand) to Taylor series foundations. It covers small deformation kinematics, displacement vectors of neighboring points, and introduces Taylor series as approximation tools for displacement functions, bridging continuum mechanics concepts with mathematical formalism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-effab31edfbc1ed53274cb587d940195</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Series Expansion of a Displacement Function (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Expansion of a Displacement Function (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the Taylor series expansion used to approximate a function via its derivatives at a reference point. In this continuum mechanics context, it mathematically justifies the linearization of the displacement field, allowing the definition of infinitesimal strain tensors by ignoring higher-order terms under the small deformations assumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b92da02c5d0dd1869c2dc092f353cdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Reference Configuration Mapping (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Reference Configuration Mapping (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the initial undeformed state in 1D continuum mechanics by equating the spatial position $y_0$ to the material coordinate $x$. It provides the necessary baseline for measuring displacement fields and deriving the infinitesimal strain tensor via Taylor series expansion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72021849640dc183602f284b9c3c66aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Displacement Position Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Displacement Position Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Describes the position y of a material point after a small displacement dx from reference position x within infinitesimal strain theory. Foundational to Taylor series expansion of displacement fields in continuum mechanics, enabling derivation of strain tensors under the assumption of small deformations in the Verzerrungszustand analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-494cb7041dd14201bf248bd093a44f0e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement Field (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement Field (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the Taylor series expansion for a function at a neighboring point $x+dx$. In the context of continuum mechanics, it serves to approximate the displacement field for small deformations by linearizing the function, which is a foundational step for defining the infinitesimal strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ed6149184844bc71d103c1a70868a6e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Multivariable Taylor Series Expansion (First-Order, 2D) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multivariable Taylor Series Expansion (First-Order, 2D) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Two-dimensional first-order Taylor series expansion of the displacement field f(x,y) around point (x₀,y₀), used in strain analysis (Verzerrungszustand) to linearize displacement fields for small deformation theory in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1fdf67f2e6527f402df1216f21f38576</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order Terms of a 2D Taylor Series Expansion (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order Terms of a 2D Taylor Series Expansion (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation details the quadratic terms of a multidimensional Taylor series used to approximate the displacement of neighboring points in a continuous medium. In the context of small deformation mechanics, these higher-order terms represent curvature and are subsequently neglected to linearize the strain-displacement relations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d3d9627dfc247bb92e4155f31d6f366</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram_of_2D_Shear_Strain_Definition (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram_of_2D_Shear_Strain_Definition (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This figure illustrates the kinematic derivation of shear strain γ_xy for a 2D differential element. It visually demonstrates how an initially rectangular element (PQRS) deforms into a parallelogram-like shape (P'Q'R'S'), utilizing Taylor series expansions to express displacements at neighboring points. The diagram specifically highlights the angular distortions α and β, whose sum constitutes the total shear strain γ_xy, directly supporting the section on 'Verzerrungszustand' (State of Strain).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab52cfc5adc385427faa15ebadce6067</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Series Approximation of Deformed Line Element Length (Normal Strain Component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Approximation of Deformed Line Element Length (Normal Strain Component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order Taylor approximation of the deformed length P'S' in small deformation continuum mechanics. Expresses the elongation of a y-oriented line element using the displacement gradient ∂v/∂y, directly yielding the normal strain εyy. Foundational equation for deriving strain tensor components in infinitesimal strain theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d47a70b13c90aef3f51661f5c2aa1ed7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component in y-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component in y-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal strain ε_y as the relative length change of a line element oriented along y, approximated via ∂v/∂y under small deformation assumptions. Part of the strain state analysis (Verzerrungszustand) in continuum mechanics, derived using Taylor series linearization. The displayed LaTeX contains severe notation corruption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abb8338dbb97dee17efc22c443655f86</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Line Element Constraint (PQ: dy = 0) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element Constraint (PQ: dy = 0) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines a differential line element PQ parallel to the x-axis with no y-variation, serving as the undeformed reference configuration for deriving normal strain and shear angle components in two-dimensional strain analysis within continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1080f92274e2a6deb71a2d9c0f108561</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 6 - Verzerrungszustand Discussion (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 6 - Verzerrungszustand Discussion (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 6 in a German mechanics document, located within Chapter 12.1 on strain state (Verzerrungszustand). The page covers angle changes α and β, total angular deformation γ_xy, and shear strain concepts under small deformation assumptions, following Taylor series foundations from preceding pages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a088fba2cb39b0c35094538e0921861e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of 2D Strain State Geometric Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of 2D Strain State Geometric Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram provides the geometric basis for defining the plane strain state (EVZ). It visualizes the transformation of an infinitesimal rectangular element (PQRS) into a deformed quadrilateral (P'Q'R'S') to derive normal and shear strain components. By utilizing first-order Taylor expansions of the displacement field (u, v), the figure illustrates how partial derivatives relate to changes in side length (normal strain) and the reduction of the right angle (shear strain γxy), directly supporting the mathematical definitions of the strain tensor found in the accompanying text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5827d7b0fa7dd15b64992bd53f253f2a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Line Element Constraint (dy = 0) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element Constraint (dy = 0) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the geometric orientation of an infinitesimal line segment PQ in a 2D continuum. Setting dy=0 restricts the segment to be parallel to the x-axis, serving as a foundational step in kinematics to derive normal strain (εx) and shear angle (α) from displacement gradients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01f868dbc2e5d1a7cfa696303e7dacc6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Strain Angular Rotation Derivation (α = ∂u/∂y) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Small-Strain Angular Rotation Derivation (α = ∂u/∂y) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Derives the angular rotation α of a y-aligned line element under small deformation assumptions. Shows that tan(α) simplifies to α = ∂u/∂y when normal strain ε_y ≪ 1, forming a key kinematic relation contributing to the engineering shear strain γ_xy = ∂u/∂y + ∂v/∂x in plane strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457a36bbc23c1a17a9097a0845eee4cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Deformation Angular Rotation Kinematic Relation (β Derivation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Small-Deformation Angular Rotation Kinematic Relation (β Derivation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Derives the rotation angle β = ∂v/∂x by linearizing the exact tangent relationship under small-deformation assumptions. This kinematic equation is essential for formulating the engineering shear strain γ_xy = α + β, which defines the off-diagonal strain tensor components in plane strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a537755fad52fb449b42d287ac577da</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Malformed Angle Equality (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Malformed Angle Equality (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically invalid and dimensionally inconsistent equation equating $160^{\circ}$ to a nonsensical fraction. It is likely an OCR artifact originating from a continuum mechanics text discussing angular changes and shear strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0f96b5896968414d1b6bf0887f9b19e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 7 - Strain State and Strain Tensor Formulation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 7 - Strain State and Strain Tensor Formulation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number identifier for a key theoretical page in a German mechanics textbook covering section 12.1 'Verzerrungszustand' (Strain State). The page establishes strain as the combination of normal strain and shear strain, defines the plane strain state (EVZ), presents the 2D strain tensor matrix formulation with kinematic relationships to displacements, and draws a structural analogy to the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51958b1ca1066bb2c5c6558a251e7c1c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
