</node>
<node id="Page 8 - Verzerrungszustand Transformation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 8 - Verzerrungszustand Transformation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '8' identifying a page in a German mechanics lecture covering plane strain state (EVZ). The page discusses strain tensor properties analogous to stress tensor, coordinate transformation equations for strain under rotation angle φ, principal directions, and principal strains. This content continues from page 7's introduction of strain tensors and supports the broader topic of strain analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ba6f9eb015d40923b465baf8cd33705</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Academic Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Academic Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic source citation appearing on page 7 of mechanics course material. Attributes content to Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), 'Technische Mechanik 2,' Springer Vieweg. This German-language footer establishes that the surrounding content on strain state (Verzerrungszustand), strain tensors, and plane strain analysis is derived from this standard engineering mechanics textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c566099995f21611a7ed8370fd0b2f96</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Right-Angle Configuration of Reference Element (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Right-Angle Configuration of Reference Element (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines a geometric configuration where angles at vertices B, C, D equal 90°, establishing an orthogonal reference element. This serves as the undeformed baseline state in strain analysis (Verzerrungszustand), against which normal and shear strains are measured. Fundamental to the plane strain tensor formulation and Mohr's circle methodology in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7bc539f6faee47a881c0bf677650535</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Corrupted Mohr's Circle Angle Relation / Principal Strain Derivation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Corrupted Mohr's Circle Angle Relation / Principal Strain Derivation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A severely corrupted equation likely related to Mohr's strain circle angle-doubling property or principal strain/direction derivation within plane strain analysis. The 1/2 factor and angle notation suggest connection to the fundamental relationship between physical rotation angles and Mohr's circle angles, consistent with the surrounding German continuum mechanics content on Verzerrungszustand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca312b7276cf1e7919da9f435f3fd2ce</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
