<node id="Verzerrungstensor und Transformationsgleichungen (Strain Tensor and Transformation Equations) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Verzerrungstensor und Transformationsgleichungen (Strain Tensor and Transformation Equations) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A malformed equation (sequence of zeros) from German mechanics lecture material on plane strain states (EVZ). Context indicates discussion of strain tensor properties, coordinate transformations, principal strains, and Mohr's strain circle. Likely intended to represent kinematic relationships or transformation equations for the symmetric 2×2 strain tensor ε with components εx, εy, and γxy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-68b0372ba7b3f4fbbe8d25d5d03bcfb1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Transformation Equations for Plane Strain (EVZ) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Strain Transformation Equations for Plane Strain (EVZ) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Corrupted representation of strain transformation equations relating strain components under coordinate rotation. Part of derivation showing how ε_x, ε_y, γ_xy transform to principal strains ε_1, ε_2. Demonstrates mathematical analogy between strain tensor and stress tensor, foundational to Mohr's strain circle analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-720c80c52f64cce1f8a2abe33e101a26</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 9 - Strain State Section (Mohr's Strain Circle) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 9 - Strain State Section (Mohr's Strain Circle) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '9' marks the ninth page of a mechanics lecture document. This page continues Section 12.1 on strain states (Verzerrungszustand), introducing Mohr's strain circle as an analogy to Mohr's stress circle, and beginning the discussion of spatial deformation states. It serves as a navigational reference within the broader topic of continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fdf056883909c82bf0c6feffebd0aa8a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Displacement Vector Decomposition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Displacement Vector Decomposition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Decomposes the position/displacement vector field r(x,y,z) into Cartesian components u, v, w along orthonormal basis vectors e_x, e_y, e_{z_i}. Serves as the kinematic foundation for deriving strain tensor components and strain-displacement relations within continuum mechanics and elasticity theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8fc5b207400d79037d6eaa00da69e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain-Displacement Relation (x-component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain-Displacement Relation (x-component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the normal strain $\varepsilon_x$ as the partial derivative of the $x$-displacement component $u$ with respect to coordinate $x$. It is the foundational kinematic equation of infinitesimal strain theory, forming the basis for the strain tensor, Mohr's strain circle, and Hooke's law within the elasticity framework discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7fecb5bafad96b80b4045b40dfe0c3d4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component in Y-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component in Y-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the normal strain in the y-direction as the partial derivative of the y-displacement with respect to the y-coordinate. It serves as a fundamental kinematic relationship in continuum mechanics, connecting physical displacement to the strain tensor used to derive principal strains and formulate the elasticity law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-933b9b2997a9637268ac409a1165480b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component in z-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component in z-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the normal strain $\varepsilon_z$ as the partial derivative of the z-displacement $w$ with respect to the z-coordinate. It is a fundamental kinematic relation used to construct the strain tensor for analyzing 3D deformation states in solid mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-043d39ba30268e6d95d3d40d40e40951</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Definition (xy-plane) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Definition (xy-plane) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the engineering shear strain γ_xy as the sum of cross-partial derivatives of displacement components u and v. It quantifies angular distortion in the xy-plane and relates to the symmetric strain tensor via ε_xy = γ_xy/2. Foundational for Mohr's strain circle and elasticity law derivations discussed in the surrounding continuum mechanics context.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7b28db832be0d02baf9c6d6a81309f8f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_xz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_xz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the engineering shear strain γ_xz as the sum of partial derivatives of displacement components u and w. This kinematic equation relates the displacement field to the strain tensor, forming the geometric foundation for the elasticity framework connecting stresses and strains in isotropic homogeneous materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cec8274bcc69e7cbbd1496ae2b1d0df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_yz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_yz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the y-z engineering shear strain as the sum of cross-partial derivatives of displacement components v and w. This kinematic equation is part of the infinitesimal strain tensor and provides the geometric link between displacement fields and deformation, serving as a foundation for elasticity analysis and Mohr's strain circle constructions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaf312d6fb072a959a65e8795d01c5f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Infinitesimal Strain Tensor (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Infinitesimal Strain Tensor (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the 3D symmetric strain tensor, mapping tensor strain components to engineering strain notation (normal strains ε and shear strains γ/2). It is the foundational kinematic quantity in the lecture's treatment of deformation states, serving as the strain analog to the stress tensor and connecting to elasticity theory via Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89fc7bd3aed57e0363144b81fded148a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 10 - Elastizitätsgesetz Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 10 - Elastizitätsgesetz Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 10 within a German mechanics textbook (Chapter 12: Festigkeitslehre). Marks the transition from strain state analysis (12.1) to the elasticity law (12.2), introducing Hooke's law, plane stress state, and material properties including Poisson's ratio. Essential navigational reference for engineering mechanics content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00395e862a9f748121116d2d209e61db</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Uniaxial Tension and Lateral Contraction (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Uniaxial Tension and Lateral Contraction (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram illustrates the deformation of a rectangular material element subjected to uniaxial tensile stress ($\\sigma_x$). By comparing the original black-outlined shape with the resulting green-filled shape, the image demonstrates longitudinal elongation coupled with lateral contraction (Poisson's effect). This visualization supports the surrounding text's explanation of the Law of Elasticity (Elastizitätsgesetz) for homogeneous and isotropic materials under uniaxial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-42608c2db97b7eaa4950aad383754772</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Hooke's Law (Normal Stress-Strain Relation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Hooke's Law (Normal Stress-Strain Relation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the fundamental linear-elastic relationship ε_x = σ_x/E, defining how normal stress produces proportional normal strain via Young's modulus. It serves as the foundational building block for deriving the generalized plane stress constitutive equations within the Elastizitätsgesetz framework, preceding the incorporation of Poisson effects and stress superposition for isotropic homogeneous materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4886f7d35e15eaf5c79ad226fa3abd54</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Poisson's Transverse Strain Relation (Querkontraktionsgleichung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Poisson's Transverse Strain Relation (Querkontraktionsgleichung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation quantifies the Poisson effect, describing transverse strain ε_y induced by uniaxial stress σ_x through Poisson's ratio ν and Young's modulus E. It is central to linear elasticity theory for isotropic materials, linking the planar stress state to the resulting three-dimensional deformation state within Hooke's Law framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03329f99c00bb7e917eed12f2fa4299d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 11 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 11 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '11' from a German mechanics of materials document. Located on a page covering Section 12.2 (Elastizitätsgesetz) discussing stress-strain relationships, material properties (homogeneous/isotropic), and Poisson's ratio. Serves as a navigational reference within lecture-style course material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f79fa3f59d2e30598be0aa8917d6e8c7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Stress-Strain Constitutive Relation with Poisson Effect (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Stress-Strain Constitutive Relation with Poisson Effect (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation describes strains produced by uniaxial normal stress σ_x in an isotropic elastic material. It combines Hooke's Law (ε_x = σ_x/E) with the Poisson effect (ε_y = -ν·σ_x/E), establishing that a single stress component produces strains in both longitudinal and transverse directions. Foundational to the plane stress constitutive law (Elastizitätsgesetz) and superposition principles in linear elasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-711796d0085a5ce3204d8fc2f4bff421</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Stress-Strain Relation with Poisson Effect (Y-Direction) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Stress-Strain Relation with Poisson Effect (Y-Direction) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation from linear elasticity describes how normal stress σ_y in an isotropic material produces both longitudinal strain ε_y (via Young's modulus E) and transverse strain ε_x (via Poisson's ratio ν). It quantifies the Poisson effect within plane stress analysis, forming part of Hooke's Law for two-dimensional stress states in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-52e6005b999e107f14d8b112e5139b51</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Plane Stress (Strain in x-direction) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Plane Stress (Strain in x-direction) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal strain in the x-direction ($\varepsilon_x$) for a plane stress state. It uses the principle of superposition to combine the axial extension from direct stress ($\sigma_x$) and the lateral contraction from transverse stress ($\sigma_y$), utilizing material constants Young's modulus ($E$) and Poisson's ratio ($\nu$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8e13ce7241cae90086ad49bfb372599</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive equation for transverse strain in plane stress (ESZ) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive equation for transverse strain in plane stress (ESZ) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the y-direction normal strain in a plane stress state as the superposition of direct axial strain from σ_y and Poisson-induced transverse contraction from σ_x. It is a component of the generalized Hooke's law for homogeneous isotropic elastic materials, requiring only E and ν as independent material constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e836eeb3357cf07ef61913680bf8ab32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Transverse Strain Equation for Plane Stress State (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Transverse Strain Equation for Plane Stress State (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the out-of-plane normal strain ($\varepsilon_z$) resulting from in-plane normal stresses ($\sigma_x, \sigma_y$) under a plane stress state. It mathematically quantifies the Poisson effect, proving that 2D planar stresses inherently induce a 3D spatial strain state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3638afe8725b24665dd3fd352297d96</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784983608</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 12 - Elastizitätsgesetz (Shear Stress &amp; Material Constants) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 12 - Elastizitätsgesetz (Shear Stress &amp; Material Constants) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '12' marks a page in a German engineering mechanics lecture covering Section 12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz. The page addresses shear stress loading on plates, shear modulus determination via torsion testing, and the principle that isotropic elastic materials have only two independent constants (E, ν). This pagination element provides structural navigation within the continuum mechanics documentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c4af9a59d5d11b62919e03b25da45c6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Stress and Strain Illustration (image)">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Stress and Strain Illustration (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a 2D material element undergoing pure shear deformation. It contrasts the original square geometry (black outline) with the resulting distorted parallelogram (green fill) caused by shear stresses τ_xy. The image specifically highlights the definition of engineering shear strain γ_xy as the angular distortion from a right angle. It serves as a key visual aid for the section on the Law of Elasticity ('Elastizitätsgesetz'), illustrating how shear stresses relate to material distortion and the determination of the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76b85c3172e42f9fdc3ecc7419923c1a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Shear Stress (Schubspannungs-Dehnungs-Gesetz) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Shear Stress (Schubspannungs-Dehnungs-Gesetz) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Linear-elastic constitutive relation τ_xy = G·γ_xy, defining shear stress as the product of shear modulus G and engineering shear strain. It is the shear analog to σ=E·ε and a fundamental component of the generalized Hooke's Law for isotropic materials, where G is derivable from E and Poisson's ratio ν.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-105c568e6743f744d81fbef0d702fb23</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Modulus Definition (Schubmodul G) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Modulus Definition (Schubmodul G) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Formal definition of shear modulus $G$ in units of $[N/mm^2]$ within the elasticity law framework. For isotropic materials, $G$ relates to Young's modulus $E$ and Poisson's ratio $\nu$ via $G = E/[2(1+\nu)]$, explaining why only two independent elastic constants exist. Determined experimentally through torsion tests on thin-walled tubes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c82bc712115f44fdb018937b3c09bcd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Elastic Moduli Relationship (Shear Modulus Equation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Elastic Moduli Relationship (Shear Modulus Equation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation relates the shear modulus $G$ to Young's modulus $E$ and Poisson's ratio $\nu$ for isotropic linear elastic materials. It demonstrates material parameter dependency, reducing three apparent constants to two independent ones. Derived through biaxial stress superposition, it is fundamental to the Elastizitätsgesetz framework and structural analysis of shear-loaded components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5380c715d41d1a9eda64809a8515df62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 13 - Elastizitätsgesetz: E-Modul and Schubmodul Relationship Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 13 - Elastizitätsgesetz: E-Modul and Schubmodul Relationship Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 13 of Section 12.2 (Elastizitätsgesetz) in a German mechanics textbook. Contains the derivation connecting Young's modulus (E-Modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) via a biaxially loaded plate analysis. Builds on prior discussion of shear stress loading and isotropic material constants (E, ν), establishing the fundamental interdependence of elastic material parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61c2a3595cf4825e79e4d9f3da1ac0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Pure Shear Analysis (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Pure Shear Analysis (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square material element subjected to biaxial loading: tensile stress $\\sigma_y = \\sigma$ and compressive stress $\\sigma_x = -\\sigma$. It features a dashed rhombus representing the $45^\\circ$ orientation where pure shear stress occurs. This visual aid is essential for the mechanical derivation linking the elastic constants E-modulus and shear modulus, as discussed in the surrounding section on elasticity laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22e6e1991ff06759715b2879f8b3d58f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrs Circle for Pure Shear Stress State (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrs Circle for Pure Shear Stress State (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image presents a Mohr's circle illustrating a biaxial stress state defined by $\sigma_x = -\sigma$ and $\sigma_y = \sigma$. The diagram visually demonstrates that for this specific loading condition, the maximum shear stress $\tau$ is equal to $\sigma$. This representation is a key component in the derivation of the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and the shear modulus (Schubmodul) for isotropic materials, as discussed in the 'Elastizitätsgesetz' section of the source document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-049624ea90ecb696be76cdbf81e5f85f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 14 - Elastizitätsgesetz: E-Modul and Schubmodul Relationship Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 14 - Elastizitätsgesetz: E-Modul and Schubmodul Relationship Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 14 of section 12.2 (Elastizitätsgesetz) in a German mechanics textbook. Continues derivation from page 12 regarding the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) using a biaxially loaded plate model. Establishes the constitutive relationships for isotropic elastic materials with two independent material constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-26bd2b70fb3b142faf35250fbe217c2b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Stress and Shear Deformation Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Stress and Shear Deformation Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes the derivation of the relationship between Young's modulus (E-modulus) and shear modulus (G-modulus) as described in section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz'. It depicts a square plate (Scheibe) undergoing pure shear deformation due to equal tensile stress in the y-direction and compressive stress in the x-direction, transforming the initial square into a rhombus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784987855</data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Strain Analysis Diagram for Elastic Moduli Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Strain Analysis Diagram for Elastic Moduli Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a geometric visualization used to derive the relationship between elastic constants (Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio, and Shear modulus). It illustrates how a biaxially loaded rectangular element (width $l - \\Delta l$) causes a square element oriented at 45 degrees to deform into a rhombus. The key feature is the quantification of the shear strain $\\gamma$ through the angular change ($90^\\circ - \\gamma$) at the vertices, linking linear deformation to angular distortion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4be99bc6b10ef5fb9755e3eaf3da4bf2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed Length Expression in Biaxial Elasticity (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Length Expression in Biaxial Elasticity (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The expression $l + \Delta l$ denotes the deformed length of a material element under axial loading, fundamental to strain definition ($\varepsilon = \Delta l / l$). In the context of deriving the E-Modul/Schubmodul relationship for a biaxially loaded plate, it describes the post-deformation edge length used in geometric analysis to establish the constitutive relation $G = E/[2(1+\nu)]$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2666f9a30c1356a2e1dfb9581e8db84c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Plane Stress Strain Equation (x-direction) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Plane Stress Strain Equation (x-direction) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal strain in the x-direction for an isotropic material under biaxial stress using generalized Hooke's Law. It is pivotal in continuum mechanics for relating stress and strain, specifically serving as the basis to derive the mathematical relationship between Young's modulus and the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-345c7cd81675a64174bdf1ab091608b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Component (Poisson-coupled) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Component (Poisson-coupled) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates a normal strain component under biaxial loading using Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio. It is central to deriving the relationship between E-modulus and shear modulus G via a biaxially loaded plate, demonstrating how superposed normal stresses produce shear on rotated planes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-579240f223cc77905782f2fd495073cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law with Poisson Effect) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law with Poisson Effect) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Constitutive equation for biaxial stress states relating normal stress, Young's modulus, and Poisson's ratio to resulting strain. Central to deriving the relationship G = E/[2(1+ν)] between elastic constants, this equation demonstrates how the factor (1+ν) emerges from multiaxial loading conditions in isotropic elasticity theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c2d7c37e7f76ca0330b7c23d7399f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Biaxial Strain (y-component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Biaxial Strain (y-component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal strain in the y-direction ($\varepsilon_y$) for a linear elastic material under biaxial stress. It uses Young's modulus ($E$), Poisson's ratio ($\nu$), and normal stresses ($\sigma_x, \sigma_y$). In this context, it serves as the foundational step to derive the relationship between Young's modulus and the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e3cc1bf7fb8a08a666089f2a56d119de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Constitutive Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Constitutive Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation relates normal strain ε to stress σ, Young's modulus E, and Poisson's ratio ν under biaxial loading conditions. The (1+ν) factor captures the Poisson contribution from transverse stress. It serves as a key intermediate result in deriving the relationship between Young's modulus and shear modulus (G = E/[2(1+ν)]) through analysis of a biaxially loaded plate within classical elasticity theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59d47ab56950f422bdfab908cf5dd3d6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Longitudinal Strain (Dehnung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Longitudinal Strain (Dehnung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines strain ε as the ratio of length change Δl to original length l, with the equivalent form Δl = εl. This fundamental kinematic relationship is foundational to the Elastizitätsgesetz and serves as the starting point for deriving constitutive laws, including the relationship between Young's modulus and shear modulus in biaxial loading scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6be1afb81779c915257c65ea4758fcb4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 15 - Elastizitätsgesetz Section (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 15 - Elastizitätsgesetz Section (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '15' from a German mechanics textbook, located within section 12.2 on Elasticity Law. The page contains content deriving the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) through analysis of a biaxially loaded plate, representing a key theoretical derivation in solid mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e52e2af153042cc195e6037c41f8c7e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Loading Diagram for Shear Modulus Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Loading Diagram for Shear Modulus Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square material element subjected to a specific biaxial stress state: tensile stress $\\sigma_y = \\sigma$ and compressive stress $\\sigma_x = -\\sigma$. The transition from the solid square to the dashed rhombus illustrates the resulting shear deformation. In the context of Section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz' (Elasticity Law), this model is used to mathematically derive the fundamental relationship between the elastic constants, specifically linking Young's modulus to the shear modulus through the analysis of strains at a 45-degree orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e96c5c4f2f92b4aa286ed81e7bb5bf45</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Deformation Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Deformation Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram illustrates the geometric deformation of a square material element under biaxial stress to support the derivation of the Law of Elasticity ('Elastizitätsgesetz'). It visually defines the relationships between normal strain ($\\varepsilon$), shown via horizontal contraction to $l(1-\\varepsilon)$, and shear strain ($\\gamma$), shown via angular distortions like $90^\\circ - \\gamma$. This figure is essential for establishing the theoretical link between Young's Modulus (E-Modul) and the Shear Modulus (Schubmodul).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-788302e321f12b98bb59ae6b39e58651</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Kinematic Strain Relationship (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Kinematic Strain Relationship (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation provides the exact geometric relationship between shear strain ($\gamma$) and principal normal strain ($\varepsilon$) in a biaxially loaded element under pure shear. It mathematically links angular distortion to linear diagonal extensions. Within the context, it serves as the fundamental kinematic basis required to derive the theoretical relationship between Young's Modulus ($E$) and Shear Modulus ($G$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f204ded74328fe648ce850a266d8aea</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Strain Definition for Deformed Length (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Strain Definition for Deformed Length (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the deformed length of a material element as either the sum of original length and elongation (l + Δl) or equivalently as l(1 + ε), introducing engineering strain ε = Δl/l. This fundamental kinematic relationship underpins the constitutive equations in the derivation connecting Young's modulus and shear modulus for biaxially loaded elastic media.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8948408da6213e93279db456f1bfd686</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Tangent Subtraction Formula for Shear Strain (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Tangent Subtraction Formula for Shear Strain (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation applies the trigonometric tangent subtraction identity to the shear strain angle \(\gamma\). It is an intermediate geometric step used to relate the deformation of principal axes to shear deformation, specifically for deriving the relationship between Young's Modulus and Shear Modulus in a biaxially loaded plate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84af4af08c337541ab4d753179d056aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear-Strain Geometric Compatibility Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear-Strain Geometric Compatibility Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation relates shear angle γ to longitudinal strain ε through tangent half-angle formulation, serving as a key geometric compatibility condition in deriving the E-Modul to Schubmodul relationship (G = E/2(1+ν)) for biaxially loaded plates in linear elasticity theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8354d191ae1a5d33420ead8f19cb2bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Relationship Between Shear Strain and Principal Strain (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Relationship Between Shear Strain and Principal Strain (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation establishes the exact geometric relationship between engineering shear strain ($\gamma$) and principal normal strain ($\varepsilon$) for a biaxially loaded element. It serves as a fundamental geometric proof in elasticity theory for deriving the relationship between Young's modulus and the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-19e68296204f8f979c6fb6d964e2d513</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Kinematic Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Kinematic Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation relates engineering shear strain ($\gamma$) to normal strain ($\varepsilon$) in a state of pure shear. It is a fundamental kinematic step in elasticity theory used to mathematically derive the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and the shear modulus (Schubmodul) for a biaxially loaded element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0d138d48b9754aaa3a9f08153f51541</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 16 - Elastizitätsgesetz Continuation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 16 - Elastizitätsgesetz Continuation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 16 of a German mechanics textbook, continuing Section 12.2 on Elastizitätsgesetz. This page is part of the derivation establishing the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) using a biaxially loaded plate model, a fundamental concept in linear elasticity theory for isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1bbfb3baa1358f43d185d28b4cad998</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain–Normal Strain Equivalence under Biaxial Loading (E-G Relationship Derivation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain–Normal Strain Equivalence under Biaxial Loading (E-G Relationship Derivation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Derives the relationship γ/2 = (σ/E)(1+ν) by equating engineering shear strain γ/2 to principal normal strain ε in a 45°-rotated frame of a biaxially loaded plate. This is the critical intermediate step in proving G = E/[2(1+ν)], establishing that isotropic elastic materials have only two independent elastic constants within Hooke's constitutive model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17134a0debda801a775f4182db30ece3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Kreis für reinen Schubspannungszustand (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Kreis für reinen Schubspannungszustand (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image presents Mohr's Circle for a state of pure shear, where the principal stresses are σx = -σ and σy = σ. By showing the circle centered at the origin, the diagram demonstrates that the maximum shear stress τ is equal to the normal stress σ. This graphical representation is integral to the derivation of the elasticity law, specifically linking the E-modulus and shear modulus as described in the surrounding chapter on strain states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1f4e67b0d37bb17b1a9a7a4c882a504</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses shear strain γ in terms of shear stress τ, Young's modulus E, and Poisson's ratio ν for isotropic linear elastic materials. Derived from a biaxially loaded plate analysis, it establishes the relationship between shear modulus G and Young's modulus: G = E/[2(1+ν)], demonstrating that only two independent elastic constants define isotropic material behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fe2cc10fcddd5753e9de663d5b9ff6f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Modulus Relationship in Isotropic Elasticity (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Modulus Relationship in Isotropic Elasticity (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation from Section 12.2 (Elastizitätsgesetz) establishes that the shear modulus G equals E/[2(1+ν)], demonstrating that for isotropic materials, G is derived from Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio rather than being independent. It connects Hooke's Law for shear stress (τ=Gγ) to the principal elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d076005662c07449f8e7f2a06f41c0f5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 17 - Hooke's Model in Elastizitätsgesetz (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 17 - Hooke's Model in Elastizitätsgesetz (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 17 of Section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz' presents Hooke's Model (Hookesches Modell), establishing that its principal directions align with the stress tensor and that it applies in any Cartesian coordinate system. It continues the derivation from Page 15 regarding E-Modul and Schubmodul relationships, providing the model's formulation in the principal axis system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-859dd624f88a965d0e3cd7711dac0865</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Plane Stress (Ebener Spannungszustand) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Plane Stress (Ebener Spannungszustand) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Constitutive equations for 2D linear isotropic elasticity relating stress and strain tensors. Provides compliance, stiffness, and principal direction formulations. Foundation for deriving the E-G relationship and extending to thermoelastic problems in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fad7744bffea8222ed907082400bd675</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal-Axis Hooke's Law for Biaxial Stress State (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal-Axis Hooke's Law for Biaxial Stress State (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A 2D formulation of Hooke's Law expressing the first principal strain ε₁ in terms of principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂, Young's modulus E, and Poisson's ratio ν. It captures the Poisson-effect coupling between orthogonal normal stresses and strains in the principal coordinate system of an isotropic linear elastic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7d5b998be73ccbce8db592ac28285fe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Hooke's Law — Second Principal Strain Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Hooke's Law — Second Principal Strain Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Constitutive equation from Hooke's model for isotropic linear elasticity under plane stress in principal coordinates. Relates principal strain ε₂ to both principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂ via Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν, capturing the Poisson cross-coupling effect between orthogonal stress and strain directions. Part of the foundational elasticity framework used to derive the E–G relationship.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1fb07b04318c61367d77e507776bcedf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 18 - Elastizitätsgesetz Temperature Extension (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 18 - Elastizitätsgesetz Temperature Extension (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 18 of a German mechanics textbook, part of Section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz'. This page presents Hooke's Model extended to 3D under temperature change conditions, noting that temperature changes in isotropic materials produce only strains (Dehnungen) without shear strains (Gleitungen). It builds upon the biaxial derivations from page 16.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ed99ffe1373d9bfb20029e9d34061b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Textbook Source Citation (Technische Mechanik 2) (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Textbook Source Citation (Technische Mechanik 2) (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic footer citing Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), Technische Mechanik 2, Springer Vieweg. It attributes the surrounding lecture content on Hooke's Model and the Elastizitätsgesetz (elasticity law), including derivations of elastic moduli and temperature-dependent material behavior, to this established engineering mechanics textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-781e882ee640c30cf6395fa29eead24d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain (3D Isotropic) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain (3D Isotropic) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation expresses normal strain in x-direction for a 3D isotropic linear-elastic material under combined mechanical stress and thermal loading. It combines Poisson-coupled mechanical strain from triaxial stress with thermal expansion. It is central to elastizitätsgesetz analysis and precedes failure hypothesis evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7310fc9ff8199a4adb9ea8ae83560e9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law (y-component with thermal effects) in principal axes (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law (y-component with thermal effects) in principal axes (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the normal strain ε_y in the principal direction y for a 3D isotropic linear elastic material, combining mechanical contributions from triaxial normal stresses via Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν with thermal expansion strain α_T·ΔT. It is a component of the constitutive tensor relationship fundamental to thermo-elastic structural analysis, serving as the bridge between stress states and failure assessment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ea9991a104d799b04daeb4d57e9e7a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law - z-component with Thermal Strain (Thermoelastic Constitutive Equation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law - z-component with Thermal Strain (Thermoelastic Constitutive Equation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation from the Elastizitätsgesetz (Section 12.2) computes the total normal strain ε_z in an isotropic material under combined triaxial stress and thermal loading. It superposes mechanical strain (incorporating Poisson's effect from lateral stresses σ_x, σ_y) with thermal expansion strain (α_T·ΔT). The equation is foundational for subsequent failure analysis using Festigkeitshypothesen, which reduce the 3D stress state to a scalar equivalent stress for design verification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ffa7bb7b801db083b8900d7e3b79369d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Shear Stress-Strain Constitutive Equations (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Shear Stress-Strain Constitutive Equations (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">These equations express the linear relationship between shear stress ($\tau$) and engineering shear strain ($\gamma$) in an isotropic material, scaled by the inverse of the Shear Modulus ($G$). They constitute the shear components of Hooke's Law in 3D, highlighting that temperature variations in isotropic materials do not induce shear strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93ab5b06eeca8e6067749a73dc8b9764</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 19 - Festigkeitshypothesen Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 19 - Festigkeitshypothesen Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 19 of a German engineering textbook marks the start of section 12.3 on Strength Hypotheses. It transitions from elasticity laws to failure criteria, introducing stress-strain diagrams, allowable stress (σ_zul), spatial stress states in bending components, and the concept of comparison stress (σ_V) for reducing multi-axial stress states to equivalent scalar values for design verification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d5e01e8731a5485f2a82ee92d6d889fd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Stress Design Criterion (Vergleichsspannung-Bedingung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Stress Design Criterion (Vergleichsspannung-Bedingung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This inequality defines the central safety condition in structural design: the scalar equivalent stress $\sigma_V$, derived from reducing a multiaxial stress state via a strength hypothesis, must not exceed the material's allowable stress $\sigma_{zul}$ obtained from uniaxial testing. It forms the basis for engineering component dimensioning and failure prevention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a04bee2b2b355ee8a86ab3a642b9069</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 20 - Festigkeitshypothesen Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 20 - Festigkeitshypothesen Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 20 of a German mechanics textbook covering Section 12.3 on Festigkeitshypothesen (strength hypotheses). This page introduces three strength hypotheses under plane stress state (ESZ) conditions, starting with the Normalspannungshypothese applicable to brittle materials. The page continues the discussion from page 18 on reducing 3D stress states to scalar comparison stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1a498a78500a61b206c4b1205b49030</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784992411</data>
</node>
<node id="Normalspannungshypothese (Maximum Principal Stress Criterion) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normalspannungshypothese (Maximum Principal Stress Criterion) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the equivalent stress σ_V as equal to the maximum principal stress σ_1, forming Rankine's failure criterion. It enables reduction of complex 3D stress states to a scalar value comparable with uniaxial test data, particularly applicable to brittle materials under the elastic stress assumption (ESZ).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c9af5fa50c83a2eef08400f4daf629d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 21 - Festigkeitshypothesen (Schubspannungshypothese) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 21 - Festigkeitshypothesen (Schubspannungshypothese) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 21 continues Section 12.3 on strength hypotheses, presenting the second hypothesis: the Shear Stress Hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese), which characterizes material loading via maximum shear stress. The page also introduces analysis of the uniaxial tensile test as a comparison reference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d5e62261b2fe0f630bd1838e7722ab1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Spannungskreis für maximale Schubspannung (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Spannungskreis für maximale Schubspannung (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides a graphical representation of Mohr's Circle, illustrating the relationship between principal stresses ($\\sigma_1$, $\\sigma_2$) and maximum shear stress ($\\tau_{max}$). By showing $\\tau_{max}$ as the radius of the circle, it visually explains the core principle behind the Shear Stress Hypothesis ('Schubspannungshypothese'), a key strength theory in technical mechanics used to evaluate material safety under complex loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45fd05d7b64d8970b5e8dfb082686144</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Stress Formula (Tresca Criterion Foundation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress Formula (Tresca Criterion Foundation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines maximum shear stress as half the difference of principal stresses in 2D. Central to the Schubspannungshypothese (Tresca yield criterion), stating material yielding occurs when τ_max reaches a critical value from the uniaxial tensile test. Applies to ductile materials under plane stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc68552c03a8ae6ffa4a5e54ba28d474</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Stress State Condition for Equivalent Stress (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Stress State Condition for Equivalent Stress (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the uniaxial stress state ($\sigma_1 = \sigma_V, \sigma_2 = 0$) used as a reference case in the derivation of the Distortion Energy Hypothesis. It equates the first principal stress to the equivalent stress, allowing complex multiaxial stress states to be normalized against a simple uniaxial tensile test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9593db88a944b2e7681fcf49fd9c75e3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Stress Relation (Tresca Criterion Baseline Equation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress Relation (Tresca Criterion Baseline Equation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the maximum shear stress as half the equivalent uniaxial stress (σ_V), serving as the mathematical foundation for the shear stress hypothesis (Tresca yield criterion). It enables comparison between multi-axial plane stress states and the uniaxial tensile test, predicting yielding in ductile materials when τ_max reaches the critical shear stress threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-46eda68e7779f4d399b83a16b3d6937c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Strength Hypothesis Label (image)">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Strength Hypothesis Label (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a blue informational label featuring the name 'Tresca' and the year '(1864)'. It identifies the shear stress hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese), which is the second of three primary strength hypotheses being discussed in the context of engineering mechanics and material behavior under load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2217f8adb634d11ee86f76c3c3e282e4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Crystal Lattice Deformation under Shear Stress (image)">
  <data key="d0">Crystal Lattice Deformation under Shear Stress (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the microscopic response of a crystal lattice to shear stress ($\\tau$). It compares an undeformed state with elastic deformation (reversible skewing) and elastic-plastic deformation (permanent slip/dislocation). The visualization supports the discussion on strength hypotheses, specifically explaining how shear forces lead to material yielding and shape change at the atomic level.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d30d14f4292cc3776855a9cb0692a5ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 22 - Gestaltänderungsenergie Hypothesis Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 22 - Gestaltänderungsenergie Hypothesis Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 22 contains the mathematical derivation of the third strength hypothesis (Gestaltänderungsenergie/distortion energy), following pages 20-21 which introduced all three hypotheses. It details principal stresses, strains, internal energy decomposition, hydrostatic components, and the equivalent stress formulation critical for plastic failure analysis of ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff2032183a42f4515ef5b962c0dd242c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law (Principal Strain) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law (Principal Strain) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates principal strain ($\varepsilon_1$) using Young's modulus ($E$), Poisson's ratio ($\nu$), and principal stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$). In the surrounding context, it is used to express strain energy in terms of stress to isolate distortion energy for the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bed92fe83da461adf2c85ef6f9fd6aae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Principal Strain (Direction 2) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Principal Strain (Direction 2) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the principal normal strain $\varepsilon_2$ as a function of the three principal stresses, Young's modulus, and Poisson's ratio. It is a vital substitution step used to express strain energy purely in terms of stress to derive the distortion energy for yield criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6adef341db6034d0f39656b9c00457b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Principal Strain (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Principal Strain (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the principal strain $\varepsilon_2$ under a triaxial stress state using generalized Hooke's Law. In the surrounding context, it is used to substitute strains into the strain energy equation to isolate distortional energy and derive the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784994983</data>
</node>
<node id="Triaxial Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Triaxial Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the total internal strain energy per unit volume in an isotropic elastic material under triaxial principal stresses and strains. It serves as the foundational step for deriving the distortion energy (von Mises) failure criterion by isolating the shape-changing energy component.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784995187</data>
</node>
<node id="Triaxial Specific Strain Energy Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Triaxial Specific Strain Energy Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the total strain energy density in an isotropic elastic material under triaxial principal stresses. It serves as the fundamental relation to mathematically derive the distortion energy (Gestaltänderungsenergie) by subtracting the hydrostatic energy component, ultimately forming the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba0ddf9b856b58923806349e5db66ad7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress State Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress State Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the condition for a hydrostatic stress state, where all three principal stresses are equal. It defines the mean stress as the average of the principal stresses. In the context of failure analysis, this hydrostatic component is isolated and subtracted from the total stress because it only causes volume change, not plastic yielding (which is driven by distortional energy).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784995419</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the hydrostatic strain energy density ($U_h$), representing the portion of elastic energy causing pure volumetric change. It mathematically isolates the hydrostatic stress component, serving as a crucial subtractive step in deriving the von Mises yield criterion (Gestaltänderungsenergiehypothese) to determine the energy solely responsible for shape deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784996699</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Strain Energy Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the hydrostatic (volumetric) strain energy density of an isotropic elastic material under multi-axial principal stresses. It is a critical mathematical step in deriving the von Mises yield criterion, allowing for the isolation of shape-changing energy by subtracting volume-changing energy from the total internal energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784996834</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density (Hydrostatische Formänderungsenergie) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density (Hydrostatische Formänderungsenergie) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation gives the hydrostatic (volumetric) part of the elastic strain energy density in terms of principal stresses, Poisson's ratio, and Young's modulus. It is the key quantity subtracted from the total strain energy to obtain the distortional energy $U_f = U - U_h$, which governs yielding in the von Mises (Gestaltänderungsenergie) failure hypothesis for ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-611523cf1e65ad3018b889895899e924</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (Gestaltänderungsenergiehypothese) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (Gestaltänderungsenergiehypothese) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Despite severe OCR corruption, this equation represents the Von Mises yield criterion. It calculates the equivalent uniaxial stress from principal stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$), allowing engineers to predict yielding in complex multiaxial loading scenarios by comparing the result to a simple uniaxial tensile test limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfd627873f02a0655f53bad0e5b5f2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents a heavily corrupted OCR output of the von Mises yield criterion (Distortion Energy Hypothesis). It mathematically derives the equivalent uniaxial stress ($\sigma_V$) by isolating the distortion energy of a 3D stress state, providing a fundamental scalar metric used to predict plastic yielding in ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b7f0c95da5d44a04462b15e81c3e63b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the Distortion Energy Hypothesis (von Mises yield criterion). It calculates the equivalent uniaxial stress ($\sigma_v$) from three principal normal stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$) by equating the shape-changing distortion energy, enabling failure analysis for ductile materials under multi-axial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ecd108b80631edec1a231da6dff63ae7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Corrupted Angular Equality Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Corrupted Angular Equality Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An anomalous mathematical relation equating angles C1, B, and C2, heavily corrupted by OCR repetition. Contextually, it likely relates to the geometric orientation of stress or strain planes, such as those analyzed using Mohr's circle in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd945bd1f482970719a3376eb991b2d6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Section 12.3: Festigkeitshypothesen (header)">
  <data key="d0">Section 12.3: Festigkeitshypothesen (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">This header introduces 'Festigkeitshypothesen' (Strength Hypotheses), a core concept in technical mechanics. It contextualizes the evaluation of multi-axial material stress using criteria like shear stress and distortion energy. It connects theoretical stress-strain calculations to practical material failure analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1df1f79836d622c23adeb9481abc60d4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="von Mises (1883) Header (header)">
  <data key="d0">von Mises (1883) Header (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">This header credits Richard von Mises for the Distortion Energy Hypothesis discussed in the surrounding context. It introduces the mathematical derivation of the von Mises equivalent stress ($\sigma_v$), linking the concept of shape modification energy to the prediction of material yielding in complex stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae85ca066a1c8b0b3cc63158acf0f984</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
<node id="Gute Übereinstimmung bei duktilem Material (header)">
  <data key="d0">Gute Übereinstimmung bei duktilem Material (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Concluding header within the Festigkeitshypothesen section stating that shear stress and distortion energy hypotheses show good agreement with experimental results for ductile materials. Serves as validation of these failure criteria (Tresca and von Mises) specifically applicable to ductile material behavior, following detailed mathematical derivations of energy-based formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cbe88a94c68663b1d6151b78359af87</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784971017</data>
</node>
