<node id="Strength Hypotheses">
  <data key="d0">Strength Hypotheses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A key topic of the chapter, labeled as 'Festigkeitshypothesen' in the image.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Festigkeitshypothesen, these are theoretical frameworks for predicting material failure under complex stress states, of which the shear stress hypothesis is a specific example.&lt;SEP&gt;The theoretical framework (Festigkeitshypothesen) surrounding the decomposition of total internal energy into volumetric and distortional components to evaluate material failure and yielding.&lt;SEP&gt;Theoretical principles focused on within the surrounding context of engineering mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypotheses used for material stress assessment, covered in the summary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28d530475b93e04e7d6582c02926072c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68f46a2f336e00af32ab51674b243da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb6bcdf61d79257b9e57addd3e70b101</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998771</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Strain State">
  <data key="d0">Plane Strain State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A key topic of the chapter, labeled as 'Ebener Verzerrungszustand' in the image.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of strain defined as ebener Verzerrungszustand (EVZ).&lt;SEP&gt;A technical continuum mechanics concept, also referred to as ebener Verzerrungszustand or EVZ.&lt;SEP&gt;Abbreviated as EVZ (ebener Verzerrungszustand), a two-dimensional strain state defined by kinematic variables εx, εy, and γxy.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional state of strain (EVZ) characterized by strain components εx, εy, and γxy/2, relevant in continuum mechanics analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional strain state also known as EBener Verzerrungszustand or EVZ.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional deformation state analysis (ebener Verzerrungszustand) that is extended to full 3D problems using the displacement field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-28d530475b93e04e7d6582c02926072c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ebf6d69a9ef72ba2950206908d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9a516857538b0dc9b96defaf17bdc45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998771</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
