</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Strain Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Strain Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Infinitesimal Strain Tensor, also known as the Cauchy strain tensor, is a foundational mathematical construct in continuum mechanics used to characterize the three-dimensional strain state and describe the deformation of a continuous material. It is specifically formulated under the assumption of small displacements and small deformations, serving as the basis for understanding the linearized kinematics of deformation.

Mathematically, the tensor is derived through a linearization process and is defined as the symmetric part of the displacement gradient. This formulation relies on strain-displacement relations derived from the linearized displacement field. Specifically, it involves the partial differentiation of displacement field components, such as ∂u/∂x, ∂u/∂y, ∂v/∂x, and ∂v/∂y.

By capturing changes in side lengths (Seitenlängen) and angles (Winkel), the Infinitesimal Strain Tensor effectively measures geometric alterations within the material. The partial differentiation and linearization processes ultimately yield the normal strains and shear strains that comprehensively quantify the deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-873966c9766a35d08748403615a24d1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998965</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
