<node id="Engineering Shear Strain">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineering Shear Strain is a fundamental measure of shear deformation, characterizing the total angular change or distortion of an initially right angle within a deformed material element. Also known by the German term "Gleitung," it specifically represents the total reduction of an original right angle at a given vertex (such as P) or, geometrically, the angle change that relates to the rotation of a diagonal in a deformed unit square. 

Typically denoted by the Greek letter gamma ($\gamma$), specific components of this deformation are expressed using subscripts, such as $\gamma_{xy}$ or $\gamma_{yz}$. Expressed generally as $\gamma_{ij}$, it quantifies the total change in the right angle between two mutually perpendicular axes, $i$ and $j$, following deformation. Under the engineering convention, this shear strain component is directly proportional to the corresponding applied shear stress component.

A defining characteristic of Engineering Shear Strain is its mathematical relationship with the tensorial definition of strain. Engineering shear strain is exactly twice the tensorial strain component, a relationship expressed by the formula $\gamma_{xy} = 2\varepsilon_{xy}$. Consequently, when formulating the symmetric strain tensor—which is essential for executing accurate coordinate transformations—half of the engineering shear strain ($\gamma/2$) is utilized as the corresponding tensorial shear strain component.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90cfec891927772de90cde9aa609de48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aac206a8f9ab7199ba4ae6138d74c7ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999013</data>
