<node id="Horizontal Line Element Constraint in Strain Analysis (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element Constraint in Strain Analysis (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines a neighboring point relationship where the y-component differential is zero, constraining the line element PQ to be horizontal. It is used within a Taylor series framework to derive strain-displacement relations for infinitesimal deformation of a two-dimensional body (Scheibe) in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784972398</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation P -&gt; Q: dy = 0">
  <data key="d0">Equation P -&gt; Q: dy = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric constraint equation stating that the y-component of the differential position vector between two neighboring material points is zero, meaning the line element is oriented purely along the x-axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998965</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Point P">
  <data key="d0">Material Point P</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A neighboring material point within a deformable body that serves as the origin of a directed line element connecting to point Q.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific point in a solid body whose position coordinate is denoted by x, serving as the center for displacement field evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
