<node id="Taylor Series Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical approach used to express the displacement at a point based on the displacement of a neighboring point and its partial derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional, first-order mathematical approximation used to evaluate the displacement field at a neighboring point.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical series representation (Taylorreihe) used to approximate the displacement field of a neighboring point.&lt;SEP&gt;A foundational mathematical tool representing the one-dimensional expansion of a function f evaluated at a neighboring point y = x + dx, expressing it as an infinite sum involving the function's derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;The truncated multivariate expansion of a scalar field retaining first-order terms to linearize the function locally.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical method used for approximating function changes at a development point, employed to determine the displacements of various points.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical method (Taylorreihe) used to approximate small displacements around an evaluation point within the context of strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998965</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strain component derived from the one-dimensional approximation along the x-axis when dy equals zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998965</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear strain is a dimensionless engineering strain component and a fundamental concept within continuum mechanics that describes the mechanical deformation, displacement, or distortion of a material element under applied stress. Also known in German as Gleitung or Scherung, this multi-dimensional property is commonly denoted by the symbol γ or specifically γ_xy.

Conceptually, shear strain represents the angular deformation of a material, defined by the change in originally right angles within a deformed body. This angular distortion is often illustrated by the change in an original right angle at a specific point P, or it can be derived through the analysis of a horizontal line element where the change in the y-axis (dy) equals zero. The mechanical principles and geometric relationships defining shear strain, particularly its connection to normal strain, are frequently detailed and visually represented in technical schematic diagrams.

In mechanical analysis, the shear strain component (γ_xy) is intrinsically linked to shear stress through the material's shear modulus (G). It serves as a measure of shear deformation defined mathematically as the ratio of shear stress (τ or τ_xy) to the shear modulus (τ_xy / G). Within a plane stress system, this relationship represents one of the three coupled strain-stress relations found in the plane stress compliance matrix. Furthermore, shear strain is an essential component evaluated in plane stress and strain states, where its effects are mathematically combined and synthesized through Hooke's generalized law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9a516857538b0dc9b96defaf17bdc45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b68662a379adb00139d0404ab69c20e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999026</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation $u_Q = u_P + (\partial u/\partial x)dx + (\partial u/\partial y)dy$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $u_Q = u_P + (\partial u/\partial x)dx + (\partial u/\partial y)dy$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation derived using a Taylor series approach that expresses the displacement at point Q based on the displacement at point P and its partial derivatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Position Vector">
  <data key="d0">Differential Position Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector representing the position difference between two neighboring material points, whose y-component is zero under the specified condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Directed Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Directed Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material vector, denoted by the notation $P \to Q$, that physically connects point P to its neighboring point Q.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$\varepsilon_x = \partial u / \partial x$">
  <data key="d0">$\varepsilon_x = \partial u / \partial x$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical expression representing the normal strain component derived from the one-dimensional approximation along the x-axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Kinematics Of Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Linearized Kinematics Of Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical framework or study in continuum mechanics that is understood by constructing the infinitesimal strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bivariate First-Order Taylor Expansion of the Displacement Field (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Bivariate First-Order Taylor Expansion of the Displacement Field (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order multivariate Taylor series expansion of the displacement function $u(x,y)$, used in continuum mechanics to relate the displacement of a neighboring point to the displacement and its gradients at a reference point. It is the foundational kinematic equation for deriving the linearized strain tensor under the small-deformation assumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784972512</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Gradient">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Gradient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represented by partial derivatives, it denotes the local rates of change of the displacement component in the x- and y-directions.&lt;SEP&gt;The partial derivative of the displacement with respect to the spatial coordinate, describing the rate of change of the displacement.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical quantity essential for continuum mechanics, derived from the Taylor series framework bridging geometric deformation observations with analytical formulation.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical operator applied to the displacement vector field, whose symmetric part yields the strain tensor components.&lt;SEP&gt;The spatial derivative of the displacement field, from which strain is derived as a normalized deformation measure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4977643a568de41c3595cee58107e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rotation Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Rotation Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensor derived from the antisymmetric part of the displacement gradient that captures rigid-body rotations.&lt;SEP&gt;A tensor defined by the antisymmetric part of the displacement gradient tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as ω, the antisymmetric part of the Jacobian of the deformation gradient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometrically Linear Theory">
  <data key="d0">Geometrically Linear Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theory assuming small deformations where higher-order terms of infinitesimal increments are neglected.&lt;SEP&gt;A theory yielded by neglecting higher-order terms, providing the mathematical bridge between kinematic description of motion and engineering strain measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain State Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Strain State Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The analysis of the deformation and strain (Verzerrungszustand) of a body, which is essential for formulating constitutive laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Referred to as Verzerrungszustand, it is a field of analysis within continuum mechanics involving the use of Taylor expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Verzerrungszustand, the continuum mechanics context in which this linearization is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Vector Field">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Vector Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector field representing the displacements within a deformable body, of which u(x,y) denotes a component.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as u(P), relates the displacement of a material point to an adjacent point through a first-order Taylor expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;A vector field representing the displacement of material points in a deformable body, described by the equation r(x,y,z) = u(x,y,z) * e_x + v(x,y,z) * e_y + w(x,y,z) * e_{z_i}.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-dimensional Disk">
  <data key="d0">Two-dimensional Disk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific type of two-dimensional deformable body, referred to as a Scheibe, whose displacement field is analyzed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Measures">
  <data key="d0">Strain Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quantitative measures of length changes and deformation derived from the displacement gradient tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantitative indicators used to describe the deformation of a continuous body, derived from displacement gradients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement Field (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion of Displacement Field (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation is the 2D Taylor series expansion of the displacement component v(x,y), used to approximate the displacement of a neighboring point (x+dx, y+dy). Under small deformation assumptions, higher-order terms are neglected, yielding the linearized displacement field fundamental to deriving the strain tensor in continuum mechanics and the analysis of Verzerrungszustand (strain state).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784972622</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical tool (Taylorreihe) used in continuum mechanics to linearize displacement fields, assuming small deformations and neglecting higher-order terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784998989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component v(x,y)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component v(x,y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of the displacement field that is expanded around an evaluation point to determine the displacement of a neighboring point.</data>
