<node id="Normal Strains">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strains</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal Strains represent a fundamental measure of deformation, specifically denoting changes in length (Längenänderungen) and relative displacement or extension perpendicular to a material's cross-section. Computed from the displacement gradient tensor, they constitute the diagonal elements of the strain tensor matrix along the x, y, and z axes. When evaluated within the principal axes system, these strains align with the principal directions and are inherently connected to the shear strain angle.

Also known by the German term "Dehnungen," Normal Strains are particularly significant in the thermomechanical behavior of isotropic materials. They can be produced exclusively by thermal loading, manifesting as deformations induced by temperature changes during thermal expansion. Furthermore, in the presence of an externally applied force, these perpendicular strains are notably affected by thermal terms, further defining their role in evaluating a material's structural and thermal response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddbe1099c718b59c4f95b165b71f72ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strains">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strains</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear strains are fundamental components in continuum mechanics and structural analysis that specifically represent angular distortions or changes in angles within a material. Mathematically, these strains are established and computed directly from the displacement gradient tensor. In a three-dimensional system, specific shear strain components—commonly denoted as γ_xy, γ_yz, and γ_xz—act in conjunction with normal strains to comprehensively characterize the complete three-dimensional strain state of a body. Additionally, shear strains can manifest and be analyzed as resulting from the evaluation of coupled deformations when a material is examined within a rotated coordinate system.

In the context of structural analysis and applied mechanics, shear strains quantify deformations that occur parallel to an applied force, a property often calculated utilizing the material's shear modulus. Also referred to by the German term "Gleitungen," shear strains possess distinct thermomechanical characteristics. Most notably, in isotropic materials, shear strains are entirely independent of thermal effects; they are not produced by temperature changes, thermal expansion, or general thermal loading. Because thermal terms do not induce angular distortion in these materials, shear strains remain strictly driven by mechanical forces rather than temperature fluctuations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bc48465db9a039c612fe9cd175c5ccc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
