<node id="First-Order Taylor Series Expansion">
  <data key="d0">First-Order Taylor Series Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical method used to evaluate small displacements at a point over a distance, approximating the value of a function using its current value plus its rate of change.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical procedure used to approximate the deformed length by evaluating the displacement function around a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal strain is a dimensionless physical quantity and a fundamental measure of deformation representing the relative elongation or contraction of a material. In a one-dimensional context, it quantifies how much an infinitesimal segment stretches or compresses. Mathematically, it is defined as the local rate of change of the displacement field—such as in the y-direction—and is calculated as the ratio of the change in length to the original length of a material element under uniaxial loading. This geometric deformation is often expressed and analyzed via differential terms.

Commonly represented by the Greek letter epsilon ($\varepsilon$), normal strain is used to specify dimensionless longitudinal deformations along given directions. Components of normal strain, such as $\varepsilon_x$ and $\varepsilon_y$, denote the elongation or contraction of an element's sides along the x- and y-axes. For instance, it can represent horizontal contraction or the strain in the principal directions of a square element subjected to biaxial loading. Fundamentally, the variable $\varepsilon$ indicates the longitudinal strain—sometimes referred to by the German term *Dehnung*—along a principal direction.

Beyond basic geometric deformation, normal strain is a critical mechanical property interrelated with complex stress states and other strain types. It possesses a distinct geometric relationship with shear strain, which is frequently illustrated in technical schematic diagrams. When evaluating a biaxial stress state, normal strain in a specific direction (e.g., $\varepsilon_x$) can be calculated as a function of applied normal stresses utilizing the generalized Hooke's Law. Furthermore, normal strain components can be extremized under coordinate rotation to determine the principal directions of deformation within the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b68662a379adb00139d0404ab69c20e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999086</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
