</node>
<node id="Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation represents the Taylor series expansion of a function $f(y)$ around an evaluation point $y_0$. In the context of continuum mechanics, it is used to approximate the displacement field of neighboring points in a deformable body. By truncating higher-order terms under the assumption of small deformations, it enables the linearization of the strain tensor, which is fundamental for analyzing the state of strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784973796</data>
</node>
<node id="f(y)">
  <data key="d0">f(y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The target function being approximated by the Taylor Series, which represents the spatial displacement field or a component of the displacement vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="y">
  <data key="d0">y</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The independent spatial variable representing the position of a neighboring point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="y_0">
  <data key="d0">y_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The expansion point, acting as the reference point in the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="f^{(n)}(y_0)">
  <data key="d0">f^{(n)}(y_0)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The n-th order derivative of the target function evaluated at the reference point y_0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="n!">
  <data key="d0">n!</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The factorial of n, serving as the normalizing denominator for the series terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="(y - y_0)^n">
  <data key="d0">(y - y_0)^n</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The distance to the n-th power, representing the spatial offset from the reference point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State Of Strain">
  <data key="d0">State Of Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The condition of deformation experienced by a continuous material.&lt;SEP&gt;A core concept in continuum mechanics, also known as 'Verzerrungszustand', describing the deformation of a material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearization Process">
  <data key="d0">Linearization Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The foundational step of keeping only f(y_0) and f'(y_0)(y - y_0) terms, truncating higher-order terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinite Summation">
  <data key="d0">Infinite Summation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation, denoted by the symbol sum, employed by the Taylor Series equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher-order Differentiation">
  <data key="d0">Higher-order Differentiation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation employed by the Taylor Series equation to calculate derivatives of various orders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Factorial Computation">
  <data key="d0">Factorial Computation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation employed by the Taylor Series equation to calculate the normalizing denominator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polynomial Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Polynomial Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation employed by the Taylor Series equation to represent a function as a sum of polynomial terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaluation Point">
  <data key="d0">Evaluation Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The point at which the function is evaluated or approximated, corresponding to y_0 in the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Auswertungspunkt, the theoretical base point necessary for applying Taylor series approximations to find the displaced position of neighboring points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Stability">
  <data key="d0">Material Stability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical condition of a continuous material remaining stable and secure, which is practically ensured by calculating stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2e8d2b1a89f6cabb7e72a9e7705515dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference Configuration Identity Mapping (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Reference Configuration Identity Mapping (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">In 1D continuum mechanics, $y_0 = x$ establishes the undeformed reference state by equating a point's initial spatial position ($y_0$) to its material coordinate ($x$). This baseline geometry is essential for defining displacement vectors and applying Taylor series to analyze small deformations and strain states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784973979</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation Y_0 = X">
  <data key="d0">Equation Y_0 = X</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the undeformed reference configuration for a one-dimensional continuous medium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Y_0">
  <data key="d0">Y_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial coordinate or position of a material point in the undeformed, initial reference state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X">
  <data key="d0">X</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Lagrangian or material coordinate, serving as a unique label identifying a specific point within the continuous body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Undeformed Reference Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Undeformed Reference Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The baseline geometry of a body before any external forces or deformations are applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field U(x)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field U(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field representing the difference between the current deformed position y(x) and the initial position x.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed Position Y(x)">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Position Y(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The current position of a material point after deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2baa9c55366fde92e0ba418495c7bae0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Position Relationship for Strain Analysis (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Position Relationship for Strain Analysis (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Fundamental kinematic equation in continuum mechanics describing the position of a neighboring point y relative to reference point x via infinitesimal displacement dx. It underpins linearized strain theory for small deformations and connects directly to Taylor series approximation of displacement fields, forming the basis for strain tensor derivation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784974044</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation y = x + dx">
  <data key="d0">Equation y = x + dx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation describing the positional relationship between a point x and an infinitesimally adjacent point y.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Position Vector x">
  <data key="d0">Position Vector x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector representing the position of a reference material point in the continuum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Displacement Vector dx">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Displacement Vector dx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector denoting an infinitesimal displacement or a differential line element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Position Vector y">
  <data key="d0">Position Vector y</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector representing the position of the neighboring point after deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Strain Theory">
  <data key="d0">Linearized Strain Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theory for small deformations (kleine Deformationen) describing the kinematic relationship between points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-dimensional Extension Equation">
  <data key="d0">Multi-dimensional Extension Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation represented as dy = dx + (∂u/∂x)dx that generalizes the one-dimensional analysis to multiple dimensions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Vector Field u">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Vector Field u</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector field, denoted as u, representing the displacement in the multi-dimensional extension equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Relationships">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Relationships</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical descriptions in solid mechanics relating displacement gradients to strain measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Element Formulations">
  <data key="d0">Finite Element Formulations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Computational methods and mathematical foundations used for solving complex engineering problems.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational method that utilizes the engineering strain measures derived from this linearization.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical formulations critical for numerical simulation and analysis, relying on fundamental constitutive equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A numerical technique for structural analysis that relies on the relationships derived from plane stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Volume Elements">
  <data key="d0">Differential Volume Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infinitesimal volumes within a continuous material, experiencing changes in side lengths and angles during deformation.&lt;SEP&gt;Small geometric volumes whose sides and angles are altered by small displacements in continuous media.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Side Lengths And Angles">
  <data key="d0">Side Lengths And Angles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Geometric properties of differential volume elements (Seitenlänge und Winkel) that change during deformation and are quantified by strain tensors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement Field Approximation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">One-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion for Displacement Field Approximation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This Taylor series expansion of f(x+dx) about point x serves as the mathematical foundation for linearizing displacement fields in continuum mechanics. By truncating higher-order terms under the small deformation assumption, it enables derivation of the strain tensor from displacement gradients, connecting point-wise displacements to the strain state (Verzerrungszustand) of a deformable body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784974132</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field u(x)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field u(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of a displacement vector field typically represented by f(x), describing the displacement in the neighborhood of a material point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearization">
  <data key="d0">Linearization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A simplification process for small deformations that reduces the Taylor series to a first-order approximation by neglecting terms of order (dx)² and higher.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of simplifying equations by truncating higher-order terms, central to the formulation of the infinitesimal strain tensor under small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Strain Tensor ε_ij">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Strain Tensor ε_ij</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensor defined by the symmetric part of the displacement gradient tensor, describing the continuous strain field governing deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function F">
  <data key="d0">Function F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function evaluated at a neighboring point and represented by f(x), typically denoting a component of the displacement vector field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Position Coordinate X">
  <data key="d0">Position Coordinate X</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable that denotes the position coordinate of a material point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Separation Dx">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Separation Dx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the infinitesimal separation to a neighboring point, acting as the increment in the Taylor series expansion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First-Order Bivariate Taylor Series Expansion (Linearization of Scalar Field) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">First-Order Bivariate Taylor Series Expansion (Linearization of Scalar Field) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation is the first-order Taylor polynomial of a bivariate function f(x,y) expanded about (x₀,y₀). In the surrounding continuum mechanics context on strain state (Verzerrungszustand), it serves as the mathematical basis for linearizing displacement fields of neighboring material points under the small-deformation assumption, ultimately yielding the infinitesimal strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784974219</data>
</node>
<node id="Scalar Field">
  <data key="d0">Scalar Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as f(x, y), the mathematical function that is linearized around a specific development point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Derivative">
  <data key="d0">Partial Derivative</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as ∂f/∂x and ∂f/∂y, these are the components of the gradient vector at the expansion point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gradient Vector">
  <data key="d0">Gradient Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as ∇f, composed of partial derivatives at the expansion point (x₀, y₀).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hessian Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Hessian Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents subsequent second- and higher-order terms in the Taylor expansion, involving mixed and pure second partial derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;A matrix formed by second partial derivatives related to the curvature of the displacement field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Strain Cauchy Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Small-Strain Cauchy Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The tensor derived from the first-order Taylor expansion of displacement components, assuming small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacobian">
  <data key="d0">Jacobian</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Jacobian of the deformation gradient, which is split into a symmetric strain tensor and an antisymmetric rotation tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Coordinates">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Coordinates</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variables x and y representing the spatial coordinates of the evaluation point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expansion Point">
  <data key="d0">Expansion Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The point (x₀, y₀) about which the scalar field is expanded or developed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The general coefficient 1/n! for n-th order terms in the Taylor series, corresponding to denominators like 1!.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformation Gradient">
  <data key="d0">Deformation Gradient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The gradient of the deformation, whose Jacobian is split into strain and rotation tensors.&lt;SEP&gt;A local measure of deformation for which the line element PQ is essential in defining it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order Terms of Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order Terms of Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the quadratic terms of a 2D Taylor series expansion around point (x₀, y₀), comprising pure second partial derivatives (∂²f/∂x², ∂²f/∂y²) scaled by 1/2! and the mixed partial derivative (∂²f/∂x∂y). In strain analysis context, these terms characterize curvature of the displacement field, essential for deriving strain tensors under small deformation assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784974554</data>
</node>
<node id="Second-order Taylor Series Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Second-order Taylor Series Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the quadratic portion of the multivariate Taylor polynomial capturing the curvature information of a function at an evaluation point, fundamental to continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalar Field f(x, y)">
  <data key="d0">Scalar Field f(x, y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function expanded using the Taylor series around a development point, often representing a component of the displacement vector field in strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Vector Field u(P)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Vector Field u(P)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical field of a material point P, typically represented by the scalar field f(x, y) in strain state analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kleine Deformationen">
  <data key="d0">Kleine Deformationen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German term for small deformations, an assumption that justifies truncating the Taylor series after the second-order terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Strain Theory">
  <data key="d0">Finite Strain Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theory in continuum mechanics dealing with large deformations, where the Green-Lagrange strain tensor is utilized.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Partial Derivative ∂²f/∂x²">
  <data key="d0">Pure Partial Derivative ∂²f/∂x²</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A second-order derivative representing changes in side lengths, weighted by squared displacement in a specific direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mixed Partial Derivative ∂²f/(∂x∂y)">
  <data key="d0">Mixed Partial Derivative ∂²f/(∂x∂y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A second-order derivative representing coupling between directions, encoding changes in angles or shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Tensor Components">
  <data key="d0">Strain Tensor Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elements derived from the symmetric part of the displacement gradient, capturing changes in side lengths and angles.&lt;SEP&gt;The set of strain measures (εx, εy, γxy) defining the strain state of a continuum, derived from the displacement field through kinematic relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress-Strain Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Strain Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental relationship in continuum mechanics whose derivation utilizes principles like the Taylor expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;The mathematical connection between applied stress and resulting strain, which when combined with the geometric equation yields the classic formula for isotropic elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73f6cf7471ec2d0c8d45890d1117b1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Differential Element Shear Strain Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">2D Differential Element Shear Strain Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram illustrates the geometric derivation of 2D shear strain ($\\gamma_{xy}$) by showing how a differential square element $PQRS$ deforms into a general quadrilateral $P'Q'R'S'$. It uses first-order Taylor series expansions to map the displacements of vertices $Q$ and $S$ based on the displacement of vertex $P$. The visual explicitly defines engineering shear strain as the reduction of the original right angle, calculated as the sum of the angular tilts $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784975262</data>
</node>
<node id="Undeformed Element PQRS">
  <data key="d0">Undeformed Element PQRS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical square representing the original state of a material element before displacement, with horizontal and vertical side lengths dx and dy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed Element P'Q'R'S'">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Element P'Q'R'S'</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A distorted quadrilateral representing the positions of the original points P, Q, R, and S after displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angular Change Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Angular Change Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle denoted as alpha between the deformed vertical edge P'S' and the original vertical orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angular Change Beta">
  <data key="d0">Angular Change Beta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle denoted as beta between the deformed horizontal edge P'Q' and the original horizontal orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Displacement v">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Displacement v</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vertical component of the movement of point P to its new position P', serving as the base variable for Taylor series expansion of other points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scherung">
  <data key="d0">Scherung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A German term used as an alternative name for the total engineering shear strain in the context of the deformation diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First-Order Taylor Approximation of Deformed Segment Length (equation)">
  <data key="d0">First-Order Taylor Approximation of Deformed Segment Length (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation uses a first-order Taylor series expansion to approximate the deformed length of an infinitesimal line element. It demonstrates that the new length equals the original length dy plus the differential displacement variation, thereby forming the foundational step for deriving the infinitesimal normal strain in the y-direction within continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784975383</data>
</node>
<node id="P'S' Line Segment">
  <data key="d0">P'S' Line Segment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The length of the line segment connecting points P' and S' in the deformed configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V Displacement Component">
  <data key="d0">V Displacement Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The displacement component in the y-direction of the reference point P, which cancels out when determining the deformed length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Linear Theory">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Linear Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical framework postulating that higher-order terms from the Taylor series are negligible, based on the small deformation assumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Displacements (kleine Verschiebungen)">
  <data key="d0">Small Displacements (kleine Verschiebungen)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Small movements that alter the sides and angles of differential volume elements within the context of geometric linear theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Series (Taylorreihe)">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series (Taylorreihe)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An abstract mathematical concept used to transition from 1D function approximation to multidimensional deformation analysis by evaluating displacement at a distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f3dea07a2a72e1fd56bdd66b98441fc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component ε_y - Kinematic Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component ε_y - Kinematic Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal strain in the y-direction as the partial derivative of the y-displacement component with respect to y-coordinate (ε_y = ∂v/∂y). Part of the infinitesimal strain tensor in continuum mechanics, relating displacement fields to deformation under small strain assumptions. Connects to Taylor series approximations for small displacements discussed in the broader context.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784975487</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Epsilon Y">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Epsilon Y</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain component in the y-direction within infinitesimal strain theory, defined as the partial derivative of the displacement field component v with respect to the y-coordinate.&lt;SEP&gt;The normal strain ε_y defined as ∂v/∂y, which is significantly smaller than 1 (≪ 1) under the small-deformation assumption, allowing simplification of the geometric equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Strain Theory">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Strain Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A framework used in continuum mechanics for discussing strain states under small deformations, also referred to as Verzerrungszustand.&lt;SEP&gt;A theory assuming strains are infinitesimally small, allowing certain terms to be neglected in equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical theory assuming small strains where geometric nonlinearities are neglected to linearize the strain tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;Theoretical framework for analyzing small deformations where the shear strain equation applies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component V">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component V</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of the displacement field whose partial derivative with respect to the y-coordinate defines the normal strain in that direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement field component v representing displacement in the y direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement field component in the y direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain-displacement Relations">
  <data key="d0">Strain-displacement Relations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinematic equations that connect displacements to strains and construct the infinitesimal strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Points">
  <data key="d0">Material Points</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Notations (P, S and P', S') referring to points before and after deformation, used to define original and deformed lengths.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Epsilon X">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Epsilon X</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain component in the x-direction, defined as the partial derivative of the displacement field component u with respect to the x-coordinate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Epsilon Z">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Epsilon Z</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain component in the z-direction, defined as the partial derivative of the displacement field component w with respect to the z-coordinate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Strain State">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Strain State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The comprehensive characterization of deformation in a material, completed by combining normal and shear strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as räumlicher Verformungszustand, this is the framework describing the complete state of deformation in a three-dimensional object.&lt;SEP&gt;A spatial state of strain (räumlicher Verzerrungszustand) that is produced by a two-dimensional plane stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain state in three dimensions (raümlicher Verzerrungszustand) produced by a plane stress state, including strain in the z-direction</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component U">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component U</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The component of the displacement field in the x-direction, whose partial derivative defines the normal strain epsilon_x.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement field component u representing displacement in the x direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement field component in the x direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component W">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component W</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The component of the displacement field in the z-direction, whose partial derivative defines the normal strain epsilon_z.&lt;SEP&gt;The displacement field component in the z-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Horizontal Line Segment PQ (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Horizontal Line Segment PQ (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematically defines an infinitesimal horizontal line segment PQ in the undeformed state by setting its differential y-component ($dy=0$) to zero. This acts as the foundational geometric constraint for deriving normal and shear strains under small deformation continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784975726</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation P Q: d y = 0">
  <data key="d0">Equation P Q: d y = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation that establishes the geometric constraints of an infinitesimal line segment connecting points P and Q within a two-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system, specifying that the differential change in the y-coordinate (dy) along the segment PQ is strictly zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain In X-direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain In X-direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The change in side length along the x-direction, which is analyzed independently using the unidirectional line element defined by the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Represents the normal strain in the x-direction (ε_x), calculated as (1/E)(σ_x - ν·σ_y), which is one of the three coupled strain-stress relations in the plane stress compliance matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Angle Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Shear Angle Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular change of horizontal lines that contributes to shear; combined with Shear Angle Beta to define the engineering shear strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Angle Beta">
  <data key="d0">Shear Angle Beta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular change of vertical lines that, when combined with Shear Angle Alpha, defines the engineering shear strain gamma_xy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multivariate Taylor Series">
  <data key="d0">Multivariate Taylor Series</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical approximation technique (Taylorreihe) applied to the displacement field to allow derivation of the linearized strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Gamma_xy">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Gamma_xy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The combined angular changes alpha and beta, defined mathematically as gamma_xy = alpha + beta.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Body">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Body</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A body of continuous material that contains neighboring material points such as P and Q in its reference, undeformed configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference Undeformed Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Reference Undeformed Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The initial, undeformed state of the continuous body where the geometric constraints of the line segment PQ are established.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A unidirectional line element along the x-axis, implied by the condition that dy = 0 and indicating zero differential change in the y-coordinate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A corresponding line element defined by the constraint dx = 0, used in conjunction with the horizontal line element to evaluate angular changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Change In Y-coordinate">
  <data key="d0">Differential Change In Y-coordinate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as dy, this value along the segment PQ is strictly zero according to the mathematical mapping condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a2dbfa0f426be66876905754d3b98be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page 6 - Verzerrungszustand (Strain State Analysis) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 6 - Verzerrungszustand (Strain State Analysis) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 6 located at bottom of page in a continuum mechanics document. The page covers Chapter 12.1 'Verzerrungszustand', continuing strain state analysis with angle changes (γ_xy = α + β) and shear strain concepts. Part of a sequence building from Taylor series foundations to multi-dimensional deformation analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784975813</data>
</node>
