</node>
<node id="Angular Changes">
  <data key="d0">Angular Changes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Angular changes denoted as α and β, representing components of strain analysis that contribute to the total angular change in multi-dimensional systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Angular Change">
  <data key="d0">Total Angular Change</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The sum of angular changes α and β, expressed as γ_xy = α + β, representing the total angular deformation in strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-dimensional Strain Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Multi-dimensional Strain Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of analyzing strain in multi-dimensional systems, focusing on angular changes, total angular change, and shear strain concepts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Displacement Considerations">
  <data key="d0">Small Displacement Considerations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concept in continuum mechanics involving the analysis of small displacements, serving as an intermediate step between Taylor series foundations and multi-dimensional strain analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Engineering Lecture Material">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Engineering Lecture Material</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The broader educational document or context containing the continuum mechanics information, cross-referenced by page numbers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b5c4f815450e226a7f0b65824388cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Series Approximation">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Series Approximation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical method used for formulating small deformations and shear strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angular Change α">
  <data key="d0">Angular Change α</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular change that, along with β, results in shear strain γ_xy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angular Change β">
  <data key="d0">Angular Change β</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular change that, along with α, results in shear strain γ_xy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c76116704bed38bc28197f2328c0a8b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Derivation of Plane Strain Components (image)">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Derivation of Plane Strain Components (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a geometric derivation for the plane strain state (EVZ) of a differential element. It visually defines normal strains (changes in side length $dx, dy$) and shear strain ($\\gamma_{xy}$) resulting from a displacement field ($u, v$). By showing how a square PQRS deforms into P'Q'R'S', the image grounds the abstract kinematic relations and strain tensor components discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784976014</data>
</node>
<node id="Undeformed State">
  <data key="d0">Undeformed State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The initial configuration of the material element, represented by a square labeled PQRS with side lengths dx and dy.&lt;SEP&gt;The original shape of the material element before being subjected to load, represented by a solid black square.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed State">
  <data key="d0">Deformed State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The final configuration of the material element after deformation, represented by a quadrilateral labeled P'Q'R'S'.&lt;SEP&gt;The skewed shape of the material element after being subjected to load, represented by a light green filled parallelogram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angle Beta">
  <data key="d0">Angle Beta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle representing the tilt of the originally horizontal side PQ, related to the gradient $\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Expansions">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Expansions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order mathematical expansions used to describe the displacements of the neighboring vertices of the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point R">
  <data key="d0">Point R</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A vertex of the undeformed square material element PQRS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77038968edb0f63a8763c004d187bda5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Line Element PQ for Strain Derivation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Line Element PQ for Strain Derivation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation $PQ: dy = 0$ specifies a differential line element PQ oriented parallel to the x-axis. It serves as the geometric starting point for deriving the normal strain $\varepsilon_x$ and the rotational component $\alpha$ of the shear strain $\gamma_{xy}$ in the kinematic strain-displacement relations of continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784976106</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation PQ: dy = 0">
  <data key="d0">Equation PQ: dy = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation defining a differential line element PQ within a two-dimensional continuum oriented parallel to the x-axis, where the differential change in the y-coordinate is zero, serving as the geometric basis for deriving strain tensor components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Line Element PQ">
  <data key="d0">Line Element PQ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A differential line element within a two-dimensional continuum, initially aligned with the x-direction, used to analyze axial deformation and compute normal strain, essential for defining the local deformation gradient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain εx">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain εx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial deformation measure computed by examining the change in length of line element PQ after deformation, equal to the partial derivative of displacement u with respect to x (∂u/∂x).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain γxy">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain γxy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shear strain component equal to the sum of angular rotations α and β, representing the total shear distortion in the xy-plane.&lt;SEP&gt;A shear strain component in strain analysis, appearing off-diagonal in the 2D strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field u(x, y)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field u(x, y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A displacement field component mapping the undeformed configuration to the deformed configuration in the x-direction, used in computing strain components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field v(x, y)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field v(x, y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A displacement field component mapping the undeformed configuration to the deformed configuration in the y-direction, used in computing strain components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Strain State (EVZ)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Strain State (EVZ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A state of plane strain that utilizes the symmetric strain tensor formulated from line element analysis and kinematic deformation relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;A strain state defined by strain components εx, εy, and γxy in two-dimensional continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Component α">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Component α</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shear strain component representing the angular rotation of the line element PQ, which is a part of the total shear strain γxy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Component β">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Component β</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shear strain component that, together with α, makes up the total shear strain γxy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain εy">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain εy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A normal strain component representing the axial deformation in the y-direction, forming part of the strain tensor components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Strain-displacement Relations">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Strain-displacement Relations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Relationships connecting deformation kinematics to the continuum's strain state, derived using geometric constraints like the equation PQ: dy = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symmetric Strain Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Symmetric Strain Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensor formulated using the line element PQ and used in the plane strain state (EVZ).&lt;SEP&gt;A tensor whose off-diagonal elements are populated by shear strain values.&lt;SEP&gt;A tensor describing material deformation, from which the normal strain component is derived as a diagonal element.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical construct in continuum mechanics that uses half the engineering shear strain (ε_xy = γ/2) as its shear component, which is essential for coordinate transformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-dimensional Continuum">
  <data key="d0">Two-dimensional Continuum</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical space within which the differential line element PQ is defined and analyzed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-623de8f24fdeee5720ee7518dc88dbb2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Derivation of Angular Strain (Small Deformations) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Derivation of Angular Strain (Small Deformations) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation derives the angular rotation $\\alpha$ from the displacement field components $u$ and $v$ using geometric relationships. By applying the infinitesimal strain approximation ($\\varepsilon_y \\ll 1$), it simplifies to $\\alpha = \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}$, which is fundamental for defining the shear strain components in the strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784976294</data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Derivation Equation">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Derivation Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A LaTeX equation detailing the geometric derivation of the rotational angle component α under the assumption of infinitesimal small deformations, demonstrating the simplification from tan(α) to ∂u/∂y.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rotational Angle Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Rotational Angle Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A rotational angle component α derived from the displacement field. Under the small-deformation assumption, it rigorously simplifies to α = ∂u/∂y.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rotational Angle Beta">
  <data key="d0">Rotational Angle Beta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A second rotational angle component β that, together with α, sums to form the total shear strain γ_xy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Gamma Xy">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Gamma Xy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total shear strain γ_xy (also called Gleitung), defined as the sum of two rotational angles (α + β), which defines the off-diagonal components of the planar strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Planar Strain Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Planar Strain Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensor whose off-diagonal components are defined by the total shear strain γ_xy, relating macroscopic deformations to the displacement field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X Direction">
  <data key="d0">X Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial direction in which the displacement field component u acts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Y Direction">
  <data key="d0">Y Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial direction in which the displacement field component v acts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric line element that undergoes deformation, characterized by an original and deformed length.&lt;SEP&gt;An infinitely small geometric line originally oriented along the x-axis, used to physically describe the fractional change in length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformed Length">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The length of the infinitesimal line element after deformation, represented by the denominator dy + ∂v/∂y dy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Macroscopic Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Macroscopic Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Large-scale deformations that are related to the underlying displacement field via the planar strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2deea6eaa78cd0a482432f78f4310b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Deformation Angular Approximation (Shear Strain Component Beta) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Small Deformation Angular Approximation (Shear Strain Component Beta) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation derives the rotational angle $\beta$ of a line element in the x-y plane. By applying the small deformation assumption, it linearizes $\tan \beta$ to show that $\beta$ equals the partial derivative of the y-displacement $v$ with respect to x, which is essential for formulating the shear strain $\gamma_{xy}$ in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784977048</data>
</node>
<node id="Beta">
  <data key="d0">Beta</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The rotation angle of a line element initially parallel to the x-axis, representing angular deformation in the planar strain state.&lt;SEP&gt;An angle component that, together with Alpha, contributes to the total change in an initially right angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma XY">
  <data key="d0">Gamma XY</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The engineering shear strain calculated as the sum of partial derivatives of u with respect to y and v with respect to x.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Linearization">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Linearization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of simplifying angular deformation equations under the assumption of small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Planar Strain State">
  <data key="d0">Planar Strain State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as ebener Verzerrungszustand (EVZ), a state in continuum mechanics where deformation occurs in a plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First-order Taylor Approximation">
  <data key="d0">First-order Taylor Approximation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical technique used to approximate the tangent of beta as approximately beta for small angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric element initially parallel to the x-axis whose rotation is measured in the planar strain state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Projection">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Projection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The term representing the partial derivative of the displacement field component v with respect to x, multiplied by dx, which forms the numerator in the tangent of beta.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Projection">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Projection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The term representing dx plus the partial derivative of the displacement field component u with respect to x, multiplied by dx, which forms the denominator in the tangent of beta.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-24d9eb0ca31bb4557fd92f27b7b1a466</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dimensionally Inconsistent Angle Expression (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Dimensionally Inconsistent Angle Expression (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically invalid and circular equation equating degrees with a fraction of radians over degrees, likely an OCR error. Within the continuum mechanics context, it relates to the geometric calculation of angular changes and the necessary conversion from degrees to radians for evaluating shear strains ($\\gamma_{xy}$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784977203</data>
</node>
<node id="Garbled Mathematical Equation">
  <data key="d0">Garbled Mathematical Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically inconsistent and circular identity equating angular measurements in degrees to fractional expressions with radians, likely an artifact of a scanning or transcription glitch.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="160 Degrees">
  <data key="d0">160 Degrees</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular measurement in degrees present on the left-hand and right-hand sides of the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="60 Degrees">
  <data key="d0">60 Degrees</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An angular measurement in degrees found in the denominator of the equation's fractional expression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="6 Pi">
  <data key="d0">6 Pi</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical value representing an angle in radians, found in the numerator of the equation's fractional expression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pi">
  <data key="d0">Pi</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical constant whose presence in the equation underscores the necessity of converting physical angles from degrees into radians.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5b74b4caf274c8a818b5e7d868904b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
