<node id="Principal Strains">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strains</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Strains are defined as the maximum and minimum normal strains occurring at a specific point within a material. Mathematically, they represent the eigenvalues of the strain tensor. These specific strain values act along the principal directions and correspond directly to the principal stress directions. In German literature and technical terminology, Principal Strains are commonly referred to as "Hauptdehnungen."

The determination of Principal Strains is fundamentally achieved through the coordinate transformation of the strain tensor. This strain transformation process is typically calculated using matrix algebra and trigonometric functions. Furthermore, Principal Strains are analyzed as a mechanical property using transformation equations and can be graphically determined using Mohr's circle. 

In academic and engineering contexts, Principal Strains are a key component in the study of strain states, particularly in 2D strain analysis. This topic is a central focus in higher education lecture content and references the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2," specifically discussed in the context of strain analysis (such as on page 8). Beyond theoretical analysis, the strains ($\varepsilon$) corresponding to the principal directions hold practical applications, notably being substituted into internal energy equations alongside the hydrostatic stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3ee9641e5bb1298b56301c99bff5e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
