<node id="Mohr's Strain Circle">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Strain Circle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohr's Strain Circle is a graphical method and geometric construction utilized to analyze, visualize, and calculate transformations of strain states. Directly analogous to Mohr's stress circle, this technique relies on trigonometric transformations to effectively represent and evaluate the strain tensor.

The construction is specifically represented within the (ε, γ/2) plane, relating normal strain (ε) to half of the shear strain (γ/2). The deliberate use of γ/2 in this graphical analysis ensures consistency between the tensorial representation and the geometric construction by properly forming a symmetric tensor component. This direct connection to the (ε, γ/2) coordinate system firmly links the graphical method to the underlying strain tensor.

Mohr's Strain Circle is broadly applied to perform strain analysis and tensor transformations in both two-dimensional and three-dimensional strain states. It is specifically used for the construction and calculation of principal strains. In academic contexts, this graphical method for analyzing strain transformations is standardly covered in lecture content, with specific reference to Section 11.4 of the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0646aad7e7ebf134b763f7b7172548fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aac206a8f9ab7199ba4ae6138d74c7ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddbe1099c718b59c4f95b165b71f72ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a02b6d758a21ac5c0b6ac41bda509d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427b2f7911554c401a806ff733b5975e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8f3f8c72a4f9a9fdeabe99c8c8ba4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
