</node>
<node id="Strain Transformation Equations">
  <data key="d0">Strain Transformation Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Equations for rotating the xy-coordinate system by angle φ to a ξη-system: εξ = ½(εx + εy) + ½(εx - εy)cos(2φ) + ½γxy sin(2φ) and ½γη = -½(εx - εy)sin(2φ) + ½γxy cos(2φ).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geotechnical Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Geotechnical Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of civil engineering dealing with earth materials, for which strain transformation theory provides a theoretical foundation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain (γxy)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain (γxy)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A kinematic variable representing shear deformation, which characteristically appears as ½γxy in tensorial formulations and strain transformation equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Directions (Hauptrichtungen)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Directions (Hauptrichtungen)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific orientations in a strained material where shear strains are zero and normal strains reach their extremum values (principal strains).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Strains (Hauptdehnungen)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strains (Hauptdehnungen)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum and minimum normal strains experienced by a material element when aligned with the principal directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ξη-system">
  <data key="d0">ξη-system</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A rotated coordinate system derived by rotating the standard xy-coordinate system by an angle φ, used as the target frame in strain transformation equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999153</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Transformation Theory">
  <data key="d0">Strain Transformation Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The theoretical framework characterizing how strain components transform under coordinate rotation, notably utilizing a factor of ½ for shear strain γxy in tensorial formulations.</data>
