</node>
<node id="Principal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum and minimum strains calculated in three dimensions, enabled by Mohr's strain circle construction.&lt;SEP&gt;The strain in the principal direction, derived from applying Hooke's generalized law to principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;The strains (ε₁, ε₂, ε₃) in the principal directions, which are substituted into the strain energy density expression to separate total energy into components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Homogeneous Materials">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Homogeneous Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Materials with uniform properties in all directions, which are analyzed for stress-strain distributions under various loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f99bd9629698e210e8fd8be478fe64f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component ε_x">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component ε_x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A dimensionless quantity representing longitudinal strain in the x-direction, defined by the local gradient of the x-displacement.&lt;SEP&gt;The normal strain in the principal x-direction, computed as the superposition of mechanical strain from triaxial stress and thermal strain from a temperature differential.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component u">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component u</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A scalar field describing the particle displacement in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ξη-System">
  <data key="d0">ξη-System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system used as a reference for coordinate rotation in transformation equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="(ε, γ/2) Plane">
  <data key="d0">(ε, γ/2) Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The two-dimensional graphical plane used to plot and visualize Mohr's strain circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88f6cb959dc0f4ce6e1902a94dcb7d12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Definition in Y-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Definition in Y-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal strain ε_y as the partial derivative of y-displacement v with respect to coordinate y. This fundamental kinematic relation is a diagonal component of the infinitesimal strain tensor, essential for analyzing deformation states, deriving principal strains, constructing Mohr's strain circle, and formulating constitutive laws (Hooke's law) in linear elasticity for homogeneous isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4140a14a908df0411dd83a324fb40b7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784980021</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain-Displacement Relation in z-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain-Displacement Relation in z-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the normal strain component $\varepsilon_z$ as the partial derivative of the z-displacement $w$ with respect to the z-coordinate. It is a fundamental kinematic equation of infinitesimal strain theory, linking displacement fields to the strain tensor within the lecture's framework on strain states and elasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784980555</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain-Displacement Relation">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain-Displacement Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The kinematic equation representing the normal strain in the z-direction within the framework of infinitesimal continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain Component Epsilon Z">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain Component Epsilon Z</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The longitudinal or normal strain component along the z-axis of the strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Continuum Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Continuum Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The theoretical framework based on small-strain assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Theory Of Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Linearized Theory Of Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The geometrically linear theory of deformation applicable to small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Deformation State">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Deformation State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of spatial deformation derived from kinematic equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A deformation state concept known as räumlicher Verzerrungszustand, resulting from the plane stress state in elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb63bc877af3e54e4e227db32248b763</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Generalized Law">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Generalized Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A generalized law connecting stresses and strains via material constants.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle in mechanics that relates stress and strain in multiple dimensions for elastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A generalized form of Hooke's law applied in two dimensions to relate stress and strain tensors.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical law combining various strain components to establish the isotropic relationship between material moduli like Young's modulus and shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Homogeneous Isotropic Material Model">
  <data key="d0">Homogeneous Isotropic Material Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material model assuming uniform and direction-independent physical properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain State (Verzerrungszustand)">
  <data key="d0">Strain State (Verzerrungszustand)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of deformation within a material, explicitly referenced as part of the surrounding lecture context.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Tensor Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Strain Tensor Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical process applied to a strain tensor to determine principal strains or strains in varying directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Material Line Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Material Line Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A small, conceptually parallel material line in a body whose relative elongation or compression is measured by the normal strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Constants">
  <data key="d0">Material Constants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical parameters characterizing material behavior, used to relate stress and strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Three material constants connected by the equation, where only two are independent and the third is deterministically derived.&lt;SEP&gt;Constants for isotropic elastic materials discussed in the Elastizitätsgesetz chapter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformable Solids">
  <data key="d0">Deformable Solids</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical solid bodies that undergo deformation, whose three-dimensional stress-strain states are analyzed in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b37ac6d222ba4898f7b2f8f457e77e5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_xy (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Component γ_xy (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the engineering shear strain γ_xy as the sum of cross-partial derivatives of displacement components u and v. Fundamental to the strain tensor formulation (Verzerrungstensor), it connects displacement kinematics to Mohr's strain circle and the linear elasticity law for isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784980893</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineering shear strain component in the xy-plane representing the total change in angle between two originally perpendicular line elements.&lt;SEP&gt;The total angular change of a right angle between originally perpendicular line elements in the x and y directions, representing the resulting deformation from applied shear stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field u">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field u</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement field component in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field v">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field v</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement field component in the y-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensorial Shear Strain ε_xy">
  <data key="d0">Tensorial Shear Strain ε_xy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensorial shear strain component related to engineering shear strain by a factor of 1/2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Modulus G">
  <data key="d0">Shear Modulus G</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material property that relates shear stress to shear strain.&lt;SEP&gt;A material property derived from Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio using the formula G = E/(2(1+$\nu$)).&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Schubmodul or modulus of rigidity, a material constant that is not independent but derivable as G = E / [2(1 + ν)] for isotropic elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ff56a8965495f3a5913389bf912308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Derivative ∂u/∂y">
  <data key="d0">Partial Derivative ∂u/∂y</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rate of change of x-displacement along the y-direction, representing rotation of an initially y-aligned line element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Derivative ∂v/∂x">
  <data key="d0">Partial Derivative ∂v/∂x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rate of change of y-displacement along the x-direction, representing rotation of an initially x-aligned line element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rigid Body Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Rigid Body Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Motion of a material element without shear strain, occurring when ∂u/∂y = -∂v/∂x for small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999179</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Homogeneous Isotropic Materials">
  <data key="d0">Homogeneous Isotropic Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class of materials for which the complete linear elastic material model applies.&lt;SEP&gt;Materials with uniform properties throughout and identical physical properties in all directions.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of material for which material constants and stress-strain relationships are discussed in the context of the elasticity law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b73317ca6218a3794fd92d7cfec3e4db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00185afb9a5d259c31643f50551104c4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain ε">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain ε</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensorial normal strain component related to normal stress through Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress σ">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress σ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress component related to normal strain through Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain γ_xz (Strain-Displacement Relation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the engineering shear strain γ_xz in the xz-plane as the sum of the partial derivatives of the displacement field components u and w. It quantifies the angular distortion between originally perpendicular line elements along the x- and z-axes, forming part of the strain tensor essential for elasticity analysis and Mohr's strain circle construction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784981003</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strain Component Gamma_xz">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strain Component Gamma_xz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The engineering shear strain component in the xz-plane, which quantifies the total angular distortion-the reduction of an originally right angle-between two infinitesimal material line elements oriented along the x-axis and z-axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component u(x,y,z)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component u(x,y,z)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denotes the displacement field component in the x-direction in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field Component w(x,y,z)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field Component w(x,y,z)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denotes the displacement field component in the z-direction in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Tensor epsilon_ij">
  <data key="d0">Strain Tensor epsilon_ij</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A symmetric tensor comprising three normal strains and three engineering shear strains, where the tensorial shear component satisfies the relation epsilon_xz = gamma_xz / 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poisson's Ratio nu">
  <data key="d0">Poisson's Ratio nu</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material parameter used in the constitutive law to relate stresses to strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999182</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lateral Contraction">
  <data key="d0">Lateral Contraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Querkontraktion, a deformation phenomenon analyzed under plane stress conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The deformation, also known as Querkontraktion, induced by normal stress in multiaxial stress states and governed by Poisson's ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4512fdc0f661bddfaf9a4f9bea1eef2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999191</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strains (epsilon_x, epsilon_y, epsilon_z)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strains (epsilon_x, epsilon_y, epsilon_z)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain components representing extensions or contractions along the coordinate axes, forming part of the complete set of strain-displacement relations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999191</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Shear Strains (gamma_xy, gamma_yz)">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Shear Strains (gamma_xy, gamma_yz)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain components representing angular distortions in the respective planes, which alongside normal strains form the symmetric strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9a00e68fbbec347e4cd88332c5f0573</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
