<node id="Uniaxial Hooke's Law with Poisson Effect for Plane Stress State (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Hooke's Law with Poisson Effect for Plane Stress State (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation defines the strain response to uniaxial normal stress σx in an isotropic, homogeneous material under plane stress conditions. It expresses longitudinal strain εx = σx/E (Hooke's Law) and transverse strain εy = -ν·σx/E (Poisson contraction). It forms the fundamental basis for deriving the full elasticity matrix through superposition, demonstrating that only E and ν are needed to describe isotropic elastic behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784982705</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Shear Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear modulus, commonly denoted as G and also referred to as μ, the Lamé second parameter, or the Schubmodul, is a fundamental elastic material property that characterizes a substance's resistance to shear deformation and spatial distortion. It quantifies a material's rigidity and stiffness under applied shear stress, serving as the constant of proportionality that directly relates shear stress to shear strain. Typically measured in Pascals [Pa], this mechanical property can be experimentally determined through methods such as the torsion testing of thin-walled tubes.

As a core component of material science and solid mechanics, the shear modulus demonstrates the fundamental interdependence of a material's elastic properties. It maintains a classical mathematical relationship with Young's modulus (E) and Poisson's ratio (ν), defined by the constitutive equation G = E / [2(1+ν)]. This relationship, which illustrates how shear stiffness is fundamentally linked to a material's overall stress-strain behavior, is a standard topic extensively covered and derived within mechanics textbooks and technical schematic diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e13c1eccdbc66212f603ee56ccf63b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427b2f7911554c401a806ff733b5975e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999391</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Elastic Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Elastic Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material behavior that can be fully characterized by only two independent material constants: E and ν</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999227</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain along the loading axis (ε_x = σ_x/E), representing direct proportionality between stress and strain</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
