<node id="Shear Deformation of a Planar Element (image)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Deformation of a Planar Element (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visually demonstrates the effect of pure shear stress ($\\tau_{xy}$) on a square material element. It depicts the transition from an undeformed square (black) to a deformed parallelogram (green), quantifying the angular distortion as shear strain ($\\gamma_{xy}$). This figure serves as a fundamental illustration for deriving the relationship between shear stress and strain, and ultimately the shear modulus (Schubmodul), within the study of isotropic elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784984374</data>
</node>
<node id="Belastung Einer Scheibe Nur Durch Schubspannungen">
  <data key="d0">Belastung Einer Scheibe Nur Durch Schubspannungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific loading condition in solid mechanics where a disk or plate is subjected exclusively to shear stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc328ffaca84d39a7bfc9df7c2f37086</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress-Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress-Strain Constitutive Relation (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the linear-elastic relationship between shear stress τ_xy and engineering shear strain γ_xy via the shear modulus G. A fundamental component of the Elastizitätsgesetz for isotropic materials, complementing normal stress relations. Within plane stress analysis, it completes the constitutive framework, with G being derivable from E and ν.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784984447</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress-Shear Strain Constitutive Relation">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress-Shear Strain Constitutive Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation τ_xy = G · γ_xy representing Hooke's law in shear for linear-elastic, isotropic materials under plane stress conditions, establishing a linear proportionality between applied shear stress and resulting angular deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law In Shear">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law In Shear</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A constitutive relation valid within the linear-elastic regime before yielding, establishing linear proportionality between applied shear stress and resulting angular deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Hooke's Law">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Hooke's Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The relation σ = E · ε for normal stress and strain, analogous to the shear stress-shear strain relation but applicable to the axial component of deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized 3D Hooke's Law">
  <data key="d0">Generalized 3D Hooke's Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The tensorial form of Hooke's law that encompasses the shear stress-shear strain relation as one component, combined with normal stress-strain relations in all directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Matrix For Plane Stress Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Matrix For Plane Stress Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The complete constitutive matrix formed by combining normal stress-strain relations (σ_x, σ_y, σ_z) with shear stress-strain relations for plane stress analysis (ebener Spannungszustand).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear-Elastic Isotropic Material">
  <data key="d0">Linear-Elastic Isotropic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material type characterized by two independent material constants (Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν), for which the shear stress-shear strain constitutive relation is valid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999263</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxially Loaded Plate">
  <data key="d0">Biaxially Loaded Plate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Biaxially Loaded Plate, frequently referred to in technical literature by its German terms "Scheibe," "biaxial belastete Scheibe," or "biaxial belasteter Scheibe," is a fundamental structural element utilized in continuum mechanics and elasticity theory. Physically, it is defined as a thin plate, membrane, or mechanical structural component that is subjected to multi-axial loading. Specifically, the element experiences normal stresses, equal and opposite normal strains, and loading applied along two perpendicular, orthogonal axes. In this configuration, the principal stress directions align with the material's principal axes.

In theoretical analyses, the Biaxially Loaded Plate is often examined under conditions of pure shear stress. Within this framework, the diagonals of a representative square element within the plate lie at a 45-degree (π/4 radians) angle to the shear plane. To properly analyze the deformation and stress distribution, mechanics frequently employ a 45°-rotated coordinate system. The behavior of this structural model is fundamentally governed by the generalized Hooke's Law, which serves as the foundational constitutive relationship for the element. Through this framework, specific strain equations and geometric relationships describing the deformation of the plate are rigorously derived.

The primary utility of the Biaxially Loaded Plate is its role as a theoretical and physical basis for establishing the mathematical relationship between fundamental material constants for isotropic elastic materials. By analyzing this structural element under biaxial stress or pure shear, engineers and physicists are able to mathematically connect normal and shear elastic constants. Most notably, the model is extensively used to derive the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul, E) and the shear modulus (Schubmodul, G). This derivation demonstrates that the shear modulus is fundamentally dependent upon Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio (ν). This critical mechanical derivation is a standard topic in educational texts and is often explicitly detailed in mechanics literature, such as on page 13 or within dedicated sections like Section 12.2 of the "Elastizitätsgesetz" (Law of Elasticity).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ce366fccc91c18aaa393823e9d843a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-235ab6bb09c88f34c57e39b028978709&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b68662a379adb00139d0404ab69c20e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec12900410ee6e1062ea25bf1aa27c44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
