<node id="Page 13 - Elastizitätsgesetz Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 13 - Elastizitätsgesetz Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number marker (13) for a page covering section 12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz, focusing on deriving the relationship between E-modulus and shear modulus using a biaxially loaded plate. This page follows earlier content on shear stress loading, shear modulus determination, and material constants for isotropic elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-235ab6bb09c88f34c57e39b028978709</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784984931</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Shear Modulus Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Shear Modulus Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square material element under a specific biaxial load (tension in one axis, equal compression in the perpendicular axis) to illustrate the equivalence to a pure shear state at a 45-degree orientation. It serves as a visual aid for the mechanical derivation linking the E-modulus and shear modulus (G) for isotropic elastic materials, specifically addressing the 'Elastizitätsgesetz' and the behavior of disks under shear stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-818241f00ae24dac642bd60f22620196</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784985141</data>
</node>
