<node id="Page 14 - Elastizitätsgesetz Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 14 - Elastizitätsgesetz Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 14 of a German mechanics textbook, located within Section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz'. This page contains the derivation of the E-modul to Schubmodul relationship using a biaxially loaded plate analysis, building on prior content about shear stress, torsion testing, and the two independent material constants for isotropic elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-82eb071fda1046164d3c4b0ddbf54509</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784985340</data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Elastic Modulus Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Stress State Diagram for Elastic Modulus Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square element subjected to a specific biaxial load (tension in one axis and compression in the perpendicular axis). According to the surrounding context regarding 'Elastizitätsgesetz', this illustration is used to derive the relationship between Young's Modulus (E-Modul) and the Shear Modulus (Schubmodul). It visually demonstrates that a state of equal tension and compression is mechanically equivalent to pure shear stress acting on planes oriented at 45 degrees, as represented by the internal dashed rhombus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd4685e0a0874c320cb468d922d54e20</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784985876</data>
</node>
