<node id="Deformed Length Expression (l + Δl) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Deformed Length Expression (l + Δl) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This expression represents the total length of a material element after deformation, combining original length l with elongation Δl. It serves as a foundational geometric relationship in deriving the constitutive elasticity law, specifically the connection between Young's modulus and shear modulus through analysis of a biaxially loaded plate element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784986267</data>
</node>
<node id="l + Δl">
  <data key="d0">l + Δl</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An expression representing the deformed length of a material specimen or structural element after undergoing axial deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poisson Effects">
  <data key="d0">Poisson Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A physical phenomenon where transverse strains result from longitudinal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999327</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Loading">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A scenario where deformations occur in multiple directions simultaneously, introducing Poisson effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Loading conditions where stresses are applied in two orthogonal directions, specifically where σ₁ = σ and σ₂ = -σ.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coupled Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Coupled Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deformations occurring in multiple directions simultaneously, analyzed on a rotated coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ε = Δl/l">
  <data key="d0">ε = Δl/l</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical definition of strain, connecting geometric deformation to material constitutive behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="G = E/[2(1+ν)]">
  <data key="d0">G = E/[2(1+ν)]</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A derived mathematical relationship establishing the connection between shear modulus, Young's modulus, and Poisson's ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law for Biaxial Normal Strain (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law for Biaxial Normal Strain (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the normal strain in the x-direction for a biaxially loaded elastic element, accounting for the Poisson effect. It is used in the surrounding context as a foundational step to mathematically derive the relationship between Young's modulus and the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784986382</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress In X-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress In X-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress denoted as σ_x applied in the x-direction, which causes elongation in that direction in an elastic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress In Y-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress In Y-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress denoted as σ_y applied in the y-direction, which through the Poisson effect causes lateral contraction or expansion in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Material">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A linear, isotropic, and homogeneous material for which the generalized Hooke's Law describes the behavior under a two-dimensional state of stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Transformations">
  <data key="d0">Strain Transformations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical transformations evaluated alongside shear deformations, derived from a biaxial stress state to determine the relationship between Young's Modulus, Poisson's ratio, and Shear Modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Shear Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deformations evaluated alongside strain transformations, derived from a biaxial stress state to demonstrate the fundamental interdependence of elastic material properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation $\varepsilon _ { x } = \frac { 1 } { E } \left( \sigma _ { x } - \nu \cdot \sigma _ { y } \right)$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $\varepsilon _ { x } = \frac { 1 } { E } \left( \sigma _ { x } - \nu \cdot \sigma _ { y } \right)$</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical formula in LaTeX format that represents the generalized Hooke's Law for calculating normal strain in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Component under Generalized Hooke's Law (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Component under Generalized Hooke's Law (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain component equation from elasticity theory for biaxially loaded plates, expressing normal strain using Young's Modulus, Poisson's ratio, and normal stress. Used to derive the relationship between elastic moduli in linear isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784986483</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Component Equation">
  <data key="d0">Strain Component Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation that computes the normal strain in a specific direction under combined stress states, derived from the generalized Hooke's Law for a biaxially loaded elastic plate or sheet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compliance Factor">
  <data key="d0">Compliance Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The term 1/E in the strain component equation, representing the inverse of Young's Modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Constitutive Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Constitutive Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses strain under biaxial loading as ε = -σ(1+ν)/E, serving as an intermediate step in deriving the relationship between Young's modulus (E) and Shear modulus (G = E/[2(1+ν)]). It arises from analyzing a biaxially loaded plate where principal stresses are equal and opposite, demonstrating the coupling between normal stress, Poisson's ratio, and resulting strain in isotropic linear elasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784986554</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Equation">
  <data key="d0">Strain Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation representing a strain-stress relationship derived for a biaxially loaded plate, stating that strain equals negative stress divided by Young's modulus multiplied by (1 + ν).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Strain">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The difference between principal strains, denoted as γ_max, which under specific loading equals 2σ(1+ν)/E.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Moduli Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Moduli Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The classic mathematical relationship between Young's modulus and Shear modulus, defined by the equation G = E/[2(1+ν)].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress-Strain Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress-Strain Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation τ = G·γ that links shear stress and shear strain, which produces the classic elastic moduli relationship when combined with principal strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poisson's Effect">
  <data key="d0">Poisson's Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical phenomenon that causes transverse or lateral strain, represented by a negative sign in the strain equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Stress/Strain Conventions">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Stress/Strain Conventions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard sign conventions in mechanics that may be indicated by a negative sign in a stress or strain equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Strain-Displacement Relation (y-component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Strain-Displacement Relation (y-component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Hooke's Law for ε_y under biaxial loading, expressing transverse strain as a function of σ_y and Poisson-coupled σ_x. Central to deriving the E-to-G modulus relationship in linear elasticity theory for plane stress conditions, specifically applied to biaxially loaded plate analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784986948</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain In Y-direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain In Y-direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the normal strain in the y-direction (ε_y) under plane stress conditions, calculated as (1/E)(σ_y - ν·σ_x).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law For Two-dimensional Linear Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law For Two-dimensional Linear Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental physics principle describing the behavior of elastic materials under two-dimensional plane stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Compliance Matrix Formulation">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Compliance Matrix Formulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical framework consisting of three coupled strain-stress relations, including the normal strain in the y-direction equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="45°-rotated Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">45°-rotated Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system rotated by 45° used to derive the relationship between Young's modulus and shear modulus by transforming pure shear into equal biaxial principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Principal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Principal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equal and opposite normal stresses (σ_x = -σ_y) that result from transforming pure shear in a 45°-rotated coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classical Relation G = E/(2(1+ν))">
  <data key="d0">Classical Relation G = E/(2(1+ν))</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental relationship between shear modulus (G), Young's modulus (E), and Poisson's ratio (ν) derived from analyzing a biaxially loaded plate in a 45°-rotated coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Coordinates">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinates</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system used to define the normal stress components σ_x and σ_y.&lt;SEP&gt;A coordinate system to which the foundational model is extended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Anisotropy">
  <data key="d0">Material Anisotropy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The directional dependence of deformation response, demonstrated by the negative coupling term (-ν·σ_x) in the strain equation, despite isotropic material properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Material Properties">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Material Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material properties that are the same in all directions, with Poisson's ratio typically ranging from 0.0 to 0.5.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Composite Laminate Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Composite Laminate Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An analytical technique for composite materials where plane stress assumptions are valid and the described constitutive relations are applied.&lt;SEP&gt;An application domain where the principal direction formula enables optimal fiber orientation in composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-walled Components">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Components</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural components for which the relationship between material properties is essential for structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plate Theory">
  <data key="d0">Plate Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A branch of structural mechanics for which the relationship between Young's modulus and shear modulus is essential.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Strain Relationship in Linear Elasticity (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Strain Relationship in Linear Elasticity (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses normal strain (ε) under biaxial loading with opposing principal stresses, combining direct strain and Poisson lateral contraction. It serves as an intermediate step in deriving the fundamental relationship between Young's modulus (E) and shear modulus (G) for isotropic materials, demonstrating that only two independent elastic constants define linear-elastic material behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5f211f631841b2f09bad4fbd9c9b66c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784987197</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Linear Normal Strain (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Linear Normal Strain (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal strain ε as the ratio of length change Δl to original length l, with the rearranged form Δl = ε·l. Serves as the foundational kinematic relationship in the derivation of the elasticity law connecting Young's modulus and shear modulus for a biaxially loaded plate element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784987693</data>
</node>
<node id="Epsilon">
  <data key="d0">Epsilon</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The variable denoting dimensionless normal strain, treated as a scalar in the one-dimensional case or as a second-rank tensor component in three-dimensional formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz">
  <data key="d0">12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Law of Elasticity section that provides the foundational kinematic relationship linking geometric deformation to constitutive laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extensometers">
  <data key="d0">Extensometers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Instruments used to measure normal strain during material testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Tests">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material testing where normal strain is measured using extensometers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil And Mechanical Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Civil And Mechanical Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fields of engineering that apply these principles for the design of load-bearing components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-003690f3bd06e7311822e9ac7e4a4393</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
