</node>
<node id="Biaxial Belastete Scheibe">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Belastete Scheibe</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A biaxially loaded plate element used in Section 12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz to mathematically derive the relationship between Young's modulus and shear modulus, involving stress transformation, strain analysis, and constitutive equations for linear elastic isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-47751b806b05c6d8ba7d4ccfae665886</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="German-language Mechanics Textbook">
  <data key="d0">German-language Mechanics Textbook</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A German-language mechanics textbook or lecture notes that contains Page Number 15 and serves as a navigational reference for Festigkeitslehre.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-47751b806b05c6d8ba7d4ccfae665886</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Elastic Isotropic Materials">
  <data key="d0">Linear Elastic Isotropic Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material for which constitutive equations and stress-strain relationships are derived in Section 12.2 Elastizitätsgesetz.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-47751b806b05c6d8ba7d4ccfae665886</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Blue Square">
  <data key="d0">Solid Blue Square</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric shape in the diagram representing the original, undeformed state of the material element with side length 'l'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Axis Arrows">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Axis Arrows</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Red outward-pointing vectors on the vertical axis indicating loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Axis Arrows">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Axis Arrows</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Red inward-pointing vectors on the horizontal axis indicating loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress pushing the material together on the horizontal axis, labeled as '$\\sigma_x = -\\sigma$'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A state of deformation resulting from a combination of equal-magnitude tensile and compressive stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Stress">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific state of stress involving two perpendicular axes, applied to the material element in the diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of stress (σ_x = σ, σ_y = -σ) applied to the plate, under which the maximum shear stress on the 45° planes is equal to σ.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Side Length L">
  <data key="d0">Side Length L</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The dimension of the sides of the solid blue square representing the original, undeformed material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5302c423fc1b3994ee440aa1eba886af</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformation Diagram for Elasticity Law Derivation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Deformation Diagram for Elasticity Law Derivation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic illustrates the geometric deformation of a square material element into a rhombus, specifically highlighting the relationship between normal strain ($\\varepsilon$) and shear strain ($\\gamma$). By showing the horizontal contraction to $l(1-\\varepsilon)$ and the resulting angular changes ($90^\\circ - \\gamma$ and $45^\\circ - \\gamma/2$), the diagram provides the visual proof needed to derive the fundamental elasticity equation connecting Young's modulus and shear modulus in a biaxial stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784987967</data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Blue Square Frame">
  <data key="d0">Solid Blue Square Frame</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric shape in the technical schematic diagram representing the initial, undeformed state of a square element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometric Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical procedure used to analyze the deformation of the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Shear-Strain Relationship for Biaxial Loading (E-G Modulus Derivation) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Shear-Strain Relationship for Biaxial Loading (E-G Modulus Derivation) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This geometric kinematic equation relates shear strain γ to normal strain ε for a biaxially loaded plate element. It serves as the key intermediate step in deriving the constitutive relationship G = E/[2(1+ν)] between Young's modulus and shear modulus, connecting deformation geometry to material elastic constants within classical linear elasticity theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988098</data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Kinematic Relationship Equation">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Kinematic Relationship Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing a geometric kinematic relationship in the deformation of a biaxially loaded plate, stating that the tangent of the deformed diagonal angle equals the ratio (1-ε)/(1+ε).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Relationship Equation">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Relationship Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The classic formula G = E/[2(1+ν)] connecting isotropic elastic constants, derived using the geometric kinematic relationship.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small-strain Approximation">
  <data key="d0">Small-strain Approximation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical approximation stating tan(π/4 - γ/2) ≈ (1-γ/2)/(1+γ/2) for small values of γ, yielding γ ≈ 2ε.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Square">
  <data key="d0">Unit Square</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric shape used to visualize deformation, where its corner originally at (1,1) moves to (1+ε, 1-ε) under biaxial strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-55f0f77e6766d722b567e55f8b17701c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Strain-Length Relationship (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Strain-Length Relationship (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the deformed length of a material element as a function of its original length and strain. In the context of elasticity theory, it serves as a fundamental kinematic step used to describe geometric deformation in a biaxially loaded plate, ultimately enabling the analytical derivation of the relationship between Young's Modulus and Shear Modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988415</data>
</node>
<node id="Absolute Elongation">
  <data key="d0">Absolute Elongation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total change in length of a line segment under longitudinal strain, denoted as Delta l.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law For Plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law For Plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principle relating stress and strain in a two-dimensional state, used to determine strain values by incorporating Young's Modulus and Poisson's ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Angle">
  <data key="d0">Shear Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angular deformation, denoted as gamma, calculated using geometric trigonometric relations of a deformed rhombus element.&lt;SEP&gt;Represented by the variable γ, it represents the angular distortion (Winkeländerung under Schubbeanspruchung) of a deforming element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Normal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses applied in two perpendicular directions to a structural element or plate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tangent Subtraction Identity for Shear Strain Analysis (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Tangent Subtraction Identity for Shear Strain Analysis (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation applies the tangent subtraction formula tan(A−B) = (tanA−tanB)/(1+tanA·tanB) to the angle (π/4 − γ/2), where γ is the shear strain angle. It serves as a key algebraic step in deriving the relationship between Young's modulus (E) and shear modulus (G) by analyzing the geometric deformation of a biaxially loaded plate under pure shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988544</data>
</node>
<node id="Tangent Equation">
  <data key="d0">Tangent Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the tangent of π/4 minus γ/2, which captures the deformed diagonal's angle relative to the original axes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tangent Subtraction Identity">
  <data key="d0">Tangent Subtraction Identity</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental trigonometric formula, also known as Tangenssubtraktionsformel, used to expand the tangent of a difference of two angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Angle">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angle, denoted as gamma (γ) or Schubwinkel/Gleitwinkel, representing the shear deformation that causes right angles of an element to distort.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensorial Strain Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Tensorial Strain Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A methodological framework in continuum mechanics that the Tangent Equation bridges with trigonometric identities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear-Strain Compatibility Equation for Biaxial Loading (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear-Strain Compatibility Equation for Biaxial Loading (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation relates shear angle γ to normal strain ε through a fractional identity arising from geometric analysis of a biaxially loaded plate. It provides the kinematic foundation for deriving the fundamental relationship G = E/(2(1+ν)) between Young's modulus and shear modulus, serving as a key step in establishing the isotropic elasticity law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988691</data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Strain Assumption">
  <data key="d0">Small-Strain Assumption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An approximation used for small angles where tan(γ/2) ≈ γ/2, allowing the simplification of complex equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Linearized Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The simplified mathematical result (γ = 2ε) derived from the main equation under the small-strain assumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classical Formula">
  <data key="d0">Classical Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The derived expression G = E/(2(1+ν)) that establishes the constitutive relationship between fundamental elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Möbius-type Fractional Expressions">
  <data key="d0">Möbius-type Fractional Expressions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fractional mathematical structure of the form (1-x)/(1+x), used to equate the tangent half-angle and the longitudinal strain in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The evaluation of the motion and deformation of a square element, from which the geometric identity emerges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Finite Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A regime of deformation where strains are not restricted to being infinitely small, for which the mathematical equation remains exact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Strain Regime">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Strain Regime</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical context assuming extremely small strains, which the equation's validity surpasses due to its nonlinear geometric structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4c4b387a69caef33cbbc0d01cd946a0a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear–Normal Strain Kinematic Relation (Biaxial Loading) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear–Normal Strain Kinematic Relation (Biaxial Loading) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Exact geometric relationship tan(γ/2) = ε linking shear strain γ to normal strain ε in a biaxially loaded plate. It is the pivotal kinematic step in deriving G = E/[2(1+ν)], connecting Young's modulus and shear modulus via Poisson's ratio within Hooke's law for isotropic elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988829</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain (γ)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain (γ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total engineering shear strain representing the angle change in radians in a deformed element.&lt;SEP&gt;Engineering shear strain, representing the angular deformation (in radians) of the material under shear loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain (ε)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain (ε)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The longitudinal normal strain in the principal direction of a biaxially loaded elastic plate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxially Loaded Elastic Plate">
  <data key="d0">Biaxially Loaded Elastic Plate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An elastic plate subjected to equal and opposite principal stresses, where a square element deforms into a rectangle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Young's Modulus (E-Modul)">
  <data key="d0">Young's Modulus (E-Modul)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material constant measuring the stiffness of an elastic material, also referred to as E-Modul.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tangent Subtraction Formula">
  <data key="d0">Tangent Subtraction Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A trigonometric identity used to derive the kinematic relationship by evaluating tan(45° − γ/2) = (1 − tan(γ/2))/(1 + tan(γ/2)).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress acting on a plane where shear stress is zero, applied as equal and opposite loads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fundamental Elastic Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Fundamental Elastic Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The derived equation G = E/[2(1+ν)] connecting Young's modulus, shear modulus, and Poisson's ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Shear-Normal Strain Relationship (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Shear-Normal Strain Relationship (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This kinematic equation states that the principal normal strain ($\varepsilon$) of an element under pure shear equals half of the engineering shear strain ($\gamma/2$). Serving as a vital geometric boundary condition in continuum mechanics, it provides the necessary link to derive the constitutive relationship between Young's modulus ($E$), the shear modulus ($G$), and Poisson's ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-723dacfa4b87cbbd890504a4b5cf1d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784988937</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 16 - Elastizitätsgesetz Section (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 16 - Elastizitätsgesetz Section (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '16' from a German mechanics textbook, located within section 12.2 'Elastizitätsgesetz.' This page continues the derivation of the relationship between Young's modulus (E-Modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) via a biaxially loaded plate analysis, serving as a navigational reference within the theoretical discussion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec12900410ee6e1062ea25bf1aa27c44</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784989429</data>
</node>
<node id="German Technical Document">
  <data key="d0">German Technical Document</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A German academic/technical document on mechanics/materials science that contains sections covering theoretical frameworks such as elasticity and material properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec12900410ee6e1062ea25bf1aa27c44</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page Index 15">
  <data key="d0">Page Index 15</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The zero-based page index of the document, corresponding to the 16th physical page.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec12900410ee6e1062ea25bf1aa27c44</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Relationship between Shear Strain and Normal Strain in Hooke's Model (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Relationship between Shear Strain and Normal Strain in Hooke's Model (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation derives the fundamental relationship between shear strain γ and normal strain ε using Hooke's Law with Poisson's ratio ν. It serves as an intermediate step in establishing the connection between Young's modulus (E) and shear modulus (G) for isotropic linear elastic materials, yielding the classical result G = E/[2(1+ν)].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784989550</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Isotropic Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Linear Isotropic Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical framework describing the elastic behavior of materials with uniform properties in all directions, governed by Hooke's Law.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical and physical context in which Hooke's Law for shear stress is applied, assuming the material behaves linearly and has identical properties in all directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Tensor Component">
  <data key="d0">Strain Tensor Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as ε, a component of the symmetric strain tensor in continuum mechanics, related to engineering shear strain by the relationship γ/2 = ε.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain Equation">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation γ/2 = ε, which establishes the tensorial relationship between engineering shear strain γ and the corresponding strain tensor component ε, essential for coordinate transformation in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Constitutive Equation">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Constitutive Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation ε = σ/E × (1 + ν), representing Hooke's constitutive law incorporating Poisson's ratio ν, where σ is normal stress and E is Young's modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Constants Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Constants Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation G = E/[2(1+ν)], the fundamental relationship between shear modulus G, Young's modulus E, and Poisson's ratio ν, derived by combining the shear strain equation with Hooke's constitutive equation using Mohr's circle principle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Principle">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical method for coordinate transformation of stress and strain states, exploiting the equivalence of strain states when viewing biaxial stress in a rotated coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999378</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Shear Strain Equation">
  <data key="d0">Combined Shear Strain Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The combined mathematical result γ/2 = σ/E × (1 + ν), which leads directly to the fundamental relationship between elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999391</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Diagram for Biaxial Stress State (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Diagram for Biaxial Stress State (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates Mohr's circle for a specific biaxial loading scenario where principal stresses are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign (σx = -σ, σy = σ). The diagram visually demonstrates that for this stress state, the maximum shear stress (τ) is equal to the magnitude of the principal stress (σ). This representation is central to the derivation of the relationship between Young's modulus (E-modul) and shear modulus (Schubmodul) as described in the surrounding text regarding the Elasticity Law (Elastizitätsgesetz) for a biaxially loaded disk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784989818</data>
</node>
<node id="c3b6b61dd9f498a8353097d9860dc3dc1a1776c0ae42b9fb81d9ad75f25abc2f.jpg">
  <data key="d0">c3b6b61dd9f498a8353097d9860dc3dc1a1776c0ae42b9fb81d9ad75f25abc2f.jpg</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The image file containing the diagram of Mohr's Circle being analyzed in the provided text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd7867c7c2376ea56c7780cb73e5ec8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999391</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Strain-Shear Stress Constitutive Relationship (Isotropic Hooke's Law) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Strain-Shear Stress Constitutive Relationship (Isotropic Hooke's Law) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the linear elastic relationship between shear strain and shear stress for isotropic materials. The factor 2(1+ν)/E represents the inverse shear modulus (1/G), demonstrating that G can be derived from Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν. It results from analyzing a biaxially loaded plate and forms part of the generalized Hooke's Law in principal coordinates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784989913</data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Material">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material requiring only two independent elastic constants to fully describe its linear elastic behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A linearly elastic material in which stresses and strains share the same principal directions.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of material where shear strains and shear stresses decouple completely from normal stresses, and thermal expansion produces only volumetric strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearly Elastic Material">
  <data key="d0">Linearly Elastic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material for which the relationship between shear strain and shear stress is established, demonstrating linear elastic behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anisotropic Material">
  <data key="d0">Anisotropic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fully anisotropic material whose mechanical behavior requires 21 independent elastic constants to be fully described.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Shear Modulus Relationship (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Shear Modulus Relationship (Hooke's Law for Shear) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents Hooke's Law for shear ($\tau = G\gamma$) for linear isotropic materials. It mathematically defines the Shear Modulus ($G$) as a function of Young's Modulus ($E$) and Poisson's ratio ($\nu$), demonstrating that an isotropic material only has two independent elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990022</data>
</node>
<node id="Stiffness Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Stiffness Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical construct in continuum mechanics that comprehensively characterizes the 3D linear elastic response of a material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial State Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial State Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress condition analyzed within continuum mechanics that involves normal stresses acting along two perpendicular axes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Herleitung">
  <data key="d0">Herleitung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German term for derivation, referring to the mathematical and physical process of deriving the relationship between normal and shear elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Axes System">
  <data key="d0">Principal Axes System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The coordinate system, also known as 'Hauptachsensystem', in which normal stresses and strains are evaluated in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Constants">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Constants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Numerical values that characterize the elastic behavior and stiffness of a material, such as Young's Modulus, Poisson's ratio, and Shear Modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Loading Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Complex Loading Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">States of stress involving multiple, simultaneous forces applied to a structure, necessitating the use of finite element analysis for accurate computation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999407</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page 17 - Elastizitätsgesetz Hooke's Model (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 17 - Elastizitätsgesetz Hooke's Model (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 17 of a German mechanics textbook containing section 12.2 on the Law of Elasticity. This page introduces Hooke's Model (Hookesches Modell) for linear elastic materials, noting that principal directions match the stress tensor and that the model applies in any Cartesian coordinate system. Builds upon the E-modulus/shear modulus relationship derived on page 15.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990147</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Elastic Materials">
  <data key="d0">Linear Elastic Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material for which a constitutive equation is introduced on page 17 of the engineering mechanics document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Equation">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation introduced on page 17 that relates stress and strain tensors, formulated in the principal axis system for linear elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Plane Stress with Principal Direction Transformation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Plane Stress with Principal Direction Transformation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Comprehensive presentation of the isotropic linear-elastic constitutive law for plane stress: compliance form (strains from stresses), stiffness form (stresses from strains with E/(1−ν²) factor), and Mohr's circle-based principal direction formula tan(2φ*). Foundational equation in structural mechanics enabling stress analysis, failure prediction, and FEM implementations within the broader elasticity theory framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990333</data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Constitutive Law For Plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Constitutive Law For Plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The linear-elastic constitutive law for isotropic, homogeneous materials under plane stress, presenting stress-strain relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stiffness Form">
  <data key="d0">Stiffness Form</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Part II of the equation expressing stresses as functions of strains, using the plane stress stiffness coefficient E/(1-ν²).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Directions Formula">
  <data key="d0">Principal Directions Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Part III of the equation determining the angle of the principal axes where normal stresses/strains reach extrema.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="3D Hooke's Law">
  <data key="d0">3D Hooke's Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The three-dimensional formulation of Hooke's law, from which the plane stress stiffness form is derived by eliminating the out-of-plane stress.&lt;SEP&gt;An advanced mechanics of materials concept discussed in the document, specifically related to thermal expansion (Elastizitätsgesetz).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db9317a3d674bad613adaf9f20eaf18d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Out-of-plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Out-of-plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress component, denoted as σ_z, which is zero under plane stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Changes">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Changes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variations in temperature, denoted as ΔT, that produce isotropic normal strains in materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Variations in temperature that, in the context of thermal expansion, induce normal strains but no shear strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Referred to as Temperaturänderung, this affects isotropic materials by producing only normal strains.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenomenon that causes thermal strain behavior in isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Coordinate Systems">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinate Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Arbitrary coordinate systems in which the plane stress formulation applies.&lt;SEP&gt;Arbitrary coordinate systems in which the model's validity was discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Component Design">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Component Design</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An engineering process utilizing the plane stress constitutive law for designing mechanical parts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biaxial Plate Derivation">
  <data key="d0">Biaxial Plate Derivation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A derivation process mentioned in the context where the relationship G = E/(2(1+ν)) connects the shear modulus to E and ν.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Plane Stress in Principal Axes (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Plane Stress in Principal Axes (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the principal normal strain ($\varepsilon_1$) in a biaxially loaded, isotropic material. It utilizes Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio to relate orthogonal principal stresses to directional strains, forming the basis of linear elasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990504</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Strain Equation">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Strain Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation representing a linear algebraic constitutive relation for a two-dimensional state of stress (plane stress), defining the principal normal strain in an isotropic, linearly elastic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuum Theory">
  <data key="d0">Continuum Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An application domain associated with solid mechanics where the formula is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A dimensionless variable ($\varepsilon_1$) in the constitutive equation representing the strain in the principal direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stress In Direction 1">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stress In Direction 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable ($\sigma_1$) representing the normal stress in direction 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stress In Orthogonal Direction 2">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stress In Orthogonal Direction 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable ($\sigma_2$) representing the normal stress in the orthogonal direction 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="3D Systems">
  <data key="d0">3D Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Three-dimensional spatial systems to which the foundational model is extended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensorial Representation">
  <data key="d0">Tensorial Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A full mathematical representation of stress and strain, bridged from basic uniaxial stress in the broader discussion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Stress">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A basic one-dimensional state of stress that is bridged to a full tensorial representation in the Law of Elasticity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5804dc5da5931340fcffd232bf52ac41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Principal Strain in 2D Isotropic Elasticity (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Principal Strain in 2D Isotropic Elasticity (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the normal strain in a principal direction for a linear elastic, isotropic material under plane stress. It accounts for both direct axial deformation and lateral contraction (Poisson effect). It serves as a fundamental part of the constitutive model used to derive relationships between elastic constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990616</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Equation (ε₂)">
  <data key="d0">Strain Equation (ε₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation ε₂ = (1/E)(σ₂ - ν·σ₁) that calculates normal strain in the second principal direction, representing a component of the generalized Hooke's Law for linear, isotropic, elastic materials formulated within the principal axis system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain (ε₂)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain (ε₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain in the second principal direction calculated by the strain equation, resulting from axial strain and transverse strain contributions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stresses (σ₁, σ₂)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stresses (σ₁, σ₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principal normal stresses acting in perpendicular principal directions, where σ₂ causes direct axial strain and σ₁ causes transverse strain</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="3D Hooke's Model">
  <data key="d0">3D Hooke's Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A temperature-dependent three-dimensional extension of the constitutive model that the strain equation sets the stage for, including the effects of thermal expansion&lt;SEP&gt;An extension of Hooke's Model discussed on page 18 that accounts for temperature changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear, Isotropic, Elastic Materials">
  <data key="d0">Linear, Isotropic, Elastic Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A class of materials whose mechanical behavior is described by the generalized Hooke's Law, specifically relating normal stresses and strains</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Model">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical framework describing how a material responds to applied forces, which the strain equation helps extend to three dimensions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Analogous Equations For Other Principal Directions">
  <data key="d0">Analogous Equations For Other Principal Directions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Equations such as $\varepsilon_1 = \frac{1}{E}(\sigma_1 - \nu\sigma_2)$ and $\varepsilon_3 = -\frac{\nu}{E}(\sigma_1 + \sigma_2)$ that typically accompany the main strain equation for plane stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7377bf43fb474164c26372581df6f053</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page 18 - Elastizitätsgesetz: 3D Hooke's Model with Temperature Effects (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 18 - Elastizitätsgesetz: 3D Hooke's Model with Temperature Effects (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 18 of a German technical document on elasticity laws. Extends Hooke's Model to 3D under temperature changes, establishing that isotropic materials under thermal loading experience only normal strains without shear deformation. Follows discussion of E-Modul/Schubmodul relationships and general Cartesian coordinate formulations from pages 16-17.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990734</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermoelastic Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Thermoelastic Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A critical extension from purely mechanical loading discussed within continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Axis Systems">
  <data key="d0">Principal Axis Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Axis systems in which the model's validity was discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehnungen">
  <data key="d0">Dehnungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German term for normal strains, produced in isotropic materials due to temperature changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gleitungen">
  <data key="d0">Gleitungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German term for shear strains, which are not produced by temperature changes in isotropic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999433</data>
