</node>
<node id="Bibliographic Citation: Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Bibliographic Citation: Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer serves as a bibliographic citation attributing the page's content to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' (2017) by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall, published by Springer Vieweg. It provides academic provenance for the surrounding discussion on 3D Hooke's Law and material elasticity under temperature changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f32b5361a87ed03fd6b5bde7c0bceb86</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784990841</data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Thermoelastic Constitutive Equation (Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Thermoelastic Constitutive Equation (Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The generalized Hooke's Law for isotropic materials under triaxial stress and thermal loading. It relates normal strain ε_x to principal stresses via Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν, plus a thermal expansion term α_T·ΔT. This constitutive law underpins subsequent failure analysis using equivalent stress concepts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784991337</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stress Components">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stress Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principal normal stress components σ_x, σ_y, σ_z (Hauptnormalspannungen) representing the triaxial stress state in the principal directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory Of Linear Thermoelasticity">
  <data key="d0">Theory Of Linear Thermoelasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The theoretical framework to which the constitutive equation belongs, combining mechanical and thermal effects in linear-elastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Stress">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The scalar equivalent stress σ_V (Vergleichsspannung) derived from the spatial stress state for comparison against the allowable stress in structural analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A scalar stress value ($\sigma_V$) derived from spatial stresses, used for structural integrity assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress (σ_v) formulated under uniaxial loading conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as σv, a calculated stress value used to determine safety factors against plastic deformation in complex 3D loaded components.&lt;SEP&gt;A single scalar stress value ($\sigma_v$) representing complex 3D loadings, computed from the squared differences of principal stresses and compared against material yield strength&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Vergleichsspannung (σ_v), a derived stress quantity whose derivation relates to internal energy and hydrostatic stress components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Failure Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Structural Failure Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The analysis of yielding or fracture prediction in loaded components, enabled by comparing equivalent stress against allowable stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999456</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Linear-Elastic Material">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Linear-Elastic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A three-dimensional material characterized by uniform properties in all directions and linear stress-strain behavior, for which the generalized Hooke's Law is formulated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999456</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triaxial Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Triaxial Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A three-dimensional state of stress involving principal normal stress components from which mechanical strain arises.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of stress involving three principal stresses (σ_1, σ_2, σ_3) to which the isotropic elastic material is subjected during the analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Axial Strain">
  <data key="d0">Direct Axial Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The strain component in the principal x-direction resulting directly from the axial stress σ_x.&lt;SEP&gt;The strain represented by the term $\frac{1}{E}\sigma_y$ resulting from uniaxial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The distribution of stresses in a three-dimensional component that must be reduced to a scalar equivalent stress for failure analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The three-dimensional distribution of stresses (räumlicher Spannungszustand) within loaded components.&lt;SEP&gt;A three-dimensional stress condition (räumlicher Spannungszustand) from which equivalent stress is derived.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-343794ade2b1d656c68e2c17454713f5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yielding">
  <data key="d0">Yielding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural failure mode characterized by the onset of plastic deformation, which can be predicted by structural failure analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The onset of plastic flow, which is governed solely by the energy required to change the material's shape per the distortion energy hypothesis.&lt;SEP&gt;A material behavior that begins when the second deviatoric stress invariant reaches a critical value, according to the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fracture Prediction">
  <data key="d0">Fracture Prediction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of forecasting structural failure or breaking in loaded components based on computed equivalent stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="3D Thermoelastic Hooke's Law for Normal Strain in the Y-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">3D Thermoelastic Hooke's Law for Normal Strain in the Y-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation calculates the normal strain in the y-direction for an isotropic material subjected to 3D stress and thermal variations. It superimposes mechanical deformation, governed by Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio, with thermal expansion. This allows for the evaluation of spatial stress states necessary for subsequent failure and equivalent stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784991530</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Strain In The Y-direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Strain In The Y-direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain ($\varepsilon_y$) defined as the linear superposition of mechanical and thermal strains in a specific Cartesian direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Difference">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Difference</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The change in temperature ($\Delta T$) that contributes to the thermal strain term in the constitutive relation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Three-Dimensional Thermoelastic Constitutive Relation">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Three-Dimensional Thermoelastic Constitutive Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical model (Hookesches Modell) defining the relationship between stress, strain, and temperature changes for a linear isotropic solid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Isotropic Solid">
  <data key="d0">Linear Isotropic Solid</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical material model exhibiting identical mechanical properties in all directions and obeying Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failure Hypotheses">
  <data key="d0">Failure Hypotheses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Theories (Festigkeitshypothesen) that reduce spatial stresses to a scalar equivalent stress to assess structural integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Theories (Festigkeitshypothesen) that reduce three-dimensional stress states to an equivalent scalar stress for comparison against allowable stress limits&lt;SEP&gt;Concepts introduced in Section 12.3 that motivate the reduction of complex 3D stress states to a scalar equivalent stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Theoretical criteria used for material assessment and predicting the failure of materials under stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8f3f8c72a4f9a9fdeabe99c8c8ba4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compliance Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Compliance Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical representation of isotropic material properties from which the mechanical strain term is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;A matrix used in Voigt notation that contains the off-diagonal shear block, mathematically representing the shear constitutive relations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Loading">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of loading condition that causes direct axial strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Orthogonal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Orthogonal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses acting in directions perpendicular to a given axis that cause transverse contraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="3D Strains">
  <data key="d0">3D Strains</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Spatial strains calculated to evaluate the stress state within loaded components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section 12.3">
  <data key="d0">Section 12.3</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific section within a document or framework where failure hypotheses are discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;A section of the textbook containing detailed theoretical derivations of strength hypotheses on pages 21-22.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427b2f7911554c401a806ff733b5975e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Structural Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assessment goal for which spatial stresses are reduced to a scalar equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95513f4440188d0181ac3f745cc0cfe1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain (z-component) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law with Thermal Strain (z-component) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The z-component of the generalized Hooke's Law for isotropic materials under combined mechanical and thermal loading. It calculates strain in the z-direction by superposing mechanical strain (including Poisson effects from transverse stresses) and thermal expansion strain, forming part of the constitutive law for 3D elasticity problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784991702</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain in Z-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Strain in Z-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The strain component in the z-direction, denoted as ε_z, in a three-dimensional isotropic elastic material</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Elastic Material">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Elastic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material with identical elastic properties in all directions, subject to both mechanical loading and thermal effects&lt;SEP&gt;A material with uniform elastic properties in all directions, subjected to a triaxial stress state, within which the hydrostatic strain energy density equation is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;A material subjected to a three-dimensional principal stress state that exhibits linear elastic stiffness and transverse strain response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Strain Contribution">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Strain Contribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The first additive term of the strain equation, representing strain due to mechanical loading, formulated as (1/E)(σ_z - ν[σ_x + σ_y])</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Strain Contribution">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Strain Contribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second additive term of the strain equation (α_T ΔT), representing strain due to thermal effects</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in Z-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in Z-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The direct normal stress in the z-direction, denoted as σ_z</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in X-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in X-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress in the x-direction (perpendicular to z), denoted as σ_x</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in Y-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in Y-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress in the y-direction (perpendicular to z), denoted as σ_y</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient of Thermal Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient of Thermal Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material property denoted as α_T (thermischer Ausdehnungskoeffizient), describing the thermal expansion characteristics of a material</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Scalar Stress">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Scalar Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A scalar stress value denoted as σ_V, used in failure hypotheses to represent a three-dimensional stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain in X-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Strain in X-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The strain component in the x-direction ($\varepsilon_x$), for which the generalized Hooke's Law has an analog equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain in Y-Direction">
  <data key="d0">Strain in Y-Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The strain component in the y-direction ($\varepsilon_y$), for which the generalized Hooke's Law has an analog equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allowable Stress Limits">
  <data key="d0">Allowable Stress Limits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum permissible stress values against which the equivalent scalar stress is compared to evaluate failure</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-650f3440dee3d2c3acd18a9d3ea4f7f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law Shear Stress-Strain Relations in 3D Isotropic Elasticity (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law Shear Stress-Strain Relations in 3D Isotropic Elasticity (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">These three relations form the shear component of the generalized Hooke's Law for isotropic linearly elastic materials, stating that each engineering shear strain γ_ij equals the corresponding shear stress τ_ij divided by the shear modulus G. They complement the normal-stress relations and confirm that thermal loading induces no shear strains in isotropic media, providing input for strength analyses via equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784991855</data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Stress">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as equivalent stress (Vergleichsspannung σ_V), it depends on the deviatoric components of the stress tensor and is used to predict yielding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Voigt Notation">
  <data key="d0">Voigt Notation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical notation method in which the relations constitute the off-diagonal shear block of the compliance matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lamé Second Parameter">
  <data key="d0">Lamé Second Parameter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material parameter, also denoted as μ or G, that characterizes a material's resistance to shear deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page 19 - Transition from Elasticity Law to Failure Hypotheses (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 19 - Transition from Elasticity Law to Failure Hypotheses (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 19 of a German mechanics textbook bridges section 12.2 (Hooke's Law under thermal effects) and introduces section 12.3 (Failure Hypotheses). It establishes the critical concept of equivalent stress (Vergleichsspannung) for reducing 3D stress states to scalar values comparable with uniaxial material strength limits, forming the theoretical basis for structural failure analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784991948</data>
</node>
<node id="German-language Engineering Mechanics Textbook">
  <data key="d0">German-language Engineering Mechanics Textbook</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A textbook containing foundational concepts of continuum mechanics and strength of materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f4c091fae29a6bd21771fd542eb9801</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999464</data>
