<node id="Shear Stress Hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Hypothesis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A classical strength hypothesis, also known as Tresca, used for ductile materials.&lt;SEP&gt;An additional strength hypothesis likely detailed in the subsequent treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A theory positing that material failure is determined by the maximum shear stress value.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Schubspannungshypothese, it is a strength hypothesis (Festigkeitshypothesen) stating that material yielding or failure initiates when the shear stress reaches a critical threshold.&lt;SEP&gt;A strength hypothesis, originally referred to as 'Schubspannungshypothese', discussed within technical mechanics documentation.&lt;SEP&gt;A hypothesis from which the page marks a transition to the distortion energy theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-343794ade2b1d656c68e2c17454713f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a498a78500a61b206c4b1205b49030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5103d6213d27573d3ccf1c58deb496d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6</data>
