<node id="Distortion Energy Hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">Distortion Energy Hypothesis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Distortion Energy Hypothesis, also widely known as the von Mises yield criterion or "Hypothese der Gestaltänderungsenergie", is a fundamental failure theory and strength hypothesis used in continuum mechanics to predict material yielding and failure. The core principle of this hypothesis states that plastic deformation and yielding in a material are governed solely by the energy required to change the material's shape. Specifically, it is a theory of strength that directly relates a material's yielding behavior to its distortional strain energy.

From a mechanical perspective, the hypothesis involves the calculation and application of principal stresses, principal strains, and equivalent stress. Its mathematical derivation is typically achieved by substituting given uniaxial stresses or principal strains into the strain energy density expression. This analytical framework is comprehensively derived and presented in technical literature, notably within academic treatments such as "Technische Mechanik 2".

Within the broader context of material science, the Distortion Energy Hypothesis serves as a critical point of comparison and evolution among failure theories. It is frequently evaluated against the shear stress hypothesis, representing a transition and refinement from the maximum shear stress hypothesis, commonly known as the Tresca criterion. By focusing on distortion energy rather than maximum shear stress alone, the hypothesis provides a highly reliable model for understanding and predicting complex material failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1a498a78500a61b206c4b1205b49030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999614</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technische Mechanik 2 Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Technische Mechanik 2 Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer serves as a bibliographic citation, attributing the page's content to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017). It provides academic provenance for the surrounding engineering concepts, specifically solid mechanics topics like Hooke's Law and strength hypotheses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db9317a3d674bad613adaf9f20eaf18d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784992487</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Hypothesis (Normalspannungshypothese) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Hypothesis (Normalspannungshypothese) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation σ_V = σ_1 defines the equivalent stress as the maximum principal stress, representing Rankine's maximum normal stress failure criterion. It reduces a complex spatial stress state to a single scalar value for comparison with uniaxial test data, providing a conservative failure prediction particularly suitable for brittle materials where tensile fracture dominates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f8a4b00ec16f808ae6a690cd67bf329</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784992652</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 21 - Schubspannungshypothese Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 21 - Schubspannungshypothese Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 21 located at bottom of a mechanics textbook page covering section 12.3 on strength hypotheses. This page presents the second of three hypotheses: the shear stress hypothesis (Schubspannungshypothese/Tresca), which characterizes material stress through maximum shear stress, and begins analysis of the uniaxial tensile test as a reference case for comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993144</data>
</node>
<node id="Elasticitätsspannungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Elasticitätsspannungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastic stress state (ESZ), an assumption under which the three main strength hypotheses are discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d57d772e71ccd830b12050844ec9eef2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
