</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Diagram for Maximum Shear Stress (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Diagram for Maximum Shear Stress (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a Mohr's circle diagram illustrating the relationship between principal stresses (σ₁, σ₂) and the maximum shear stress (τ_max). It serves as a visual aid for the Shear Stress Hypothesis (Tresca criterion), one of the three strength hypotheses discussed in the text. The diagram clearly shows that the maximum shear stress corresponds to the radius of the circle formed by the principal stresses on the normal stress axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16cb12dda8d30577bdf35b6755d86117</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993319</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Stress Equation (Tresca Criterion) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress Equation (Tresca Criterion) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the maximum shear stress as half the difference between principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂, forming the mathematical foundation of the Tresca yield criterion (Schubspannungshypothese). It connects principal stress states to shear-driven plastic deformation, particularly relevant for ductile materials in the context of strength hypothesis comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993404</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Stress Equation">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation τ_max = (σ₁ - σ₂)/2 representing the maximum shear stress in a two-dimensional stress state in terms of principal normal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Principal Normal Stress (σ₁)">
  <data key="d0">First Principal Normal Stress (σ₁)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The first principal normal stress, representing the maximum principal stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Principal Normal Stress (σ₂)">
  <data key="d0">Second Principal Normal Stress (σ₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second principal normal stress, representing the minimum principal stress in the 2D case.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Tensile Test">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tensile Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing consideration where σ₁ equals equivalent stress and σ₂ equals 0, yielding τ_max = σ_v/2.&lt;SEP&gt;A standardized simple test used to determine the material's yield strength under one-dimensional load.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as einachsiger Zugversuch, it is a standard material test from which the equation $\tau_{max} = \frac{\sigma_V}{2}$ is derived by analyzing the stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical test whose energy state is equated to the distortional energy to establish the yield boundary.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental material testing procedure used to determine the energy and stress at which a material yields under a single axial load.&lt;SEP&gt;A simple material testing method used to establish the baseline equivalent stress for predicting material yielding.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical test used to establish the distortion energy threshold where principal stresses are set such that sigma_1 = sigma_v and sigma_2 = sigma_3 = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Stress (σ_v)">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Stress (σ_v)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress value that the first principal normal stress equals during a uniaxial tensile test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Stress (σ_y)">
  <data key="d0">Yield Stress (σ_y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress value at which yielding occurs, where equivalent stress equals yield stress, making the critical shear stress σ_y/2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The process that begins when the maximum shear stress within a material reaches a critical value.&lt;SEP&gt;A deformation process that only the deviatoric (shape-changing) portion of energy contributes to.&lt;SEP&gt;A permanent change in the material's shape that, along with yielding, is governed solely by the distortion energy according to the distortion energy hypothesis.&lt;SEP&gt;A permanent change in the shape of a material, against which safety factors are determined using equivalent stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A permanent type of deformation that is not caused by hydrostatic stress but is driven by deviatoric stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Irreversible deformation occurring when a ductile material yields, the onset of which is defined mathematically by the yield criterion&lt;SEP&gt;A permanent change in shape that the von Mises yield criterion helps predict in complex, multi-axial loading scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Shear Stress (τ_critical)">
  <data key="d0">Critical Shear Stress (τ_critical)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The threshold value of shear stress at which yielding occurs, calculated as half of the yield stress (σ_y/2).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plasticity Theory">
  <data key="d0">Plasticity Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of study in mechanics where the maximum shear stress equation is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af588c739fd8cf2b760927ad23d5c9f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999477</data>
