</node>
<node id="Von Mises Yield Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Yield Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Von Mises Yield Criterion is a foundational failure theory and yield criterion used to predict the yielding of ductile materials. It is recognized by several alternate names across different contexts and languages, including the Distortion Energy Hypothesis, the maximum distortion energy theory, the Huber-von Mises-Hencky theory, Hypothese der Gestaltänderungsenergie, and Vergleichsspannung. 

The theoretical framework of the Von Mises Yield Criterion is anchored in energy principles, specifically postulating that hydrostatic stress states do not cause yielding. Instead, yielding is driven solely by the deviatoric, or shape-changing, portion of the stress, which is responsible for plastic deformation. The criterion is formulated by isolating this distortional energy from the material's total internal strain energy. Fundamentally, it dictates that yielding begins when the second deviatoric stress invariant reaches a critical value.

To evaluate complex, multi-axial stress states, the criterion is derived by equating the distortion energy of a complex 3D stress state to that of a simple uniaxial tensile test state ($\sigma_1 = \sigma_v, \sigma_2 = \sigma_3 = 0$). By calculating the effective distortion energy and determining an equivalent uniaxial stress, the theory provides a highly effective comparative metric for material failure. The detailed mathematical derivation of this framework is historically well-established and routinely anchored by foundational mechanics literature, such as the content found on Page 22 of corresponding reference texts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortion Energy (Uf)">
  <data key="d0">Distortion Energy (Uf)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy associated with the change in shape of a material, isolated by subtracting hydrostatic energy from total internal energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Energy (Uh)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Energy (Uh)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy associated with the volumetric change of a material under stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Internal Energy (U)">
  <data key="d0">Total Internal Energy (U)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The overall energy stored within a material, comprising both distortion and hydrostatic energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Distortion Energy Equation">
  <data key="d0">General Distortion Energy Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical formula representing distortion energy in a general three-dimensional stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A general state of stress in three dimensions, equated to a uniaxial stress state to derive equivalent stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A general state of stress in continuum mechanics characterized by three principal stresses, whose distortion energy is equated with a uniaxial tensile stress state to formulate an equivalent stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Axial Stress States">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Axial Stress States</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex stress conditions acting on a mechanical component across multiple directions.&lt;SEP&gt;Complex stress conditions in a material that are compared directly to uniaxial material properties using the derived mathematical relationship to predict structural failure.&lt;SEP&gt;Complex stress states in mechanical engineering evaluated using strength hypotheses like the von Mises and Tresca criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stresses represented by the variables $\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$.&lt;SEP&gt;The three principal normal stresses (σ₁, σ₂, σ₃) acting along the orthogonal principal directions in a three-dimensional stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as σ1, σ2, and σ3, these are the eigenvalues of the stress tensor acting on planes where shear stress is zero.&lt;SEP&gt;The variables $\sigma_1$, $\sigma_2$, and $\sigma_3$ representing the eigenvalues of the Cauchy stress tensor acting at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineers">
  <data key="d0">Engineers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Professionals who use equivalent stress to compare complex stress states against material yield strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
