<node id="Maximum Shear Stress Relation (Tresca Criterion) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress Relation (Tresca Criterion) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the maximum shear stress ($\tau_{max}$) as half of the equivalent uniaxial tensile stress ($\sigma_V$). It forms the mathematical basis for the shear stress hypothesis (Tresca yield criterion), linking complex multi-axial stress states to the uniaxial tensile test to predict yielding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993639</data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Uniaxial Tensile Stress">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Uniaxial Tensile Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also denoted as $\sigma_V$, it represents the equivalent uniaxial tensile stress used to compare complex multi-axial stress states directly to uniaxial material properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory of Plasticity">
  <data key="d0">Theory of Plasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sub-domain of material science and continuum mechanics concerned with the behavior of materials undergoing permanent deformation, where the shear stress equation is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation $\tau_{max} = \frac{\sigma_V}{2}$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $\tau_{max} = \frac{\sigma_V}{2}$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical formula that establishes a fundamental scalar relationship between the maximum shear stress and the equivalent uniaxial tensile stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Material Properties">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Material Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material characteristics derived from a uniaxial stress state, against which complex multi-axial stress states are evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;Material properties derived from a single axis of stress, such as yield strength from a tensile test</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-154d19114c3f301c7c7a909d141ce692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e4734b30eabf1c888d7ceb2c9dd8d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Yield Criterion Label (image)">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Yield Criterion Label (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a blue label containing the text 'Tresca (1864)'. It serves as a visual identifier for the Tresca yield criterion, also known as the Shear Stress Hypothesis ('Schubspannungshypothese'), within a lecture on strength hypotheses ('Festigkeitshypothesen'). The label marks the section explaining that material yielding is determined by the maximum shear stress, a fundamental concept in technical mechanics and material science.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5103d6213d27573d3ccf1c58deb496d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784993716</data>
</node>
