</node>
<node id="Section 12.3 'Festigkeitshypothesen'">
  <data key="d0">Section 12.3 'Festigkeitshypothesen'</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A section in the German Technical Mechanics document focusing on Strength Hypotheses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortion Energy Theory">
  <data key="d0">Distortion Energy Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex theory presenting mathematical derivations involving principal stresses and energy components.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as "Hypothese der Gestaltänderungsenergie", it is a framework defining material failure based on distortional energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strains">
  <data key="d0">Strains</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deformations (ε₁, ε₂, ε₃) involved in the mathematical derivations.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical formulations associated with the derivation of the Distortion Energy Hypothesis and equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Energy">
  <data key="d0">Internal Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy (U) involved in the mathematical derivations.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as U, a component of material behavior whose breakdown relates to the derivation of equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress Components">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress components (U_h) involved in the mathematical derivations.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress components that only change the volume of a material and do not induce plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-00a305b8793d9f114323bcd40953cba6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technische Mechanik 2 Source Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Technische Mechanik 2 Source Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer serves as a bibliographic citation attributing the mathematical derivations and theoretical explanations on the page to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017). It supports the academic validity of the content detailing the Distortion Energy Hypothesis (Gestaltänderungsenergie) and equivalent stress calculations within the broader discussion of failure criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784994266</data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also referred to as ESZ, a stress state context within which failure criteria (Festigkeitshypothesen) are discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8557dd78e46c36e30dd3fe520b64408c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law in Principal Coordinates (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law in Principal Coordinates (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This constitutive equation calculates the principal strain ($\varepsilon_1$) from the principal stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$) using Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio. In the surrounding context, it provides the mathematical substitution necessary to derive the distortion energy hypothesis (von Mises yield criterion) by expressing strain energy strictly in terms of stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa696301ae87e3f1491d0bc4e02b28bd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784994367</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Strain Component Epsilon 2">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strain Component Epsilon 2</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal strain in the second principal direction (ε₂), computed as a linear function of the three principal normal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Internal Strain Energy">
  <data key="d0">Total Internal Strain Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total strain energy U of a material, expressed entirely in terms of principal stresses through substitution of strain components.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as U, the total internal strain energy within an isotropic elastic material that can be decomposed into distortional energy and hydrostatic energy components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortional Energy">
  <data key="d0">Distortional Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy component U_f associated with shape change, isolated by subtracting the hydrostatic energy component from the total internal strain energy.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as U_f, it is the effective distortion energy causing shape changes and responsible for plastic yielding, calculated as U_f = U - U_h by subtracting the hydrostatic strain energy density from the total internal strain energy.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as $U_f$, it is the component of total strain energy responsible for shape change (distortion), isolated by subtracting hydrostatic energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Energy Component">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Energy Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy component U_h associated with volumetric (hydrostatic) deformation, subtracted from total energy to obtain distortional energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The volumetric component (denoted U_h) of the total strain energy density, representing the hydrostatic portion that is subtracted from the total energy U.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Substitution Von Epsilon 1, Epsilon 2 Und Epsilon 3">
  <data key="d0">Substitution Von Epsilon 1, Epsilon 2 Und Epsilon 3</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An explicit algebraic step in Technische Mechanik where principal strains are replaced to express total internal strain energy entirely in terms of principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalar Tensor Invariants">
  <data key="d0">Scalar Tensor Invariants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical properties on which the fundamental relation of Hooke's Law operates purely, despite the presence of vector or bold formatting symbols in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nabla Symbol">
  <data key="d0">Nabla Symbol</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical symbol ($\vec{\nabla}$) present in the provided equation, noted as likely being a typographical or OCR artifact since the relation operates on scalar invariants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a375818483fffe9d5cdc368f45b60b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Strain Hooke's Law (Third Principal Direction) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strain Hooke's Law (Third Principal Direction) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation is the generalized Hooke's law expressing principal strain ε₃ in terms of the three principal stresses σ₁, σ₂, σ₃ using Young's modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν. It is used as a substitution step in deriving the distortion energy hypothesis (Gestaltänderungsenergiehypothese) by expressing strains needed to compute the total, hydrostatic, and deviatoric strain energy components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784995067</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Energy Density">
  <data key="d0">Strain Energy Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An expression U = ½(σ₁ε₁ + σ₂ε₂ + σ₃ε₃) into which principal strains are substituted to separate total energy into hydrostatic and deviatoric components.&lt;SEP&gt;The total elastic potential per unit volume (denoted U) for a linearly elastic, isotropic continuum under triaxial stress, defined as half the scalar dot product of the principal stress vector and principal strain vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Component">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The volume-changing portion of the total strain energy, derived from the strain energy density.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Component">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shape-changing portion of the total strain energy (U_f), which contributes to plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Elastic Isotropic Material">
  <data key="d0">Linear Elastic Isotropic Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material whose mechanical behavior is described by the generalized Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Form">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Form</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A formulation of the generalized Hooke's law presented as ε_x = (1/E)(σ_x - ν[σ_y + σ_z]) in a Cartesian coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Vector">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector of principal stresses (σ1, σ2, σ3) used in the principal coordinate system to formulate the strain energy density as a simplified scalar dot product.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Strain Vector">
  <data key="d0">Principal Strain Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector of principal strains (ε1, ε2, ε3) used in the principal coordinate system to formulate the strain energy density as a simplified scalar dot product.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortion Energy">
  <data key="d0">Distortion Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shape-changing component (denoted U_f) of the total strain energy density, derived by subtracting the hydrostatic portion U_h from the total energy U; governs plastic deformation and yielding per the distortion energy hypothesis.&lt;SEP&gt;The energy ($U_f$) required to change the shape of a material, isolated from total internal energy by subtracting the hydrostatic volume change energy ($U_h$)&lt;SEP&gt;The energy required to change the shape of a material, as opposed to its volume. Yielding of a ductile material begins when this energy reaches a critical threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isotropic Continuum">
  <data key="d0">Isotropic Continuum</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A linearly elastic material with direction-independent properties, subjected to a triaxial state of stress, for which the strain energy density equation is defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Stress-Strain Curve">
  <data key="d0">Linear Stress-Strain Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical representation of the linear relationship between stress and strain; the area under this curve corresponds to the 1/2 coefficient in the strain energy equation derived from Hooke's Law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triaxial State Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Triaxial State Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress state characterized by three principal stresses, to which the isotropic continuum is subjected.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f89ffe71648491b9b2c362db33adecfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triaxial Total Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Triaxial Total Strain Energy Density Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the total elastic strain energy per unit volume in an isotropic 3D body subjected to principal stresses. It forms the mathematical basis for deriving the distortion energy ($U_f$) by subtracting the hydrostatic energy component ($U_h$), a critical step in formulating the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784995319</data>
</node>
<node id="U">
  <data key="d0">U</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Total elastic strain energy density, representing the elastic potential energy stored in a material per unit of volume.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ν">
  <data key="d0">ν</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Poisson's ratio, characterizing the transverse mechanical strain response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Volumetric Energy Component">
  <data key="d0">Volumetric Energy Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as Uh, it is the hydrostatic component of the total elastic strain energy which does not cause yielding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortional Energy Component">
  <data key="d0">Distortional Energy Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as Uf, it is the shape-changing component of the total elastic strain energy isolated to establish the yield boundary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Potential Energy Integral">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Potential Energy Integral</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental integral $U = \frac{1}{2} \int \sigma : d\varepsilon$ from which the specific elastic strain energy equation is derived.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Strength Theories">
  <data key="d0">Material Strength Theories</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Festigkeitshypothesen, these are theories within solid mechanics where the equation is applied to predict failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Tensor Algebra">
  <data key="d0">Linear Tensor Algebra</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical field intrinsically linked to the equation through the use of eigenvalues and the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Boundary">
  <data key="d0">Yield Boundary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The limit of stress or energy that establishes the onset of yielding in a material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses calculated as $s_i = \sigma_i - \sigma$ that drive shape changes (distortion) and are central to the von Mises yield criterion.&lt;SEP&gt;Shape-changing stresses that solely cause yielding or plastic deformation in ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Volumetric Changes">
  <data key="d0">Volumetric Changes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also called dilatation, changes in volume caused by the hydrostatic component of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shape Changes">
  <data key="d0">Shape Changes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also called distortion, changes in the shape of a material driven by deviatoric stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arithmetic Mean">
  <data key="d0">Arithmetic Mean</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical operation used to calculate the single scalar value ($\sigma$) from the principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Continuum">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Continuum</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial and physical context within which the hydrostatic stress state and mean normal stress are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dilatation">
  <data key="d0">Dilatation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Another term for volumetric changes caused by hydrostatic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as volumetric deformation, it is the change in volume of a deformable body without a change in shape.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortion">
  <data key="d0">Distortion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Another term for shape changes driven by deviatoric stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Trace">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Trace</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The trace of the total stress tensor, denoted as $\text{Tr}(\boldsymbol{\sigma})$, used to calculate the hydrostatic stress component.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as U_h, it represents the energy stored purely due to volume dilation in an isotropic elastic material, calculated as (1-2ν)/(2E) · 3σ². Ductile materials do not yield under this hydrostatic component because it produces no shear stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Also denoted as $U_h$, it is the component of the total internal mechanical energy in a deformable body responsible for volumetric deformation (dilatation) without causing distortion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Tensile Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tensile Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress state characterized by σ_1 = σ_v, σ_2 = σ_3 = 0, which is equated to the effective distortion energy to formulate the von Mises yield criterion.&lt;SEP&gt;A simple 1D stress state ($\sigma_v$) used to determine material yield strength, whose distortion energy is equated with the general 3D stress state&lt;SEP&gt;A specific state of stress used as a reference to equate the distortion energy of a general three-dimensional stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Volume Dilation">
  <data key="d0">Volume Dilation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The volumetric change in a material; the hydrostatic strain energy density U_h represents the energy stored purely due to this volume dilation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Energy">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of the total internal strain energy that specifically causes volumetric changes within the material, corresponding to U_h.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Constitutive Equations For Internal Energy">
  <data key="d0">General Constitutive Equations For Internal Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The general equations into which the hydrostatic stress is substituted to algebraically isolate the purely volumetric component.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bulk Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Bulk Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as $K$, it is a material property used to express the hydrostatic strain energy density concisely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Failure Theory">
  <data key="d0">Material Failure Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Theories used to predict the yielding or failure of materials, such as the Distortion Energy Theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Equivalent Stress">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Equivalent Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as $\sigma_v$, it is a scalar stress value calculated using the hydrostatic strain energy density formulation to predict yielding.&lt;SEP&gt;An equivalent stress (Vergleichsspannung) derived from the Distortion Energy Hypothesis, establishing a yield criterion for ductile materials by equating distortion energy of a general 3D stress state with that of a uniaxial tensile stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Strain Energy">
  <data key="d0">Total Strain Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as $U$, it is the overall internal mechanical energy stored in a deformable body, decomposed into volumetric and distortional components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Stress">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as shear stress, it is the stress component responsible for change in shape (distortion) and causes yielding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c347d0d02f605dcaeeba6088d212ddc8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density ($U_h$)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density ($U_h$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The portion of total internal mechanical energy responsible for volumetric changes (compression or dilation) without changing the material's shape.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Strain Energy ($U$)">
  <data key="d0">Total Strain Energy ($U$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total internal mechanical energy stored within a material, which can be decomposed into volumetric and distortional components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortional Strain Energy ($U_f$)">
  <data key="d0">Distortional Strain Energy ($U_f$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The component of total strain energy responsible for changing the material's shape (distortion), equated to uniaxial equivalent stress to predict failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poisson's Ratio ($\nu$)">
  <data key="d0">Poisson's Ratio ($\nu$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material constant characterizing the transverse strain response of an elastic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Normal Stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Normal Stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses acting orthogonally to each other within a three-dimensional stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalent Stress ($\sigma_v$)">
  <data key="d0">Equivalent Stress ($\sigma_v$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An uniaxial equivalent stress used to predict material failure by equating it to distortion energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The actual intended variable behind the OCR misrecognition, also known as Vergleichsspannung, used to predict material yielding under multi-axial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastoplasticity">
  <data key="d0">Elastoplasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of study where the calculation of hydrostatic strain energy density is profoundly significant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-axial Stress States">
  <data key="d0">Multi-axial Stress States</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex stress states evaluated in finite element analysis and structural engineering using equations like hydrostatic strain energy density.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Limit">
  <data key="d0">Yield Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum stress threshold that equivalent stress must remain below to ensure mechanical components are designed safely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corrupted OCR of Von Mises Yield Criterion Derivation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Corrupted OCR of Von Mises Yield Criterion Derivation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A malformed equation representing the 'equating' step in deriving the Von Mises equivalent stress from the distortion energy hypothesis. The angle symbols are OCR errors for principal stresses ($\sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3$) and equivalent stress ($\sigma_v$), utilized to predict yielding in ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784997015</data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Hypothesis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum shear stress hypothesis for predicting material failure, serving as the predecessor to the distortion energy hypothesis (Von Mises)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Yield Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical condition defining the onset of plastic (irreversible) deformation in ductile materials, established by the Von Mises equivalent stress equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The average of principal stresses ($\sigma = \frac{\sigma_1 + \sigma_2 + \sigma_3}{3}$) representing the hydrostatic stress component, substituted into internal energy equations</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Internal Energy">
  <data key="d0">Total Internal Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total strain energy ($U$) stored in a material under stress, from which distortion energy is separated by subtracting hydrostatic volume change energy</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Volume Change Energy">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Volume Change Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy ($U_h$) associated with hydrostatic volume change, subtracted from total internal energy ($U$) to isolate distortion energy ($U_f$)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Stress Components">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Stress Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shape-changing stress components separated from the hydrostatic (volume-changing) components in the derivation of equivalent stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometric Angles">
  <data key="d0">Geometric Angles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical angles that appear literally in the severely corrupted equation, serving as OCR misreadings of the principal stresses and equivalent stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2ada8da0499542e52dd4b0404a4d97</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OCR-Corrupted Equivalent Stress (von Mises) Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">OCR-Corrupted Equivalent Stress (von Mises) Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation mathematically defines a scalar variable $\psi_i$ and its cubic form, but represents a heavily OCR-corrupted derivation of the von Mises equivalent stress ($\sigma_v^2$). In the context of the distortion energy hypothesis, it serves as the transitional step equating complex 3D stress states to a uniaxial equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784997215</data>
</node>
<node id="Variable $\psi_i$">
  <data key="d0">Variable $\psi_i$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A scalar variable presented in the analyzed equation, identified as an OCR misrecognition of the equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distortion Energy ($U_f$)">
  <data key="d0">Distortion Energy ($U_f$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The energy calculated by subtracting the hydrostatic stress components from the total internal energy of an elastic body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Energy ($U$)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Energy ($U$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total energy within an elastic body, which is proportional to the square of the stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress Components ($U_h$)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress Components ($U_h$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress components subtracted from the total internal energy to determine the distortion energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7593a3adc530882efa765d69efe9aa0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784999493</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
