<edge source="Young's Modulus" target="Shear Modulus">
  <data key="d8">17.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relationship between Shear Modulus and Young's Modulus describes the mathematical interdependence between these two fundamental elastic material properties. The Shear Modulus (G) is not an independent variable but is mathematically derived from Young's Modulus (E) and Poisson's ratio (ν). This constitutive relationship is classically expressed by the rational formula G = E / [2(1+ν)]. This equation establishes that a material's resistance to shear deformation is intrinsically linked to its stiffness in tension or compression, alongside its lateral expansion characteristics.

The derivation of this formula is established through the analysis of strain transformations, shear deformations, and biaxial stress states. By utilizing technical schematic diagrams to provide a geometric basis, the Shear Modulus can be isolated and expressed directly in terms of Young's Modulus. This derivation process serves to explicitly demonstrate the fundamental interdependence of elastic constants within continuum mechanics. 

In technical and academic documentation, this relationship is a standard topic in material science. For instance, the formal derivation of the relationship between these properties—occasionally referred to in technical literature by their German equivalents, "E-Modul" for Young's modulus and "Schubmodul" for Shear Modulus—is systematically introduced and developed across standard engineering texts, with specific derivations historically documented across sequential pages, such as pages 15 and 16 of referenced materials.</data>
  <data key="d10">Depends on,Relates to,calculated,constitutive relationship,correlation,dependency,derivable,derivation,derived from,elastic constants,formula,interdependent,mathematical relationship,proportional relationship,related to,relationship,relationship derived</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-9d473000f8b85e21224cdb4fbff1c039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e5bb0f8636d66563ae50a1af38ba22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a50d9bf013c0aaad48cb4e73c069c450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ce85f9ca143dc31ac04d4214fb77217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73b7a69e161212c37519887b41d07b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5deab09d1c9d30fa6463bb3156b4278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce2fbc89309c5fa28f062356d8fd0dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90b46de3a3db50a2d58052925f2088b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f286bbba14a03f81052794f20378e5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926a4442fa510b596bda13f5de11afb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-529957e83da207a93efc376cc085ff93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e81d879c9812c155c5e161dfb7c5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-963728870ca7c1f5d880eae3e1e47f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4640da85a6d88df2121f23eab1d68369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd5ab7377144955c4c6d31264ee5359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718443b278fc05d9b29615dd32329bd6</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999840</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
