<edge source="Normal Stress σ(x)" target="σ(x=0) = 4·σ(x=l)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation evaluates the normal stress function σ(x) at the two boundary points of the conical bar, establishing a quantitative 4:1 stress ratio showing stress at the narrower end is four times greater than at the wider end.</data>
  <data key="d10">evaluates,stress ratio</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897128</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress σ(x)" target="Internal Normal Force N(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Normal stress is defined as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), the ratio of internal normal force to cross-sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d10">defined as,ratio</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897128</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress σ(x)" target="Cross-sectional Area A(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The stress distribution is inversely proportional to the cross-sectional area A(x), as the 4:1 stress ratio corresponds to the 4:1 area ratio between the ends.</data>
  <data key="d10">inversely proportional</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897128</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress σ(x)" target="Solid Mechanics">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">In solid mechanics, normal stress is defined as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), providing the theoretical framework for stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d10">defines,theoretical framework</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
