<edge source="x" target="Differential Segment">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The variable x indicates the distance from the left origin to the left boundary of the green differential segment.</data>
  <data key="d10">measures distance to</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897129</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="x" target="N(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The internal normal force N(x) decreases linearly from G at the support (x=0) to zero at the free end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d10">decreases linearly,varies with</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897130</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="x" target="σ(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The axial stress σ(x) varies linearly with position x, being maximum (G/A) at x=0 and zero at x=l.</data>
  <data key="d10">linear,varies with</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897130</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="x" target="First-Order Taylor Series Approximation of Displacement (equation)">
  <data key="d9">Entity x belongs to First-Order Taylor Series Approximation of Displacement (equation)&lt;SEP&gt;Entity x belongs to First-Order Taylor Series Approximation of Displacement (equation)</data>
  <data key="d10">belongs_to,contained_in,part_of</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-afdf7262993e514ad26ba97ab4f2a420</data>
  <data key="d8">20.0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999720</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="A(x)" target="σ(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The normal stress distribution σ(x) is calculated by dividing the internal force N(x) by the cross-sectional area A(x).</data>
  <data key="d10">computed from,depends on</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897127</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="σ(x)" target="N(x)">
  <data key="d8">2.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The normal stress distribution σ(x) depends on the constant compressive internal force N(x).&lt;SEP&gt;The axial stress σ(x) is defined as the ratio of internal normal force N(x) to cross-sectional area A (σ = N(x)/A).</data>
  <data key="d10">computed from,depends on,ratio of</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897127</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="σ(x)" target="Axial Stress Distribution Equation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation defines the axial stress distribution σ(x) = N(x)/A = (G/A)(1 - x/l) along the hanging rod.</data>
  <data key="d10">defines,describes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897130</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="N(x)" target="Equation dN/dx + n = 0">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation describes the differential equilibrium condition in which the spatial gradient of the normal force N(x) must exactly balance the applied distributed load.</data>
  <data key="d10">describes,involves</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784897128</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="N(x)" target="Stress Definition">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The stress definition involves the internal normal force N(x).</data>
  <data key="d10">involves,uses</data>
