<edge source="Stress Tensor" target="Strain Tensor">
  <data key="d8">9.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relationship between the strain tensor and the stress tensor is fundamentally characterized by strong mathematical, structural, and conceptual analogies. In continuum mechanics, both tensors share the same symmetric matrix form, which includes off-diagonal shear terms. Specifically, the formulation of the strain tensor is directly analogous to that of the stress tensor. Mathematically, the normal strain components ($\epsilon_x, \epsilon_y$) correspond to the normal stress components ($\sigma_x, \sigma_y$), and half of the engineering shear strain ($1/2\gamma_{xy}$) corresponds directly to the shear stress component ($\tau_{xy}$). 

This mathematical and structural analogy extends into their practical applications, particularly during stress state and plane strain analyses. Because of their parallel properties, the principal directions of the strain tensor perfectly align with those of the stress tensor when analyzing linear elastic materials. 

Beyond their conceptual and mathematical similarities, the strain tensor and stress tensor share a direct physical and mechanical connection. They are fundamentally linked to one another by the constitutive equation, commonly known as the Generalized Hooke's Law, which defines the relationship between stress and strain within a continuous material.</data>
  <data key="d10">Analogy,alignment,analogous to,analogy,connected by,mathematical structure,principal directions,related through,shares properties,structurally similar</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-3c0ebf6d69a9ef72ba2950206908d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa48b17598f3ede3e70d188e862d9e1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86444d41dc2e58cbf77ceb58838e96c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7992b48b195836a401d5e2640f4cca33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca4d23260501c2edf0dd05f9db78a075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e750891ac5c8d915fb4ae25dceeef48b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddbe1099c718b59c4f95b165b71f72ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef2929c1e9ff7a4d28ae04567e99b7f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f3dbcdc7efa91f1ffd56d0596ff25b</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999769</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
