<edge source="Circumferential Stress" target="Longitudinal Stress">
  <data key="d8">4.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The circumferential stress σ_φ is equal to twice the longitudinal stress σ_x (σ_φ = 2σ_x), explaining why pressure vessels typically fail by longitudinal cracking.&lt;SEP&gt;The longitudinal stress σx and circumferential stress σφ are orthogonal normal stresses forming a biaxial stress state with no shear stresses, indicating they are both principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;In a cylindrical vessel, the circumferential stress is twice the longitudinal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The circumferential stress is exactly twice the longitudinal stress due to force equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d10">comparison,equals,force equilibrium,orthogonal,principal stresses,twice</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784904247</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Circumferential Stress" target="Vertical Force Equilibrium">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Vertical force equilibrium in panel d is fundamental for deriving the relationship between internal pressure and hoop stress σφ.</data>
  <data key="d10">derives,determines</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784904247</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Circumferential Stress" target="Longitudinal Cracking">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Longitudinal cracking occurs because failure happens perpendicular to the larger principal stress, which is the circumferential stress.</data>
  <data key="d10">caused by,perpendicular to</data>
