  <data key="d13">1784999734</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Equation $u_Q = u_P + (\partial u/\partial x)dx + (\partial u/\partial y)dy$">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The displacement approximation equation for point Q is derived using the Taylor series expansion approach.</data>
  <data key="d10">derived using</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999716</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Geometrically Linear Theory">
  <data key="d8">2.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion approximation neglects higher-order terms under the assumption of small deformations known as the geometrically linear theory.&lt;SEP&gt;The Geometrically Linear Theory is yielded by neglecting higher-order terms in the Taylor Series Expansion.</data>
  <data key="d10">assumes,neglects,yielded by</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999717</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Strain State Analysis">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion serves as the analytical bridge between the kinematic description of deformation and the strain state analysis.</data>
  <data key="d10">serves as bridge</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-266b49cf12495861b99ca9dc21275e0d</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999717</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Equation y = x + dx">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation is directly connected to the Taylor series expansion used for approximating the displacement field.</data>
  <data key="d10">connected to</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-5388c9868e6383d81b0b079e7adc1c81</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999726</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Displacement Field u(x)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor expansion acts as the foundational mathematical tool for describing the displacement field in the neighborhood of a material point.</data>
  <data key="d10">describes,mathematical tool</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999727</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Function F">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion is used to mathematically expand the function f.</data>
  <data key="d10">expands</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-39b2f0f24ccabd94f6101afe155c90b1</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999727</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Scalar Field">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor Series Expansion linearizes the Scalar Field locally around a development point.</data>
  <data key="d10">linearizes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Partial Derivative">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor Series Expansion utilizes Partial Derivatives to linearize the function.</data>
  <data key="d10">utilizes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Hessian Matrix">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The trailing '+' in the Taylor Series Expansion implies subsequent terms involving the Hessian Matrix.</data>
  <data key="d10">implies subsequent terms</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Small-Strain Cauchy Tensor">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor Series Expansion is the foundational step in deriving the Small-Strain Cauchy Tensor.</data>
  <data key="d10">foundational step</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Displacement Vector Field">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The displacement of a material point and an adjacent point are related through a first-order Taylor Series Expansion.</data>
  <data key="d10">related through</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Expansion Point">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor Series Expansion is developed about the Expansion Point (x₀, y₀).</data>
  <data key="d10">expanded about</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Spatial Coordinates">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Spatial Coordinates x and y are used to evaluate the function in the Taylor Series Expansion.</data>
  <data key="d10">used in</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999728</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Taylor Coefficient">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The denominators in the Taylor Series Expansion correspond to the general Taylor Coefficient.</data>
  <data key="d10">corresponds to</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-eaecf14c48f995d5b61d93422a7fc4e2</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999729</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Deformed Element P'Q'R'S'">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series method is used to approximate and label the displacements of points in the deformed element.</data>
  <data key="d10">calculates displacements for</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999730</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Vertical Displacement v">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion uses the base displacement v to calculate displacements at other points.</data>
  <data key="d10">uses as base variable</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-916a73aa729828741cee96b82bd0ae84</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999731</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Taylor Series Expansion" target="Infinitesimal Strain Theory">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion provides the mathematical basis for approximating small displacements within infinitesimal strain theory.</data>
  <data key="d10">Basis for</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-8f7db4e56e1df22cfa2dc24d5b3992b9</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999733</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Strain Component" target="$\varepsilon_x = \partial u / \partial x$">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The normal strain component derived from the one-dimensional approximation is mathematically represented by $\varepsilon_x = \partial u / \partial x$.</data>
  <data key="d10">represented by</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-646c9364260cc8f0c76b71d04358c907</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999716</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
