<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="Displacement Field Component v(x,y)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion is used to linearize the displacement field component v(x,y) in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d10">linearizes,represents</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="Green-Lagrange Strain Tensor">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor expansion serves as the mathematical foundation for deriving the Green-Lagrange strain tensor.</data>
  <data key="d10">foundation for</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="Engineering Strain Definitions">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor expansion serves as the mathematical foundation for engineering strain definitions in small-strain theory.</data>
  <data key="d10">foundation for</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="Base Displacement">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion includes the zeroth-order term v(x,y) as the base displacement.</data>
  <data key="d10">includes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="Partial Derivatives">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor series expansion includes first-order partial derivatives representing the linear rate of change.</data>
  <data key="d10">includes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Two-Dimensional Taylor Series Expansion" target="One-Dimensional Taylor Series Concept">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The two-dimensional Taylor series expansion is the multidimensional generalization of the one-dimensional Taylor series concept.</data>
  <data key="d10">generalizes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Displacement Field Component v(x,y)" target="Displacement Gradient Tensor">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The partial derivatives of the displacement field component form the displacement gradient tensor.</data>
  <data key="d10">derives,forms</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Displacement Field Component v(x,y)" target="Two-Dimensional Bodies">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Taylor expansion of the displacement field enables a continuous field description of the deformation of two-dimensional bodies.</data>
  <data key="d10">describes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-603ebcda3c395baf69674e7e06ddebff</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999718</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
