  <data key="d13">1784999848</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Displacement Field u">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">γ_xy includes the partial derivative ∂u/∂y, representing the rate of change of the x-displacement along the y-direction.</data>
  <data key="d10">partial derivative,rate of change</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999784</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Displacement Field v">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">γ_xy includes the partial derivative ∂v/∂x, representing the rate of change of the y-displacement along the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d10">partial derivative,rate of change</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999784</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Tensorial Shear Strain ε_xy">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The engineering shear strain relates to the tensorial shear strain component by the formula ε_xy = γ_xy/2.</data>
  <data key="d10">proportional relationship,scaling</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999784</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Partial Derivative ∂u/∂y">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">γ_xy is computed as the sum of two partial derivatives, one of which is ∂u/∂y.</data>
  <data key="d10">mathematical component,summation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999785</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Partial Derivative ∂v/∂x">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">γ_xy is computed as the sum of two partial derivatives, one of which is ∂v/∂x.</data>
  <data key="d10">mathematical component,summation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999785</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Rigid Body Rotation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The shear strain equation distinguishes pure deformation from rigid body rotation; when ∂u/∂y = -∂v/∂x, the motion is pure rotation with zero shear strain.</data>
  <data key="d10">distinction,physical significance</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-b83a395393ea0f85f23d45221be850ae</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999785</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Engineering Shear Strain γ_xy" target="Shear Stress-Shear Strain Constitutive Relation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The constitutive relation equation involves the engineering shear strain γ_xy as the resulting deformation variable.</data>
  <data key="d10">component,involves</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-372d45ee57b3e8dd4b75b8ce18594fad</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999847</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
