<edge source="Shear Strain (γ)" target="Normal Strain (ε)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The exact kinematic relationship tan(γ/2) = ε connects shear strain to normal strain for a biaxially loaded elastic plate; for small strains, the linearization yields γ ≈ 2ε.</data>
  <data key="d10">equation,kinematic relationship</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999867</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shear Strain (γ)" target="Biaxially Loaded Elastic Plate">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The shear strain arises from the geometric analysis of a square element in a biaxially loaded elastic plate deforming into a rectangle.</data>
  <data key="d10">deformation,geometry</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999868</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shear Strain (γ)" target="Tangent Subtraction Formula">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The tangent subtraction formula is used to relate the geometric angle change to the principal strains, yielding tan(γ/2) = ε.</data>
  <data key="d10">mathematical derivation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999868</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shear Strain (γ)" target="Linearly Elastic Material">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation establishing the relationship between shear strain and shear stress describes the behavior of an isotropic, linearly elastic material.</data>
  <data key="d10">applies to,describes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-004e08ca4afebf77525a1d7810f76686</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999869</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Strain (ε)" target="Tangent Subtraction Formula">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">By equating the tangent subtraction formula with the deformed diagonal angle, it yields tan(γ/2) = ε.</data>
  <data key="d10">mathematical derivation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999868</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Young's Modulus (E-Modul)" target="Fundamental Elastic Relationship">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation G = E/[2(1+ν)] defines the relationship connecting Young's modulus to the shear modulus.</data>
  <data key="d10">equation,material constants</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-f132449988465ad1aa01eccbc48da1a0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999868</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
