<edge source="Equation (σ1 = σV &amp; σ2 = 0)" target="Uniaxial Stress State">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The equation establishes the mathematical boundary conditions for a uniaxial stress state.</data>
  <data key="d10">boundary conditions</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uniaxial Stress State" target="Equivalent Stress (σV)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The uniaxial stress state is used to mathematically calibrate the equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d10">calibration</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uniaxial Stress State" target="Principal Stresses (σ2, σ3)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Principal stresses σ2 and σ3 are defined as zero under a uniaxial stress state.</data>
  <data key="d10">definition</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uniaxial Stress State" target="Three-Dimensional Stress State">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The uniaxial stress state is equated to a general three-dimensional stress state to derive the formula for equivalent stress.</data>
  <data key="d10">equating</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uniaxial Stress State" target="General Distortion Energy Equation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The uniaxial stress state is substituted into the general distortion energy equation.</data>
  <data key="d10">substitution</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uniaxial Stress State" target="Principal Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The uniaxial stress state represents the simplification of the principal stress tensor under a one-dimensional tensile load.</data>
  <data key="d10">simplification</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Equivalent Stress (σV)" target="Maximum Principal Stress (σ1)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The maximum principal stress is set equal to the equivalent stress in the uniaxial condition.</data>
  <data key="d10">equality</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Equivalent Stress (σV)" target="General Distortion Energy Equation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The formula for equivalent stress is analytically derived by substituting a uniaxial stress state into the general distortion energy equation.</data>
  <data key="d10">derivation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Equivalent Stress (σV)" target="Multi-Axial Stress States">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Equivalent stress allows engineers to compare complex multi-axial stress states against a single scalar value.</data>
  <data key="d10">comparison</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Equivalent Stress (σV)" target="Engineers">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Engineers use the equivalent stress to compare complex multi-axial stress states.</data>
  <data key="d10">usage</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Maximum Principal Stress (σ1)" target="Principal Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The principal normal stresses include the maximum principal stress $\sigma_1$.</data>
  <data key="d10">representation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal Stresses (σ2, σ3)" target="Principal Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The principal normal stresses include the other principal stresses $\sigma_2$ and $\sigma_3$.</data>
  <data key="d10">representation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
