  <data key="d8">20.0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (Distortion Energy Hypothesis) (equation)">
  <data key="d9">Entity Von Mises Yield Criterion belongs to Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (Distortion Energy Hypothesis) (equation)&lt;SEP&gt;Entity Von Mises Yield Criterion belongs to Von Mises Equivalent Stress Equation (Distortion Energy Hypothesis) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d10">belongs_to,contained_in,part_of</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d8">20.0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Von Mises Yield Criterion Origin (header)">
  <data key="d9">Entity Von Mises Yield Criterion belongs to Von Mises Yield Criterion Origin (header)&lt;SEP&gt;Entity Von Mises Yield Criterion belongs to Von Mises Yield Criterion Origin (header)</data>
  <data key="d10">belongs_to,contained_in,part_of</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d8">20.0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999884</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Deviatoric Component">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion development considers only the deviatoric component.</data>
  <data key="d10">based on,uses</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-665c0dff808ce919d3762159ff91d81a</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999879</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Distortional Energy Component">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion is formulated by equating the distortional energy component to the energy of a uniaxial tensile test.</data>
  <data key="d10">based on,formulated using</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-7bb75667b9aeb311bb7c6315d62a2149</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999880</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Deviatoric Stresses">
  <data key="d8">2.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Deviatoric stresses are central to the von Mises yield criterion (Hypothese der Gestaltänderungsenergie).&lt;SEP&gt;The von Mises yield criterion states that deviatoric stresses are solely responsible for causing plastic deformation in ductile materials.</data>
  <data key="d10">causes yielding,central to,plastic deformation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-7a30bb490f8e8f9e0d00884e4cba69c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999880</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Distortional Energy">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion (Vergleichsspannung) is formulated by equating the effective distortion energy U_f to the uniaxial tensile stress state.</data>
  <data key="d10">formulation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999881</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Uniaxial Tensile Stress State">
  <data key="d8">2.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion equates the effective distortion energy to the uniaxial tensile stress state where σ_1 = σ_v and σ_2 = σ_3 = 0.&lt;SEP&gt;The von Mises yield criterion equates the distortion energy of a general three-dimensional stress state to that of a uniaxial tensile stress state.</data>
  <data key="d10">equates,equivalence</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-6b27cb9b72646a5e65e44fef0102a7b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999881</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Hydrostatic Strain Energy Density ($U_h$)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">According to the von Mises yield criterion, hydrostatic stress states associated with hydrostatic strain energy do not cause yielding.</data>
  <data key="d10">independent,no yielding</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-a757fe6cf2d7246e4d34ad6c4db2db27</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999881</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Principal Stress Formulation">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The principal stress formulation equation mathematically represents the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d10">formulation,mathematical representation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Equivalent Uniaxial Stress">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion calculates the equivalent uniaxial stress by equating the distortion energy of a general state to a uniaxial state.</data>
  <data key="d10">calculates,equates</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Second Deviatoric Stress Invariant">
  <data key="d8">2.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion equation is derived by taking the square roots of the second deviatoric stress invariant.&lt;SEP&gt;The von Mises yield criterion posits that yielding begins when the second deviatoric stress invariant reaches a critical value.</data>
  <data key="d10">depends on,derived from,governed by</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d00610e182d45a94eb2e712fcaa111a3</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Distortion Energy">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion postulates that yielding begins when the distortion energy reaches a critical threshold.</data>
  <data key="d10">based on,postulates</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Principal Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion utilizes the differences between principal normal stresses to filter out purely hydrostatic components.</data>
  <data key="d10">utilizes</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Hydrostatic Stress Components">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion effectively filters out purely hydrostatic stress components because they only change volume and do not induce plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d10">filters out</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Tresca Maximum Shear Stress Hypothesis">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion serves as a more accurate alternative to the simpler Tresca maximum shear stress hypothesis.</data>
  <data key="d10">alternative to</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Huber-Von Mises-Hencky Theory">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Huber-von Mises-Hencky theory is another name for the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d10">also known as</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Maximum Distortion Energy Theory">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The maximum distortion energy theory is another name for the von Mises yield criterion.</data>
  <data key="d10">also known as</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="General Three-Dimensional Stress State">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion calculates the equivalent stress by equating the distortion energy of a general three-dimensional stress state to a uniaxial state.</data>
  <data key="d10">equates</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Von Mises Yield Criterion" target="Multi-Axial Loading Scenarios">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The von Mises yield criterion is applied to predict the onset of plastic deformation in complex, multi-axial loading scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d10">applied in</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-192ac0f71348510b81fe5091648ee0bb</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999883</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Distortion Energy (Uf)" target="Total Internal Energy (U)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Distortion energy is isolated by subtracting hydrostatic energy from the total internal energy.</data>
  <data key="d10">calculation</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-808053be7d7f2ab70eb24b63ee8e1f52</data>
  <data key="d12">Kapitel12_Verzerrungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1784999877</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hydrostatic Energy (Uh)" target="Total Internal Energy (U)">
  <data key="d8">1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">Hydrostatic energy is subtracted from the total internal energy to isolate distortion energy.</data>
  <data key="d10">subtraction</data>
